window.STUDY_DATA = [{"topic": "cardio", "title": "Cardiovascular — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Hypertension first-line agents", "bullets": ["ACEI, ARB, thiazide/thiazide-like diuretic, or long-acting CCB — all first-line", "Diabetes + hypertension or CKD with albuminuria: ACEI or ARB preferred", "Black patients: CCB or thiazide often preferred over ACEI/ARB monotherapy", "Never combine ACEI + ARB (or + direct renin inhibitor) — hyperkalemia/AKI risk", "Uncomplicated HTN target: <140/90; diabetes target: <130/80 per Diabetes Canada", "Hypertension is now defined as BP ≥130/80 mmHg; pharmacotherapy is recommended once BP ≥140/90, or if SBP 130-139 with high CVD risk", "Treatment target for most patients is SBP <130 mmHg under current Hypertension Canada guidance", "Initial therapy should be a low-dose single-pill combination of 2 first-line classes rather than monotherapy once pharmacotherapy is indicated", "Beta-blockers are NOT recommended first-line for uncomplicated HTN — less CV risk reduction and higher discontinuation than other classes; reserve for compelling indications (HF, post-MI, angina)", "Pregnancy: methyldopa, labetalol, and long-acting nifedipine are first-line; ACEI/ARB are contraindicated (teratogenic) and hydralazine/clonidine are second-line"]}, {"heading": "Heart failure — the 4 pillars (HFrEF)", "bullets": ["ARNI (or ACEI/ARB) + Beta-blocker + MRA + SGLT2i = 4 pillars, start early and in parallel where possible", "Beta-blockers with HF mortality evidence: bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate (ER) ONLY", "SGLT2i (empagliflozin/dapagliflozin) benefit HF regardless of diabetes status", "Loop diuretics = symptom control only, no mortality benefit", "Wash out ACEI 36h before starting ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) — angioedema risk if overlapped", "HFnrEF (LVEF >40%): SGLT2i + MRA form the backbone; add ARNI (or candesartan if ARNI unavailable/not tolerated) and consider a GLP-1 RA if LVEF ≥45% and BMI ≥30 kg/m² — benefit is mainly fewer hospitalizations, not mortality", "Ivabradine can be added in HFrEF with resting HR ≥70 bpm in sinus rhythm despite optimal GDMT; watch for visual phosphenes and bradycardia", "Vericiguat is considered after a recent HF hospitalization on optimal therapy; avoid if SBP <100 mmHg or eGFR <15", "Hydralazine/isosorbide dinitrate is added in Black patients with advanced HFrEF symptoms on standard therapy, or used as an ACEI/ARB/ARNI alternative when those aren't tolerated", "During acute decompensation, continue a beta-blocker the patient is already on — abrupt withdrawal worsens HF — and start one only once hemodynamically stable if beta-blocker naive"]}, {"heading": "Lipid management", "bullets": ["High-intensity statin = atorvastatin 40-80mg or rosuvastatin 20-40mg (≥50% LDL reduction)", "Statin + high triglycerides not controlled: consider icosapent ethyl or fibrate add-on", "Statins contraindicated in pregnancy/breastfeeding and active liver disease", "Muscle pain + fever/dark urine = check CK, think rhabdomyolysis", "Simvastatin (unlike atorvastatin/rosuvastatin) has more CYP3A4 interactions — dose cap with amlodipine/diltiazem", "When triglycerides >1.5 mmol/L, non-HDL-C or ApoB (not LDL-C) becomes the preferred lipid target for screening and treatment decisions", "ASCVD secondary prevention target is LDL <1.8 mmol/L (or ApoB <0.70 g/L, non-HDL-C <2.4 mmol/L); add ezetimibe first if not at target on max statin, then a PCSK9 inhibitor if still above target", "PCSK9 inhibitors (alirocumab, evolocumab) are preferred over ezetimibe as the add-on in diabetes, polyvascular disease, recurrent MI, or LDL ≥2.6 mmol/L despite statin therapy", "Fenofibrate is the most potent agent for lowering triglycerides (especially TG >10 mmol/L), but combining a fibrate with a statin — especially gemfibrozil — raises myopathy risk", "Pravastatin undergoes no CYP metabolism, giving it the fewest drug interactions — a useful choice in patients on multiple interacting medications"]}, {"heading": "Anticoagulation & antiplatelets", "bullets": ["DOACs preferred over warfarin for non-valvular AFib (no routine monitoring)", "Warfarin still required for mechanical heart valves and moderate-severe mitral stenosis", "Apixaban dose reduction: ≥2 of [age ≥80, weight ≤60kg, SCr ≥133 µmol/L]", "DAPT (ASA + P2Y12 inhibitor) post-stent: typically 6-12 months, individualized to bleeding risk", "Warfarin target INR 2-3 (2.5-3.5 for mechanical mitral valve)", "CHADS2-65 (Canadian algorithm): any of age ≥65, prior stroke/TIA, hypertension, heart failure, diabetes, or vascular disease triggers oral anticoagulation in AFib", "HAS-BLED ≥3 flags high bleeding risk, but the benefit of anticoagulation almost always outweighs the risk unless bleeding risk is very high and stroke risk is very low", "AFib + PCI/ACS on anticoagulation: triple therapy (OAC + ASA + P2Y12) is minimized to as little as 1 day-1 month, then switched to dual therapy (OAC + clopidogrel) — ticagrelor/prasugrel are generally avoided in combination with an anticoagulant", "Cardioversion anticoagulation: if AFib onset <12h with no stroke history, or 12-48h with CHADS2 <2, cardioversion can proceed without the usual 3-week lead-in; all patients need ≥4 weeks of anticoagulation afterward", "Ticagrelor and prasugrel achieve more potent, faster P2Y12 inhibition than clopidogrel, but clopidogrel remains the DAPT partner of choice after fibrinolysis for STEMI"]}, {"heading": "Key interactions to know", "bullets": ["Amiodarone + warfarin → potentiates INR, reduce warfarin dose ~30-50%", "Amiodarone + digoxin → increases digoxin level, reduce digoxin dose", "Verapamil/diltiazem + beta-blocker → additive bradycardia/AV block, generally avoid combo", "Clopidogrel + omeprazole/esomeprazole → reduced antiplatelet activation, use pantoprazole instead", "NSAIDs blunt ACEI/ARB/diuretic effect and worsen renal function — 'triple whammy' with ACEI+diuretic+NSAID", "DOACs (rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban) are CYP3A4 and P-gp substrates — potent inhibitors (azole antifungals, HIV protease inhibitors) raise levels and bleeding risk", "Digoxin toxicity risk rises sharply with hypokalemia — correct potassium before assuming a dose change is needed", "Class Ic antiarrhythmics (flecainide, propafenone) are contraindicated in CAD or structural heart disease due to proarrhythmia risk, and should be paired with an AV-nodal blocking agent", "St. John's Wort induces metabolism of warfarin and DOACs (reduced anticoagulant effect); gingko biloba adds bleeding risk with any anticoagulant/antiplatelet"]}, {"heading": "Monitoring parameters", "bullets": ["ACEI/ARB/ARNI: SCr and K+ at baseline and 1-2 weeks after start/titration", "Statins: baseline LFTs; CK only if symptomatic myopathy", "Digoxin: trough level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL for HF, plus renal function and electrolytes", "Amiodarone: TFTs, LFTs, chest X-ray/PFTs, eye exam at baseline and periodically", "Warfarin: INR regularly, more frequently at initiation or with dose/diet/drug changes", "SGLT2i: expect a transient eGFR dip of 15-25% in the first 1-3 months after starting — this resolves and is not a reason to stop", "Ivabradine: watch for visual phosphenes (light rings/spots) and bradycardia, especially in the first two months", "Sotalol: baseline ECG plus a repeat 48-72 hours after outpatient initiation to catch QT prolongation before it becomes dangerous", "MRA (spironolactone/eplerenone/finerenone): recheck SCr and potassium within 1 week of starting or any dose change, then every 3 months"]}, {"heading": "Red flags / when to escalate", "bullets": ["Angioedema (ACEI/ARNI) → stop immediately, do not rechallenge with any ACEI", "Resting HR <50 bpm or symptomatic bradycardia on beta-blocker/digoxin → hold and reassess", "Rapid weight gain >2kg in a few days in HF patient → possible decompensation", "Signs of digoxin toxicity: nausea, visual halos, arrhythmia → check level, consider DigiFab if severe", "Unexplained severe muscle pain + dark urine on statin → check CK, think rhabdomyolysis", "TIMI score 6-7, hemodynamic instability, or recurrent ischemia post-ACS → high risk, favors an early invasive (PCI) strategy over medical management alone", "INR >9 with no bleeding → hold warfarin (and consider oral vitamin K); most patients with INR 4.5-10 and no bleeding need only a held dose, not vitamin K", "A 'pill-in-the-pocket' AFib episode that hasn't resolved within 6-8 hours, or a second episode within 24 hours → go to the emergency department, do not redose", "New confusion, nausea, or visual disturbance in a patient newly started on amiodarone while on digoxin → suspect the amiodarone-digoxin interaction raising digoxin levels"]}, {"heading": "Classic exam traps", "bullets": ["Metoprolol TARTRATE (IR) has no HFrEF mortality benefit — only the SUCCINATE (ER) salt does", "Amlodipine edema is NOT fluid overload — don't reflexively add a diuretic", "DOACs are NOT for mechanical valves or significant mitral stenosis — warfarin required", "Furosemide has sulfa-like structure — caution (not absolute contraindication) in sulfa allergy", "Spironolactone + ACEI/ARB = high hyperkalemia risk — always check K+ before and after starting", "CHADS2-65 (Canadian) and CHA2DS2-VASc (American) are different scoring systems — know which one a question is asking about before calculating", "Nifedipine IMMEDIATE-release is dangerous in angina/ACS due to reflex tachycardia — only long-acting CCB formulations are used for ischemic heart disease", "Ezetimibe, not a PCSK9 inhibitor, is the default first add-on when a patient is above LDL target on maximally tolerated statin — PCSK9 inhibitors are reserved for higher-risk add-on scenarios", "Bivalirudin is preferred over UFH/LMWH specifically in STEMI+PCI patients with a history of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia or very high bleeding risk"]}, {"heading": "Atrial fibrillation: risk scoring & rate/rhythm strategy", "bullets": ["CHADS2-65: age ≥65, prior stroke/TIA, hypertension, heart failure, diabetes, or vascular disease — any one of these triggers oral anticoagulation per Canadian guidelines", "HAS-BLED ≥3 identifies high bleeding risk but is a prompt to address modifiable risk factors, not a reason to withhold anticoagulation on its own", "Long-term rate control target is resting HR <100 bpm — there's no evidence that tighter control improves outcomes", "Rate control first-line: beta-blocker or non-DHP CCB if LVEF >40%; beta-blocker preferred (bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol) if LVEF ≤40%", "Rhythm control is preferred over rate control in newly diagnosed AFib (<1 year), high symptom burden, multiple recurrences, or when rate control is difficult to achieve", "'Pill-in-the-pocket' antiarrhythmic therapy (flecainide/propafenone) requires pretreatment with an oral AV-nodal blocker 30 minutes beforehand, and is reserved for infrequent, well-tolerated paroxysmal episodes without structural heart disease"]}, {"heading": "Warfarin: dosing, INR adjustment & reversal", "bullets": ["Typical starting dose is 5 mg daily; reduce to 1-2 mg daily in frail, elderly, underweight, or Asian-descent patients", "For acute VTE, overlap warfarin with LMWH for at least 5 days AND until the INR is ≥2 for 2 consecutive days before stopping the heparin", "INR 3.6-4.0: hold 0-1 dose and reduce the maintenance dose by 10-15%; INR 4.1-8.9: hold up to 2 doses and reduce 10-20%; INR >9: hold 2 doses and consider vitamin K", "For an INR of 4.5-10 with no bleeding, holding the dose alone is usually enough — routine vitamin K is not required in that range", "Emergent reversal for major bleeding: IV vitamin K PLUS 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate — INR drops significantly within 30 minutes", "Perioperative bridging with therapeutic LMWH is reserved for high thromboembolic risk (mechanical mitral valve, CHADS2 5-6, stroke/VTE within the last 3 months) — most other patients can simply stop warfarin 5 days pre-op with no bridging"]}]}, {"topic": "endo", "title": "Endocrine & Diabetes — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "T2DM drug therapy — where each agent fits", "bullets": ["Metformin = first-line for essentially everyone with T2DM (unless eGFR <30 or other contraindication)", "Established ASCVD, HF, or CKD → add SGLT2i or GLP-1 RA with proven benefit, regardless of A1C", "SGLT2i: cardiorenal protection, weight loss, euglycemic DKA risk, genital infections", "GLP-1 RA: strong A1C + weight benefit, GI side effects, avoid in personal/family MTC or MEN2 history", "Sulfonylureas/TZD: effective but weight gain + hypoglycemia (SU) or HF risk (TZD) — lower priority now", "Canagliflozin and dapagliflozin slow diabetic nephropathy progression alongside empagliflozin — all three have renal-protective evidence", "GLP-1 RAs with proven MACE reduction: liraglutide, dulaglutide, and subcutaneous semaglutide; oral semaglutide has less robust cardiovascular outcome data", "DPP-4 inhibitors and acarbose are weight-neutral, low-hypoglycemia options but produce only modest A1C lowering (≤1%)", "Never combine a GLP-1 RA with a DPP-4 inhibitor — overlapping incretin mechanism with no added benefit", "Insulin (± metformin) is started first-line, ahead of oral agents, whenever a patient presents with symptomatic hyperglycemia and metabolic decompensation (ketosis, unintended weight loss, polyuria)"]}, {"heading": "Insulin basics", "bullets": ["Basal (glargine, detemir): once-daily, peakless, covers background needs", "Rapid-acting (aspart, lispro): mealtime coverage, given just before/with meals", "Never mix glargine with other insulins in the same syringe", "Insulin = high-alert drug — always double check units, insulin type, and pen vs. vial", "Hypoglycemia treatment: 15g fast-acting carbs, recheck glucose in 15 min ('15-15 rule')", "Degludec is ultra-long-acting (duration up to 42h) with no pronounced peak — even more forgiving of dosing-time variability than glargine or detemir", "Starting basal insulin in T2DM: 0.1-0.2 units/kg/day (roughly 10 units at bedtime), titrated by 1 unit/day toward a fasting target of 4-7 mmol/L", "Stop titrating a basal insulin dose upward if the patient has any nocturnal hypoglycemia or 2 hypoglycemic episodes in a week", "Somogyi effect (rebound hyperglycemia after 3am hypoglycemia) vs. dawn phenomenon (early-morning hormone-driven hyperglycemia with no preceding low) are distinguished by checking a 3am glucose — management differs (decrease evening insulin vs. shift meal/exercise timing)", "Severe hypoglycemia in a patient who can't swallow: glucagon 1 mg SC/IM (or 3 mg intranasally) outside hospital, or IV dextrose in a healthcare setting"]}, {"heading": "Guideline targets & numbers", "bullets": ["A1C target for most adults with T2DM: ≤7.0%", "Fasting/preprandial glucose target: 4-7 mmol/L; 2h postprandial: 5-10 mmol/L", "BP target in diabetes: <130/80", "LDL target in diabetes with additional risk factors: often <2.0 mmol/L or ≥50% reduction — statin usually indicated", "Dose-reduce metformin at eGFR 30-45; generally avoid/stop below eGFR 30", "Diagnostic thresholds for diabetes: fasting glucose ≥7.0 mmol/L, random glucose ≥11.1 mmol/L, 2-hour OGTT ≥11.1 mmol/L, or A1C ≥6.5% — an abnormal result must be repeated to confirm unless the patient is symptomatic", "Prediabetes = impaired fasting glucose (6.1-6.9 mmol/L), impaired glucose tolerance (2h OGTT 7.8-11.0 mmol/L), or A1C 6.0-6.4%", "An A1C target of ≤6.5% may be used in low-hypoglycemia-risk T2DM patients to further reduce retinopathy and CKD risk; higher targets (7.1-8.5%) suit frail or elderly patients with hypoglycemia unawareness", "Statins are indicated in most people with diabetes age ≥40 (or ≥30 with ≥15 years of diabetes, or with microvascular disease), independent of baseline LDL"]}, {"heading": "Thyroid & bone essentials", "bullets": ["Levothyroxine: take on empty stomach, separate from calcium/iron/PPIs by ≥4 hours", "Recheck TSH 6-8 weeks after any levothyroxine dose change", "Methimazole preferred over PTU except 1st trimester pregnancy or thyroid storm", "Bisphosphonates: take fasting with full glass of water, stay upright 30-60 min after", "Bisphosphonate rare risks: osteonecrosis of jaw, atypical femur fracture — dental work before starting ideally", "Levothyroxine dosing starts at roughly 1.6-1.7 mcg/kg/day; lower starting doses and slower titration are used in the elderly or those with angina", "Pregnancy increases levothyroxine requirements by up to 50% — patients should increase their dose by about 2 extra tablets per week as soon as pregnancy is confirmed and recheck TSH every 4-6 weeks", "Graves' disease: propranolol (non-selective beta-blocker) has the most evidence for symptom control, but avoid it in confirmed asthma/COPD or PVD in favor of a cardioselective agent", "Thyroid storm is treated with PTU (blocks peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion), a beta-blocker, corticosteroids, and iodine (Lugol's solution) — avoid NSAIDs, which can displace thyroid hormone from binding globulins and worsen thyrotoxicosis", "Myxedema coma requires IV corticosteroids before IV levothyroxine, plus supportive care for hypothermia, hypotension, and hypoglycemia — thyroid levels are checked every 1-2 days until improvement"]}, {"heading": "Major contraindications & cautions", "bullets": ["Metformin: avoid if eGFR <30, acute illness/dehydration, contrast dye (hold around procedure)", "GLP-1 RA/exenatide-class: avoid with personal/family history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2", "TZDs (pioglitazone): avoid in NYHA III-IV heart failure", "SGLT2i: hold during 'sick days' (vomiting, not eating/drinking, acute illness, pre-surgery) — DKA risk", "Sulfonylureas: use cautiously in elderly/renal impairment — higher hypoglycemia risk", "Bupropion/naltrexone (obesity therapy): contraindicated with concurrent opioid use, seizure disorder, or eating disorders", "GLP-1/GIP and GIP/GLP-1 agents (tirzepatide, semaglutide) slow gastric emptying — hold or space other oral medications requiring rapid absorption by at least 1 hour, and consider holding before surgery", "Orlistat is contraindicated in chronic malabsorption syndrome or cholestasis and reduces absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and some medications (cyclosporine, levothyroxine)", "Radioactive iodine is contraindicated in pregnancy/breastfeeding, and antithyroid drugs should be stopped 2-3 days beforehand since they blunt its uptake"]}, {"heading": "Key interactions to know", "bullets": ["Levothyroxine + calcium/iron/PPI/antacids → reduced absorption, separate dosing", "Insulin/sulfonylurea + GLP-1 RA or SGLT2i → additive hypoglycemia risk, may need dose reduction", "Corticosteroids (prednisone) → cause/worsen hyperglycemia, may need diabetes med adjustment", "Beta-blockers → can mask adrenergic hypoglycemia symptoms (tachycardia) but sweating persists", "Fluoroquinolones → can cause both hyper- and hypoglycemia in patients on diabetes meds", "Cimetidine and dolutegravir increase metformin levels via reduced renal clearance", "Gemfibrozil inhibits pioglitazone/rosiglitazone metabolism (CYP2C8) and prolongs their hypoglycemic effect", "Acarbose lowers digoxin levels and loses effect when taken with digestive enzyme products", "Orlistat increases INR with warfarin and reduces cyclosporine and levothyroxine absorption — space administration by several hours"]}, {"heading": "Monitoring parameters", "bullets": ["Metformin: renal function (eGFR) at least annually, B12 periodically with long-term use", "Levothyroxine: TSH 6-8 weeks post dose change, then periodically", "SGLT2i/GLP-1: renal function, volume status; watch for DKA symptoms even with normal glucose", "Sulfonylureas/insulin: home blood glucose monitoring, hypoglycemia awareness", "Long-term corticosteroids: blood glucose, BP, bone density", "Antithyroid drugs (methimazole/PTU): baseline CBC and LFTs, then monitor for neutropenia and hepatotoxicity throughout therapy — TFTs every 4-6 weeks until euthyroid, then every 2-3 months", "Obesity pharmacotherapy: track weight/BMI monthly for the first 3 months (goal ≥5% loss), then quarterly; screen for pancreatitis symptoms at each visit", "Acarbose: check LFTs every 3 months during the first year of therapy", "GLP-1 RAs: monitor for retinopathy progression, especially with pre-existing retinopathy, when A1C drops rapidly"]}, {"heading": "Classic exam traps", "bullets": ["Euglycemic DKA can occur on SGLT2i even with near-normal blood glucose — don't rule out DKA just because glucose looks fine", "Metformin does NOT cause hypoglycemia on its own — think of other agents if a patient reports lows", "Rybelsus (oral semaglutide) needs empty stomach + minimal water + wait 30 min — very different from injectable Ozempic", "Methimazole is teratogenic in 1st trimester — PTU is the pregnancy-preferred antithyroid in that window", "'Sick day rules' apply to metformin AND SGLT2i — hold both during acute illness/dehydration/vomiting", "Semaglutide is marketed as both Ozempic (diabetes) and Wegovy (obesity, higher dose) — same molecule, different approved indication and dose", "A rash from ampicillin/amoxicillin during acute mononucleosis is not a true penicillin allergy — an exam trap that also comes up outside endocrinology", "Levothyroxine dose needs typically increase, not decrease, during pregnancy — a fall in requirement should prompt double-checking adherence and timing", "Finerenone, not spironolactone, is the mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist with proven cardiorenal benefit in T2DM with albuminuria — don't confuse the two MRAs"]}, {"heading": "Obesity pharmacotherapy", "bullets": ["Pharmacotherapy for obesity is considered at BMI ≥30 kg/m² or ≥27 kg/m² with a weight-related complication, always as an adjunct to lifestyle change, not a replacement", "Orlistat blocks fat absorption by about 30%; take with a fat-containing meal and separate fat-soluble vitamins by 2+ hours — contraindicated in chronic malabsorption or cholestasis", "Bupropion/naltrexone combines an antidepressant with an opioid antagonist; contraindicated with opioid use, seizure disorder, or eating disorders", "Liraglutide and semaglutide are GLP-1 receptor agonists titrated to a higher weight-loss dose than their diabetes counterparts (liraglutide 3 mg daily, semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly)", "Tirzepatide, a dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist, can reduce absorption of oral contraceptives — counsel on backup contraception", "A 5-10% weight loss is enough to produce meaningful improvement in blood pressure, glycemia, and lipids — this is the realistic first target, not normalization of BMI"]}, {"heading": "Hyperthyroidism management", "bullets": ["Methimazole is generally preferred over PTU (faster onset, longer duration, once-daily dosing, less hepatotoxicity) except in the 1st trimester of pregnancy or thyroid storm, where PTU is preferred", "Both thionamides carry a small risk of agranulocytosis — tell patients to stop the drug and seek care immediately for fever, sore throat, or jaundice", "Beta-blockers (propranolol preferred) are recommended in essentially all symptomatic hyperthyroid patients, especially with resting HR >90 bpm, to control adrenergic symptoms while the antithyroid drug takes effect", "Radioactive iodine ablation requires a negative pregnancy test within 48 hours in women of childbearing age, and pregnancy should be avoided for at least 6 months afterward", "Graves' ophthalmopathy can worsen with radioactive iodine treatment — a risk to weigh against thionamides or surgery in patients with eye involvement", "Thionamide therapy is typically continued 12-18 months before a trial off; relapse risk depends on persistence of thyroid receptor antibodies (TRAb)"]}]}, {"topic": "resp", "title": "Respiratory — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Asthma: controller ladder (GINA)", "bullets": ["Track 1 (preferred): as-needed low-dose ICS-formoterol at every step, even mild asthma", "Step 1-2: as-needed low-dose ICS-formoterol (or low-dose ICS + SABA if track 2)", "Step 3: low-dose ICS-formoterol maintenance + as-needed reliever (MART/SMART)", "Step 4: medium-dose ICS-formoterol MART", "Step 5: add LAMA, refer for biologics (severe eosinophilic/allergic asthma)", "SABA-only reliever therapy is now discouraged — increases exacerbation risk vs ICS-containing reliever", "Good asthma control = daytime symptoms ≤2 days/week, no night waking, no activity limitation, and reliever use ≤2 doses/week; 3 or more unmet criteria = uncontrolled", "Step up therapy if control is inadequate after 2-3 months (once adherence, technique, and triggers are confirmed); step down every 3-6 months once stable, but never during pregnancy, travel to a different climate, or a respiratory infection", "Biologic add-ons target specific phenotypes: omalizumab for severe allergic asthma, mepolizumab/benralizumab/reslizumab for eosinophilic asthma, dupilumab for eosinophilic/type-2 asthma, tezepelumab as a broader add-on for severe asthma", "Excessive SABA use — more than 3 canisters a year — predicts fatal or near-fatal exacerbations and should trigger reassessment of controller therapy"]}, {"heading": "COPD: drug choice by GOLD group", "bullets": ["Group A (low symptoms/risk): a bronchodilator (SABA/SAMA or LABA/LAMA)", "Group B (more symptoms, low risk): LABA or LAMA — LAMA often preferred", "Group E (high exacerbation risk): LABA+LAMA; add ICS if eos ≥300 cells/µL or history of asthma overlap", "ICS in COPD only if frequent exacerbations + high eosinophils — NOT first-line like in asthma", "Smoking cessation is the single intervention that changes disease trajectory", "COPD diagnosis requires FEV1/FVC <0.7 after bronchodilator, plus symptoms and risk factor history — spirometry alone doesn't diagnose it", "GOLD groups are now A/B/E (E = any exacerbation history, replacing the old C/D split) — group E gets LABA+LAMA regardless of symptom burden", "Add roflumilast (if FEV1 <50% with chronic bronchitis) or azithromycin (in non-smokers, after checking QT and MAC status) as adjuncts when LABA+LAMA(±ICS) isn't controlling exacerbations", "Antibiotics for an exacerbation are reserved for a prior positive sputum culture, need for mechanical ventilation, or at least 2 of increased dyspnea/sputum volume/sputum purulence (with purulence required as one of the two)", "Complicated AECOPD (FEV1 <50%, ≥4 exacerbations/year, ischemic heart disease, chronic oral steroids, recent antibiotic use) needs coverage for Klebsiella/Pseudomonas — a respiratory fluoroquinolone or amoxicillin-clavulanate over plain amoxicillin"]}, {"heading": "Inhaler technique & spacers", "bullets": ["Spacers reduce oropharyngeal deposition — cuts thrush/dysphonia risk with ICS", "Rinse and spit after every ICS dose (thrush, hoarseness prevention)", "HandiHaler capsules are inhaled, never swallowed", "Formoterol has fast onset (~1-3 min) — enables MART; salmeterol does NOT (slow onset, never for rescue)", "DPI technique requires a fast, forceful inhalation to disperse the powder — the opposite of the slow, steady breath used for an MDI", "Device selection by age: face-mask valved holding chamber for ages 0-3, mouthpiece holding chamber for 4-6, and DPI or MDI+mouthpiece chamber from age 7 up", "Prime an MDI before first use and after any period of non-use; never shake or invert a DPI, which can dump or waste the dose"]}, {"heading": "Numbers & thresholds that get tested", "bullets": ["SABA overuse red flag: >1 canister/month or >2 canisters/year", "Topical decongestant (oxymetazoline): max 3 consecutive days — rebound congestion after", "Blood eosinophils ≥300 cells/µL favours adding ICS in COPD", "LABA monotherapy in asthma = boxed warning; always pair with ICS", "COPD GOLD spirometry severity: Gold 1 (mild) FEV1 ≥80% predicted, Gold 2 (moderate) 50-79%, Gold 3 (severe) 30-49%, Gold 4 (very severe) <30%", "Blood eosinophils <100 cells/µL argues against adding ICS in COPD; 100-299 'favours' ICS only with ≥1 moderate exacerbation/year; ≥300 'strongly favours' ICS", "Oxygen saturation targets in an asthma exacerbation: ≥92% in children ≤5 years, upper limit 95% in those ≥6 years — don't over-oxygenate", "AECOPD systemic corticosteroid course: 40 mg prednisone-equivalent daily for 5 days is the standard, evidence-based duration — not longer"]}, {"heading": "Key contraindications & cautions", "bullets": ["LAMA (tiotropium): caution in narrow-angle glaucoma, BPH/urinary retention", "Pseudoephedrine: avoid in uncontrolled HTN, CVD, and with MAOIs (hypertensive crisis)", "Montelukast: boxed warning for neuropsychiatric effects (agitation, suicidality)", "Bupropion (smoking cessation): contraindicated in seizure disorder, eating disorder", "Home oxygen therapy is contraindicated in a patient who continues to smoke — fire risk with supplemental O2", "Leukotriene receptor antagonists are second-line to low-dose ICS for monotherapy — less effective at preventing exacerbations despite fewer administration hurdles", "Theophylline is largely avoided today given its narrow therapeutic index, many drug interactions, and need for level monitoring — rarely first-choice in current guidelines", "NSAIDs/ASA, beta-blockers, and sulfites are classic asthma symptom triggers — screen for them when asthma control suddenly worsens"]}, {"heading": "Smoking cessation pharmacotherapy", "bullets": ["Varenicline = most effective single agent; start 1 week pre-quit, course ~12 wks", "Combination NRT (patch + gum/lozenge) beats any single NRT form alone", "NRT is safe in cardiovascular disease — classic exam trap (it's NOT contraindicated post-MI)", "Bupropion and varenicline both start 1-2 weeks before the quit date", "Counselling on smoking cessation should be offered at every single visit for a COPD or asthma patient — it's the one intervention proven to change disease trajectory", "Bupropion for smoking cessation is contraindicated in seizure disorder and eating disorders — the same restriction as its use for depression/obesity", "Nicotine replacement, bupropion, and varenicline are all more effective when combined with behavioural counselling rather than used alone"]}, {"heading": "Monitoring parameters", "bullets": ["ICS long-term: growth (kids), bone density, cataracts with high-dose chronic use", "Roflumilast: monitor weight (significant weight loss risk), mood/psychiatric symptoms", "Varenicline/bupropion: monitor mood and behaviour changes", "Spirometry (FEV1) tracks COPD severity and guides GOLD grouping", "Montelukast: monitor for mood or behavioural changes at every follow-up, given its neuropsychiatric boxed warning", "Track SABA refill frequency at every pharmacy visit — more than 2 doses/week or 3 canisters/year signals poor control needing escalation", "After an asthma exacerbation, review the patient within 1 week and increase the maintenance ICS (or ICS-LABA) dose for the following 2-4 weeks"]}, {"heading": "Exam traps to remember", "bullets": ["LABA alone in asthma = dangerous; always with ICS (never true for COPD)", "Oxymetazoline >3 days → rhinitis medicamentosa, worse congestion", "First-gen antihistamines (diphenhydramine) — avoid in elderly (Beers Criteria, anticholinergic/fall risk)", "Pseudoephedrine is behind-the-counter due to methamphetamine precursor status", "Formoterol vs salmeterol onset difference is the reason only formoterol combos are used in SMART/MART", "Asthma and COPD look similar but differ in onset age (usually <40 vs ≥35 with a long smoking history), reversibility, and allergy association — a classic differentiation question", "ACE inhibitor cough can mimic or worsen respiratory symptoms in COPD but is not itself a cause of the disease", "Drug-induced pulmonary toxicity from amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate, or bleomycin usually resolves within 1-2 days of stopping the drug in acute cases, but chronic/fibrotic reactions take much longer and may need corticosteroids for hypersensitivity pneumonitis", "Antibiotics are NOT routinely recommended for an asthma exacerbation (unlike AECOPD) unless there's fever, purulent sputum, or radiographic pneumonia"]}, {"heading": "Drug-induced pulmonary toxicity", "bullets": ["Drug-induced lung disease is a diagnosis of exclusion — there's no confirmatory test; the strongest evidence is symptom improvement after withdrawing the suspected drug", "Classic causative agents: amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate, bleomycin, ACE inhibitors (cough, not toxicity per se), and NSAIDs", "Onset ranges from days to years after starting the drug, so a thorough medication history (including recently stopped drugs) is essential", "Presentation is nonspecific: cough, dyspnea, fever, wheeze, or pleuritic chest pain — can mimic infection or heart failure", "Management: stop the offending drug first; acute reactions often resolve in 1-2 days, while chronic/fibrotic reactions need longer supportive care and sometimes corticosteroids for hypersensitivity pneumonitis", "Risk factors include cumulative dose, combination therapy, concurrent oxygen or radiation therapy, and underlying lung or autoimmune disease"]}, {"heading": "COPD exacerbation (AECOPD) management", "bullets": ["AECOPD is defined as acute worsening of dyspnea, sputum volume, or sputum purulence over up to 14 days", "Mild/moderate AECOPD: SABA ± SAMA, ± a 5-day course of prednisone 40 mg/day, ± antibiotics if criteria are met", "Severe AECOPD needs added respiratory support/oxygen and IV or PO corticosteroids", "First-line antibiotics for a simple exacerbation: amoxicillin, doxycycline, azithromycin, clarithromycin, or TMP-SMX; complicated exacerbations (risk factors present) need a respiratory fluoroquinolone or amoxicillin-clavulanate to cover resistant/gram-negative organisms", "Switch antibiotic class if a course is repeated within 3 months to limit resistance development", "Vaccines recommended for COPD/asthma patients include influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19, Tdap, RSV, and shingles — review status at the start of every respiratory virus season"]}]}, {"topic": "cns", "title": "Neurology & Psychiatry — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Antidepressants: first-line & timing", "bullets": ["SSRIs are first-line for MDD and most anxiety disorders", "Full effect takes 4-6 weeks; side effects (GI, anxiety) often appear first and fade", "All antidepressants carry boxed warning: increased suicidality risk <25 years old, esp. first weeks", "Never combine SSRI/SNRI with MAOIs — 14-day washout required (serotonin syndrome risk)", "Venlafaxine/paroxetine have the worst discontinuation syndromes — taper slowly", "Mild depression: psychotherapy or exercise alone can be first-line and is as effective as medication; moderate depression can start with either or both; severe depression should get both psychotherapy and pharmacotherapy together", "A long list of drugs can cause or worsen depression, including corticosteroids, beta-blockers, interferons, isotretinoin, hormonal contraceptives, and several antiepileptics — screen the medication list before assuming a primary mood disorder", "A structured suicide safety plan (warning signs, coping strategies, social supports, professional contacts, and means restriction) should be developed collaboratively with any patient at elevated suicide risk", "If there's no improvement after an adequate trial (usually 4 weeks), first optimize the dose and adherence and consider adding psychotherapy before switching agents", "Partial response to a well-tolerated antidepressant favors augmentation (e.g., aripiprazole or brexpiprazole) over switching; poor tolerability favors switching to a different class"]}, {"heading": "Antipsychotics: typical vs atypical", "bullets": ["Atypicals (quetiapine, risperidone, olanzapine) = lower EPS, higher metabolic risk (weight gain, dyslipidemia, hyperglycemia)", "Typicals, esp. high-potency (haloperidol) = high EPS/tardive dyskinesia, low sedation/anticholinergic burden", "Risperidone stands out among atypicals for dose-dependent EPS and hyperprolactinemia", "All antipsychotics: boxed warning for increased mortality in elderly dementia-related psychosis", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome = fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, altered mental status — emergency", "Asenapine has no anticholinergic activity at therapeutic doses (no dry mouth/blurred vision from it), but must not be followed by food or drink for at least 10 minutes because it's absorbed sublingually", "Ziprasidone requires administration with a substantial meal (≥500 calories) for adequate absorption and is essentially weight-neutral compared to most other atypicals", "Olanzapine and aripiprazole IM formulations are first-line choices for acute agitation requiring rapid intramuscular treatment", "Paliperidone is the active metabolite of risperidone with a similar side-effect profile but needs renal dose adjustment and has minimal drug interactions since it isn't hepatically metabolized", "Cariprazine is unique among antipsychotics for treating both mania (higher dose) and bipolar depression (lower dose) with the same molecule"]}, {"heading": "Mood stabilizers: monitoring essentials", "bullets": ["Lithium target trough 0.6-1.2 mmol/L, drawn 12h post-dose; toxicity = coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion", "Lithium raised by NSAIDs, ACEi/ARBs, thiazides — classic interaction", "Valproate: boxed warnings — teratogenicity, hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis; needs contraception counselling", "Carbamazepine: boxed warnings — SJS/TEN (check HLA-B*1502 in Asian ancestry), aplastic anemia/agranulocytosis", "Lamotrigine: slow titration mandatory — rapid titration or valproate co-administration raises SJS/TEN risk", "Lithium dosing target varies by indication: 0.8-1.2 mmol/L for acute mania, 0.6-1.0 mmol/L for maintenance, and a lower 0.4-0.8 mmol/L target in elderly patients", "Lithium can cause hypothyroidism (more common than hyperthyroidism), hyperparathyroidism, and hypercalcemia — check thyroid and renal function at least every 6 months", "Dehydration and abrupt changes in sodium intake (not just overdose) can precipitate lithium toxicity by altering renal clearance", "Valproate and lamotrigine interact significantly: valproate roughly doubles lamotrigine levels, requiring a slower, lower lamotrigine titration when combined", "First-line monotherapies for bipolar disorder include lithium, quetiapine, divalproex/valproic acid, asenapine, and aripiprazole"]}, {"heading": "Parkinson's disease pharmacology", "bullets": ["Levodopa/carbidopa = most effective for motor symptoms but causes long-term dyskinesias/'wearing off'", "Never stop levodopa abruptly — risk of parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia crisis (like NMS)", "Dopamine agonists (pramipexole): watch for impulse control disorders (gambling, hypersexuality) and sudden sleep attacks", "High-protein meals can reduce levodopa absorption — timing matters as disease advances", "Non-selective MAOIs contraindicated with levodopa (hypertensive crisis)"]}, {"heading": "Cholinesterase inhibitors (Alzheimer's)", "bullets": ["Donepezil provides modest SYMPTOMATIC benefit only — not disease-modifying", "GI side effects (nausea, diarrhea) are dose-related, most common on titration", "Avoid combining with anticholinergic drugs — opposing mechanisms, common prescribing-cascade error", "Caution/avoid in bradycardia or conduction disease without a pacemaker", "Rivastigmine is available as a transdermal patch for patients who can't swallow, isn't metabolized by CYP enzymes, and tends to be better tolerated than the oral form", "Donepezil is the only cholinesterase inhibitor with evidence across all severities of Alzheimer's disease; the others have the strongest evidence in mild-to-moderate disease", "If symptoms worsen despite a maximum-tolerated dose of one cholinesterase inhibitor for at least 6 months, switching to a different agent in the class is reasonable before giving up on the drug class", "Memantine (NMDA receptor antagonist) can be added to donepezil in patients with an inadequate long-term response, and should be avoided in angle-closure glaucoma, sick sinus syndrome, or left bundle branch block"]}, {"heading": "Benzodiazepines, Z-drugs & triptans", "bullets": ["Lorazepam (LOT drugs: Lorazepam/Oxazepam/Temazepam) — no active metabolites, safer in hepatic impairment/elderly", "Benzo + opioid combo = boxed warning for fatal respiratory depression", "Z-drugs (zopiclone) — short-term use only; Beers Criteria flags them same as benzos for elderly falls/cognition", "Triptans (sumatriptan) contraindicated in CVD, uncontrolled HTN, stroke/TIA history — vasoconstriction risk", "Triptan + triptan/ergot within 24h = contraindicated; limit use <10 days/month to avoid medication-overuse headache", "Migraine with aura + estrogen-containing hormonal contraceptives is a hard contraindication at any age due to increased stroke risk — migraine without aura can be considered case-by-case", "SNOOPPPP red flags (systemic symptoms, neurologic deficits, sudden thunderclap onset, older age >50, pattern change, papilledema, precipitated by valsalva, pregnancy) mean a secondary headache workup, not routine migraine treatment", "Medication-overuse headache develops with simple analgesics used ≥15 days/month, or triptans/opioids used ≥10 days/month, for 3+ months — treatment is tapering/stopping the overused drug plus starting migraine prophylaxis", "CGRP antagonists (ubrogepant, rimegepant) are an acute migraine option for patients who can't take triptans (e.g., cardiovascular contraindications)", "Dihydroergotamine and triptans should never be combined within 24 hours of each other — additive vasoconstriction risk"]}, {"heading": "Key interactions to know", "bullets": ["Serotonin syndrome: SSRI/SNRI + MAOI, triptan, tramadol, or another serotonergic agent", "Lithium levels raised by NSAIDs, ACEi/ARBs, thiazide diuretics", "Valproate inhibits lamotrigine metabolism — requires lower/slower lamotrigine titration", "Carbamazepine (CYP3A4 inducer) reduces efficacy of hormonal contraceptives and warfarin", "Levodopa + non-selective MAOIs = hypertensive crisis", "Triptans are serotonergic and add to serotonin syndrome risk when combined with an SSRI/SNRI or MAOI — the same mechanism as the tramadol/triptan interaction already flagged", "Enzyme-inducing antiepileptics (carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, topiramate ≥200mg/day, perampanel ≥8mg/day) reduce efficacy of hormonal contraceptives; lamotrigine, levetiracetam, valproate, and gabapentin do not induce enzymes", "Carbapenem antibiotics reduce valproic acid levels significantly and can lower the seizure threshold — a dangerous combination in a patient with epilepsy"]}, {"heading": "Red flags requiring urgent action", "bullets": ["Fever + rigidity + autonomic instability + confusion → suspect NMS, stop antipsychotic, seek emergency care", "New rash on lamotrigine or carbamazepine → hold drug, assess for SJS/TEN immediately", "Worsening mood, agitation, or suicidal ideation on any antidepressant, esp. in youth → urgent reassessment", "Coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion on lithium → suspect toxicity, check level, hold dose", "Abrupt levodopa discontinuation with fever/rigidity → parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia crisis, emergency", "Thunderclap headache (peak intensity within 1 minute) → treat as possible subarachnoid hemorrhage, do not treat as migraine until ruled out", "A seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery between them, meets the definition of status epilepticus — treat as a medical emergency (IV lorazepam/midazolam)", "Severe opioid or alcohol withdrawal with autonomic instability, hallucinations, or seizures (delirium tremens) → requires inpatient management, not outpatient taper"]}, {"heading": "Classic exam-trap pearls", "bullets": ["Risperidone is the atypical most associated with hyperprolactinemia/EPS — don't lump it with quetiapine/olanzapine", "Lithium toxicity is precipitated by dehydration/low-sodium diets, not just overdose", "Valproate needs contraception counselling in anyone of childbearing potential — always flag", "NRT is safe post-MI — smoking itself is the danger, not the nicotine patch", "Donepezil + anticholinergic OTC sleep aid = therapeutic antagonism, a common prescribing-cascade trap", "A rash from ampicillin/amoxicillin during acute mononucleosis is not a true drug allergy — the same trap appears whether the topic is infectious disease or general pharmacotherapy", "Valproic acid is first-line for tonic-clonic, tonic-atonic, AND myoclonic generalized seizures — but ethosuximide, not valproate, is first-line for absence seizures", "Many common OTC/prescription drugs (gabapentin, first-gen antihistamines, muscle relaxants, PPIs/H2RAs) carry anticholinergic or cognitive risk in dementia — a frequent prescribing-cascade exam trap beyond just donepezil-anticholinergic pairing"]}, {"heading": "Seizures & antiepileptic drug selection", "bullets": ["First-line AED by seizure type: focal (lamotrigine, levetiracetam, lacosamide, oxcarbazepine, carbamazepine), tonic-clonic/tonic-atonic/myoclonic (valproic acid, plus lamotrigine or levetiracetam depending on type), absence (ethosuximide)", "Enzyme-inducing AEDs (carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, primidone, oxcarbazepine, high-dose topiramate/perampanel) reduce efficacy of hormonal contraceptives — barrier methods, depot progesterone, or an IUD are unaffected alternatives", "Idiosyncratic skin rash risk (including SJS/TEN) is highest with phenytoin, carbamazepine, and lamotrigine, usually within the first 6-8 weeks — stop the drug immediately at the first sign of a rash", "Avoid valproate, phenobarbital, and primidone in pregnancy; lamotrigine and levetiracetam are considered safer choices, though their levels can fall during pregnancy and need monitoring", "Status epilepticus (seizure >5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery) is treated with IV lorazepam or midazolam first-line, then IV phenytoin or propofol", "AEDs are usually lifelong, but discontinuation can be considered after ≥2 years seizure-free with a normal neuro exam and EEG — treat the patient, not the drug level, since therapeutic ranges are reference points, not treatment targets"]}, {"heading": "Substance withdrawal syndromes", "bullets": ["Alcohol withdrawal begins hours to days after the last drink; mild symptoms (tremor, anxiety, insomnia, sweating) can progress to seizures, hallucinations, and delirium tremens (~5% mortality)", "Benzodiazepines are first-line for alcohol withdrawal, especially with a PAWSS score ≥4 (high risk); long-acting agents (diazepam, chlordiazepoxide) are preferred in general adults, while intermediate-acting agents (lorazepam, oxazepam) are safer in the elderly or significant liver disease", "Thiamine 100-200 mg (given before or with glucose) is routine in alcohol withdrawal to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy — giving glucose first in a thiamine-deficient patient can precipitate it", "Opioid withdrawal is rarely life-threatening but is very uncomfortable (autonomic hyperactivity, GI symptoms, myalgia, yawning, piloerection); onset and duration depend on the opioid's half-life", "Buprenorphine/naloxone is first-line for opioid use disorder — its ceiling effect on respiratory depression makes it safer than methadone, though it can precipitate withdrawal if started too soon after the last opioid dose", "Methadone has no ceiling dose and a long half-life (24-36h); initial doses must be witnessed and can prolong the QTc — an ECG and drug interaction check are part of safe initiation"]}]}, {"topic": "infd", "title": "Infectious Disease — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Empiric Therapy by Syndrome", "bullets": ["Uncomplicated cystitis: nitrofurantoin x5d, TMP-SMX x3d, or fosfomycin single dose (first-line)", "Pyelonephritis: fluoroquinolone or ceftriaxone; avoid nitrofurantoin (no tissue penetration)", "CAP outpatient, healthy: amoxicillin or doxycycline or macrolide", "CAP with comorbidities: amoxicillin-clavulanate + macrolide, or respiratory fluoroquinolone", "Cellulitis (non-purulent): cephalexin or cloxacillin; add MRSA coverage (TMP-SMX/doxycycline) if purulent/abscess", "Animal/human bite: amoxicillin-clavulanate (covers Pasteurella + anaerobes)", "Bacterial meningitis empiric therapy by age: neonates get ampicillin + cefotaxime (or an aminoglycoside); infants/children get a 3rd-gen cephalosporin + vancomycin; adults >50 or immunocompromised add ampicillin for Listeria coverage", "Sepsis/septic shock: start broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within the first hour — mortality rises with every hour of delay — then narrow once cultures return", "Otitis media/sinusitis first-line remains amoxicillin (high-dose) in most patients; amoxicillin-clavulanate is reserved for recent antibiotic exposure, treatment failure, or concurrent conjunctivitis", "Diabetic foot infection and complicated intra-abdominal infection empiric regimens need broader gram-negative and anaerobic coverage than simple skin/urinary infections, reflecting polymicrobial risk", "Antibiotics for a COPD exacerbation are reserved for a prior positive sputum culture, need for ventilation, or ≥2 of increased dyspnea/sputum volume/sputum purulence (with purulence required)"]}, {"heading": "Penicillin Allergy Pearls", "bullets": ["True IgE-mediated allergy: avoid all penicillins; cephalosporin cross-reactivity is low (~1-2%) but caution with shared side-chain agents", "Ampicillin/amoxicillin rash + mononucleosis is NOT a true allergy — common exam trap", "Severe reactions (anaphylaxis, SJS/TEN) = avoid entire beta-lactam class including cephalosporins/carbapenems", "For meningitis in a patient with a severe beta-lactam allergy, alternative regimens substitute vancomycin + moxifloxacin (± TMP-SMX for Listeria coverage in those >50) for the standard cephalosporin-based regimen", "Meropenem has under 1% cross-reactivity with penicillin and may be tolerated even after anaphylaxis, with close monitoring — carbapenems are not automatically off-limits in a severe beta-lactam allergy", "A single mild reaction like isolated hives (without anaphylaxis features) still allows a carbapenem to be considered when Listeria coverage is needed and no better option exists", "In severe skin reactions (SJS/TEN, DRESS), avoid the entire beta-lactam class, including meropenem — vancomycin/TMP-SMX-based regimens are used instead"]}, {"heading": "Key Contraindications & Renal Cutoffs", "bullets": ["Nitrofurantoin: avoid CrCl <30 mL/min (ineffective + toxic)", "TMP-SMX: avoid near-term pregnancy (kernicterus); avoid 1st trimester (neural tube defects)", "Fluoroquinolones: boxed warning tendon rupture, aortic dissection, avoid first-line in uncomplicated cystitis", "Doxycycline/tetracyclines: avoid in pregnancy and children <8 years (teeth/bone)", "Ceftriaxone: no renal adjustment needed (biliary excretion) — do not give with IV calcium in neonates", "Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock unresponsive to fluids; vasopressin is added as an adjunct (never as monotherapy) to reduce the norepinephrine dose needed", "Routine corticosteroids are NOT recommended in septic shock (no mortality benefit) — reserve for patients on chronic steroids or with adrenal insufficiency", "Dexamethasone for bacterial meningitis should be given before or with the first antibiotic dose — giving it late loses most of its benefit for preventing hearing loss and neurologic sequelae", "Meningococcal/H. influenzae chemoprophylaxis (rifampin or ciprofloxacin, ceftriaxone in pregnancy) is for close contacts only, not routine treatment of the index patient"]}, {"heading": "Classic Drug Interactions", "bullets": ["Metronidazole + alcohol = disulfiram-like reaction", "Macrolides/azoles = CYP3A4 inhibition → statin toxicity (hold simvastatin/lovastatin with clarithromycin)", "Fluoroquinolones/tetracyclines + divalent cations (Ca, Mg, Fe, Al, Zn) = chelation, reduced absorption — separate 2-6h", "TMP-SMX + warfarin = increased INR (CYP2C9 inhibition)", "Clarithromycin + colchicine (renal/hepatic impairment) = contraindicated, fatal toxicity risk", "Rifampin (used for meningococcal prophylaxis) is a potent enzyme inducer that reduces efficacy of hormonal contraceptives and warfarin", "Linezolid is a weak MAO inhibitor — avoid with serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, SNRIs, triptans) to prevent serotonin syndrome", "Vancomycin plus other nephrotoxic or ototoxic drugs (aminoglycosides, loop diuretics) compounds toxicity risk — extra caution with combination therapy in sepsis", "Carbapenems significantly lower valproic acid levels and can trigger breakthrough seizures — a dangerous combination in patients with epilepsy who develop an infection"]}, {"heading": "Monitoring Parameters", "bullets": ["Vancomycin: AUC/MIC or trough levels, SCr (nephrotoxicity), hearing (ototoxicity)", "Aminoglycosides: peak/trough levels, SCr, hearing", "Long-term nitrofurantoin: pulmonary function/symptoms", "Fluoroquinolones/macrolides: QTc if other QT-prolonging drugs on board", "Sepsis resuscitation targets within the first 6 hours: central venous pressure 8-12 mmHg, mean arterial pressure >65 mmHg, urine output >0.5 mL/kg/hour, and central venous oxygen saturation >70%", "Bacterial meningitis: expect symptom and CSF/blood chemistry improvement within 1-2 days of appropriate antibiotics — lack of improvement should prompt repeat imaging/LP", "Antiepileptic drugs: baseline CBC and LFTs, repeated at 4-6 weeks then annually once stable; bone density testing after 2-5 years of use given fracture risk with enzyme-inducing agents and valproate"]}, {"heading": "Red Flags — Escalate Care", "bullets": ["Rash + fever + mucosal involvement = possible SJS/TEN, stop drug immediately", "Sudden tendon pain/swelling on fluoroquinolone = stop drug, avoid activity", "Severe/bloody diarrhea on any antibiotic = think C. difficile, consider oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin", "New confusion + antibiotic in elderly = consider drug toxicity/accumulation (renal dosing)", "Fever + neck stiffness + altered mental status (classic meningitis triad) → urgent lumbar puncture and immediate empiric antibiotics plus dexamethasone, don't wait for imaging if no contraindication", "qSOFA ≥2 (respiratory rate ≥22, systolic BP ≤100, altered mental status) flags a patient at high risk of sepsis mortality — escalate rapidly, start antibiotics within the hour", "Thunderclap-onset headache with fever and confusion should raise suspicion for meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage — treat as an emergency, not routine headache"]}, {"heading": "Antifungals & Antivirals Quick Hits", "bullets": ["Fluconazole: CYP inhibitor, single 150mg dose for uncomplicated vaginal candidiasis", "Oseltamivir: start within 48h of flu symptom onset, renal dose adjust <60 mL/min", "Valacyclovir: start within 72h of zoster rash, hydrate well (crystalline nephropathy risk)", "Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin) are first-line for invasive candidiasis in critically ill patients — broad activity, low toxicity, minimal drug interactions compared to azoles", "Amphotericin B is reserved for severe/refractory fungal infections given its significant nephrotoxicity and infusion-related reactions", "TMP-SMX is first-line prophylaxis and treatment for Pneumocystis pneumonia in HIV/immunocompromised patients", "Voriconazole has extensive CYP2C19/3A4 interactions and requires level monitoring — a common cause of under- or over-dosing in antifungal therapy"]}, {"heading": "Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Nitrofurantoin turns urine brown — harmless, not a reason to stop", "Metronidazole causes metallic taste — expected, not an allergy", "Ceftriaxone is the ONE cephalosporin that does NOT need renal dose adjustment", "A macrolide rash reaction during mono is expected with ampicillin/amoxicillin, not true allergy", "'Culture and sensitivity pending' + sick patient = start empiric broad coverage, narrow later (de-escalation)", "SIRS criteria are largely retired in current sepsis screening — qSOFA, NEWS, and MEWS are the tools tested now", "Dexamethasone timing matters more than the antibiotic choice for preventing meningitis-related hearing loss — give it before or with the first antibiotic dose, not after", "'Broad spectrum first, narrow later' is the sepsis and meningitis mantra — the same de-escalation principle from routine antibiotic stewardship applies even in life-threatening infection", "Vasopressin is always an add-on to norepinephrine, never a first-line monotherapy vasopressor, in septic shock"]}, {"heading": "Sepsis & septic shock management", "bullets": ["Sepsis is a dysregulated host response to infection; qSOFA (respiratory rate ≥22, SBP ≤100, altered mental status), NEWS, and MEWS have replaced SIRS as the standard screening tools", "Therapeutic approach mnemonic BAPA: Breathing (oxygen/ventilation), Access (central line), Perfusion (fluids ± vasopressors), Antibiotics (broad-spectrum IV within the first hour)", "Crystalloids (normal saline, Ringer's lactate) are first-line fluid resuscitation; hyperoncotic starch solutions are avoided due to increased kidney injury and mortality", "Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor for fluid-refractory septic shock; vasopressin is added on to reduce the norepinephrine dose, and dobutamine is reserved for low cardiac output/late shock given its inotropic effect", "Routine corticosteroids don't improve sepsis mortality and are reserved for patients already on steroids or with suspected adrenal insufficiency (hydrocortisone 200 mg/day IV in divided doses)", "Blood transfusion in sepsis is reserved for hemoglobin <70 g/L or active hemorrhage — a restrictive transfusion strategy, not a fixed target"]}, {"heading": "Bacterial meningitis", "bullets": ["Classic triad: fever, neck stiffness (nuchal rigidity), and altered mental status — though all three are present in only a minority of adult cases", "Empiric therapy: neonates get ampicillin + cefotaxime (or an aminoglycoside); ages 1 month-50 years get a 3rd-generation cephalosporin + vancomycin; patients over 50 or immunocompromised add ampicillin for Listeria coverage", "Dexamethasone reduces hearing loss and neurologic sequelae, particularly for S. pneumoniae in adults and H. influenzae in children — give it before or with the first antibiotic dose for maximum benefit", "Severe beta-lactam allergy alternative regimens substitute vancomycin + moxifloxacin (± TMP-SMX in patients >50 for Listeria coverage) for the cephalosporin-based regimen", "Chemoprophylaxis for close contacts of N. meningitidis or H. influenzae cases uses rifampin or ciprofloxacin (ceftriaxone in pregnancy) — this is prevention for contacts, not treatment for the patient", "Duration of targeted therapy varies by organism: 7 days for N. meningitidis, 7-10 for H. influenzae, 10-14 for S. pneumoniae, and 14-21+ days for Listeria or gram-negative bacilli"]}]}, {"topic": "renal", "title": "Renal & Electrolytes — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Diuretics by Class", "bullets": ["Loop (furosemide, bumetanide): works even at low GFR, causes hypoK/hypoMg/hypoNa/metabolic alkalosis, ototoxicity risk if rapid IV/high dose", "Thiazide (HCTZ, chlorthalidone): loses efficacy at eGFR <30, causes hypoK/hypoNa/hyperCa/hyperuricemia/hyperglycemia", "Potassium-sparing (spironolactone, eplerenone, amiloride): risk of hyperkalemia, avoid/caution with ACEi/ARB or eGFR <30", "Spironolactone causes gynecomastia (antiandrogen effect) — eplerenone is selective alternative", "Amiloride/triamterene block ENaC channels directly in the collecting duct (not aldosterone receptor antagonists like spironolactone/eplerenone) — same net K-sparing effect", "Osmotic diuretics (mannitol, glucose, urea) can cause hypovolemic hyponatremia and are a less common renal-loss cause of AKI", "IV loop diuretics in AKI/fluid overload should only be given AFTER hypovolemia is corrected — diuresing a volume-depleted patient worsens pre-renal AKI", "Thiazides are considered ineffective (not just riskier) at eGFR <30 — avoid rather than dose-adjust"]}, {"heading": "Hyperkalemia Management Ladder", "bullets": ["Stabilize cardiac membrane: IV calcium gluconate (does not lower K, protects heart)", "Shift K+ intracellularly: insulin + glucose, salbutamol (fast, temporary)", "Remove K+ from body: patiromer (onset ~7h) or sodium zirconium cyclosilicate (onset ~1h) for chronic; dialysis for emergent/refractory", "Sodium bicarbonate can help shift K+ intracellularly if concurrent metabolic acidosis present", "Potassium binders are NOT for acute life-threatening hyperkalemia as monotherapy — too slow", "Severity thresholds: mild 5.0-5.9, moderate 6.0-6.4, severe ≥6.5 mmol/L; ECG changes (peaked T waves, widened QRS, shortened QT) mean cardiac monitoring + calcium first regardless of exact level", "Calcium gluconate 1g IV bolus: immediate onset, lasts 30-60 min, stabilizes membrane only — repeat if ECG changes persist since it does not lower serum K+", "Insulin 10 units IV + 25-40g of 50% dextrose: onset ~15 min, lasts 4-6h; give enough dextrose or hypoglycemia is the main complication", "Nebulized salbutamol 20mg: onset ~30 min, lasts ~2h, causes transient tachycardia; effect is additive when combined with insulin", "IV furosemide 40-80mg eliminates K+ but risks hypotension from volume depletion — only appropriate if hypervolemic with no urinary obstruction"]}, {"heading": "CKD-MBD (Mineral Bone Disease) Management", "bullets": ["Phosphate binders (sevelamer, calcium carbonate) must be taken WITH meals to bind dietary phosphate", "Non-calcium binders (sevelamer) preferred if vascular calcification risk or hypercalcemia present", "Calcitriol (active vitamin D) needed because failing kidneys lose 1-alpha-hydroxylase — suppresses PTH, raises Ca/phosphate", "Cinacalcet (calcimimetic) LOWERS calcium while suppressing PTH — opposite calcium effect vs. vitamin D analogs", "Target: control PTH without over-suppressing (risk of adynamic bone disease)", "Calcium-based binders (calcium carbonate/acetate) are first-choice for cost/convenience but risk hypercalcemia — need acidic gastric pH to dissolve, so H2RA/PPI co-use blunts effect (except calcium citrate)", "Lanthanum: large chewable tablets that must be fully chewed; separate other drug dosing by 2h due to interaction risk", "Sucroferric oxyhydroxide: newest binder, contains iron with minimal absorption; causes stool discoloration and diarrhea, especially initially", "Aluminum-based binders are potent but avoided — no safe dose, risk of aluminum toxicity/dialysis encephalopathy, not guideline-recommended", "Renal osteodystrophy spans high-turnover bone disease (osteitis fibrosa cystica from ↑PTH) and low-turnover disease (adynamic bone/osteomalacia from PTH over-suppression or aluminum)"]}, {"heading": "Renal Dose Adjustment — Classic Examples", "bullets": ["Nitrofurantoin: avoid CrCl <30", "Gabapentin: significant reduction needed, accumulation causes myoclonus/encephalopathy in renal failure", "Digoxin: reduce dose, narrow therapeutic index (target 0.5-0.9 ng/mL), toxicity worsened by hypoK/hypoMg/hyperCa", "Enoxaparin: dose reduce CrCl <30, consider anti-Xa monitoring", "Metformin: avoid/reduce per eGFR thresholds (contraindicated <30, caution 30-45)", "Allopurinol, colchicine, and sulfonamides also require dose reduction in CKD, in addition to gabapentin/pregabalin", "Glyburide accumulates and causes prolonged hypoglycemia in CKD — use gliclazide instead", "Meperidine accumulates its neurotoxic metabolite (normeperidine) in renal impairment, raising seizure risk — avoid", "Long-acting opioids should be avoided in CKD in favor of short-acting formulations due to drowsiness from accumulation", "Cockcroft-Gault (not eGFR/CKD-EPI) remains the standard equation for drug dosing purposes; eGFR is used for CKD staging"]}, {"heading": "Anemia of CKD (ESAs)", "bullets": ["Epoetin alfa / darbepoetin alfa stimulate erythropoiesis — boxed warning for CV death/MI/stroke/VTE if Hgb target too high", "Target Hgb 100-115 g/L; avoid normalizing to >130 g/L", "Correct iron deficiency (ferritin/TSAT) BEFORE/DURING ESA therapy — iron deficiency blunts response", "Uncontrolled hypertension is a contraindication until BP controlled", "Initiate iron if ferritin <100 ng/mL and TSAT <40% (non-dialysis/PD) or ferritin ≤500 ng/mL and TSAT ≤30% (on hemodialysis); can withhold iron once ferritin >700 ng/mL or TSAT ≥40%", "IV iron is more effective than oral in CKD but needs monitoring for hypersensitivity/anaphylactoid reactions and hypotension; oral iron causes GI intolerance and poor compliance", "Epoetin alfa (shorter half-life, dosed up to 3x/week) vs. darbepoetin alfa (longer half-life/higher potency, weekly-to-monthly dosing, less immunogenic)", "ESA hyporesponsiveness — check iron studies (ferritin/TSAT) before increasing the ESA dose; iron deficiency is the first differential"]}, {"heading": "Acid-Base & Bicarbonate", "bullets": ["KDIGO recommends oral sodium bicarbonate if serum bicarb persistently <22 mmol/L to slow CKD progression", "Watch for volume overload/hypertension worsening from sodium load", "Metabolic acidosis in CKD relates to reduced ammoniagenesis and acid excretion as nephrons are lost", "Anion gap = (Na+ + K+) − (Cl− + HCO3−), normal 5-12 mmol/L; elevated AG metabolic acidosis in CKD/AKI classically driven by uremia, lactate, and ketones (mnemonic CAT MUDPILES)", "Acute severe metabolic acidosis may need IV bicarbonate; chronic CKD-associated acidosis is corrected with oral sodium bicarbonate", "Distinguish anion-gap (uremia, lactate, ketones, toxins) vs. normal-anion-gap (GI bicarbonate loss, renal tubular acidosis) metabolic acidosis when working up CKD/AKI acid-base status"]}, {"heading": "Monitoring Parameters", "bullets": ["Diuretics: electrolytes (K, Na, Mg), renal function, blood pressure, daily weights", "ESAs: Hgb (q2-4 weeks during titration), iron studies, blood pressure", "Phosphate binders/vitamin D/cinacalcet: serum calcium, phosphate, PTH regularly", "Potassium binders: serum potassium and magnesium (patiromer can cause hypomagnesemia)", "Monitor K+ and SCr/eGFR within 2 weeks of starting or increasing an ACEi/ARB dose (sooner in high-risk patients)", "CKD therapeutic targets: SBP <120 mmHg, LDL <2.0 mmol/L, A1C <7% (range 6.5-8%), urine ACR <3 mg/mmol or PCR <50 mg/mmol", "Cinacalcet: check calcium after 1 week, then every ~3 weeks due to hypocalcemia risk"]}, {"heading": "Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Thiazides RAISE calcium (favor them in patients with osteoporosis/hypercalciuric stones); loops LOWER calcium", "Patiromer/SZC are for CHRONIC hyperkalemia — not fast enough for a crashing hyperkalemic emergency", "Ceftriaxone needs no renal adjustment (biliary clearance) — contrast with nitrofurantoin (avoid <30) as opposite examples", "Spironolactone + ACEi/ARB + K supplement = classic triple-hit hyperkalemia combination to flag", "Cinacalcet lowers calcium; calcitriol raises calcium — easy to mix up on exam, both lower PTH", "IV potassium must NEVER be given as a push — infusion only, with cardiac monitoring required if the rate exceeds 10 mEq/hour", "Sick-day mnemonic SADMANS — hold Sulfonylureas, ACEi, Diuretics, Metformin, ARBs, NSAIDs, SGLT2i during acute illness/dehydration to prevent AKI", "Metformin must be held at/before iodinated contrast administration if eGFR <30, due to lactic acidosis risk — a separate trigger from the general eGFR<30 contraindication"]}, {"heading": "AKI — Pre-renal vs. Intrinsic vs. Post-renal", "bullets": ["Pre-renal (~70% of cases): hypoperfusion from hypovolemia, systemic vasodilation (sepsis), or intrarenal vasoconstriction (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB, contrast) — functional and reversible if perfusion is restored quickly", "Intrinsic (~10%): structural damage — tubular (ischemia, aminoglycosides, contrast, rhabdomyolysis), glomerular (glomerulonephritis), interstitial (most often drug-induced), or vascular", "Post-renal (~20%): obstruction to urine outflow — BPH, tumors, kidney stones, tumor lysis syndrome, neurogenic bladder", "KDIGO AKI definition: SCr rise ≥26.5 µmol/L within 48h, OR SCr ≥1.5x baseline within 7 days, OR urine output <0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours", "eGFR is NOT reliable for diagnosing AKI (assumes steady-state creatinine) — use the SCr trend and urine output instead", "Tumor lysis syndrome prevention: hydration plus allopurinol (xanthine oxidase inhibitor, started 1-2 days pre-chemo) or rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase, for higher-risk patients) to prevent uric acid nephropathy", "Contrast-induced AKI prevention: use the lowest volume of low-osmolar (non-ionic) contrast, with IV isotonic fluids pre/post-procedure; sodium bicarbonate/N-acetylcysteine have weak evidence"]}, {"heading": "Electrolyte Disorders — Na, Mg, Ca, Phosphate Quick Reference", "bullets": ["Hyponatremia correction rate: chronic (>48h) correct slowly ≤10 mEq/L/24h — too-fast correction risks central pontine myelinolysis; acute (<48h) can be corrected more quickly", "SIADH drug causes: SSRIs/SNRIs, TCAs, carbamazepine, antipsychotics, cyclophosphamide/vincristine, NSAIDs, opioids — causes euvolemic hyponatremia, treated with fluid restriction", "Hypomagnesemia drug causes: diuretics, aminoglycosides, chronic PPI use; magnesium is a required cofactor for potassium reabsorption — refractory hypokalemia despite repletion suggests concurrent hypomagnesemia", "Hypermagnesemia treatment: IV calcium gluconate/chloride antagonizes cardiac/neuromuscular effects; most often caused by Mg-containing antacids/laxatives in CKD patients", "Hypocalcemia drug causes: IV bisphosphonates (more than oral), denosumab, PPIs, cinacalcet; treat acute symptomatic hypocalcemia with IV calcium, chronic with oral calcium plus vitamin D/calcitriol", "Hypercalcemia drug causes: thiazides, lithium, teriparatide; treatment sequence is hydration first, then IV bisphosphonates, calcitonin, or loop diuretics (only after volume repletion)", "Never use an agent that combines two electrolytes in the wrong direction — e.g., avoid potassium phosphate to correct hypophosphatemia in a patient who is also hyperkalemic", "Key normal ranges: Na 136-146, K 3.5-5.1, Mg 0.74-1.03 mmol/L, Ca (total) 2.12-2.52 mmol/L, phosphate 0.81-1.58 mmol/L"]}]}, {"topic": "gi", "title": "GI & Hepatic — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "GERD / Acid Suppression Ladder", "bullets": ["Lifestyle first: weight loss, avoid trigger foods, elevate head of bed, avoid late meals", "Step-up: antacids/OTC H2RA → PPI (most effective for erosive esophagitis)", "PPIs taken 30-60 min before breakfast for max efficacy — needs active proton pumps", "Long-term PPI risks: B12/Mg deficiency, fracture risk, C. diff, rebound hyperacidity on stopping", "Alarm features requiring referral/endoscopy: dysphagia, weight loss, GI bleed, age >60 new onset", "Red flags requiring referral/endoscopy: dysphagia, odynophagia, unexplained weight loss, GI bleed, anemia, persistent vomiting, new onset age >50-60 — same triage logic used for dyspepsia", "Barrett's esophagus screening is warranted with chronic GERD symptoms plus one of: male sex, age >50, Caucasian, central obesity, smoking history, or family history of Barrett's/esophageal cancer", "PPI trial for GERD is 4-8 weeks; if symptoms resolve, taper off; if not, refer for endoscopy (stop PPI 2-4 weeks before the procedure)", "All PPIs have similar efficacy — choice is driven by interactions/cost/formulation, not superior acid suppression"]}, {"heading": "H. pylori Eradication", "bullets": ["Standard triple: PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin (or metronidazole if PCN-allergic) x 7-14 days", "Quadruple (bismuth): PPI + bismuth subsalicylate + metronidazole + tetracycline — preferred if clarithromycin resistance/prior macrolide exposure", "Confirm eradication with urea breath test or stool antigen ≥4 weeks post-therapy, off PPI ≥1-2 weeks before test", "Test-and-treat approach for uninvestigated dyspepsia in younger patients without alarm features", "PBMT (bismuth quadruple: PPI BID + bismuth QID + metronidazole QID + tetracycline QID) is first-line if penicillin-allergic", "Restricted first-line triple options (PAC, PMC, PAM) are only appropriate where local clarithromycin resistance is <15% or local eradication rates exceed 85%", "Treatment failure: switch classes — if PAMC failed use PBMT next (and vice versa); if triple therapy was already used, escalate to quadruple therapy", "Third-line after two failures: PAR (PPI+amoxicillin+rifabutin) is preferred over PAL (add levofloxacin), except with known levofloxacin sensitivity"]}, {"heading": "Antiemetics — Match Mechanism to Cause", "bullets": ["Ondansetron (5-HT3 antagonist): chemo/post-op nausea; watch QT prolongation", "Metoclopramide (D2 antagonist/prokinetic): gastroparesis, limit to <12 weeks (EPS/tardive dyskinesia boxed warning)", "Domperidone: peripheral D2 antagonist, less EPS than metoclopramide but QT/cardiac risk — avoid in cardiac disease, age >60 caution", "Dimenhydrinate: motion sickness, sedating, anticholinergic — caution in elderly/glaucoma/BPH", "Domperidone does NOT cross the BBB (less EPS, still carries QT risk) vs. metoclopramide, which DOES cross the BBB (higher EPS/tardive dyskinesia risk)", "5-HT3 antagonists (ondansetron, granisetron) risk serotonin syndrome when combined with other serotonergic drugs and decrease tramadol's analgesic effect", "Haloperidol is used for PONV/refractory CINV at low doses — highest EPS risk of the antiemetics; use caution with other QT-prolonging combinations", "First-line for nausea/vomiting of pregnancy: pyridoxine (B6) alone or doxylamine/pyridoxine (Diclectin), taken on a SCHEDULED (not PRN) basis; add dimenhydrinate if inadequate"]}, {"heading": "Laxative Selection", "bullets": ["PEG 3350 (osmotic): first-line for chronic constipation, well tolerated, safe long-term", "Docusate: stool softener, prevention/prophylaxis only — weak evidence to treat established constipation", "Senna (stimulant): onset 6-12h, dose at bedtime; first-line add-on for opioid-induced constipation", "Lactulose: constipation AND hepatic encephalopathy (titrate to 2-3 soft stools/day) — expect flatulence", "PEG 3350 has an onset of 1-3 days, is more effective and better tolerated than lactulose, and should be separated from other medications by 2 hours", "Bulk-forming agents (psyllium) are contraindicated in fecal impaction and must be taken with at least 8oz of water to prevent obstruction (onset 12-72h)", "Osmotic saline laxatives (magnesium citrate/hydroxide, sodium phosphate enema) have rapid onset but contain electrolytes — avoid in renal failure/cardiac disease", "Prucalopride (5-HT4 agonist) is reserved for chronic idiopathic constipation refractory to standard laxatives; discontinue if no response after 4 weeks", "PAMORAs (methylnaltrexone, naloxegol) are reserved for opioid-induced constipation refractory to laxatives; naloxegol is contraindicated with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors"]}, {"heading": "Antidiarrheals — Red Flags", "bullets": ["Loperamide: avoid in bloody diarrhea/fever (invasive infection risk, toxic megacolon)", "High-dose/misuse loperamide → QT prolongation, torsades — Health Canada safety warning", "Bismuth subsalicylate: causes harmless black stool/tongue; avoid in children with viral illness (Reye's), ASA allergy", "Loperamide max 16 mg/day OTC; discontinue if diarrhea persists beyond 48 hours; contraindicated <2 years old, avoid <12 years old", "Diphenoxylate/atropine (Lomotil) should be avoided in pregnancy/breastfeeding, is less effective than loperamide, and has atropine added specifically to deter abuse", "Empiric antibiotics are generally NOT recommended for acute diarrhea (self-limiting) — reserve for severe dysentery, signs of sepsis, or immunocompromised patients with bloody diarrhea", "Cholestyramine treats bile-acid diarrhea (e.g., in Crohn's disease) but binds other drugs — separate other dosing by 1 hour before or 4-6 hours after"]}, {"heading": "IBD (Ulcerative Colitis) Basics", "bullets": ["5-ASA (mesalamine) first-line for mild-moderate UC; formulation matched to disease location (topical for distal, oral+rectal for pancolitis)", "Sulfasalazine = sulfapyridine + 5-ASA; more side effects, give folic acid supplementation", "Monitor renal function with mesalamine (rare nephrotoxicity)", "Combination oral + rectal therapy > oral alone for distal UC", "UC = continuous, mucosal-only inflammation limited to the colon/rectum starting at the rectum; Crohn's = patchy, transmural, can affect mouth-to-anus with typical rectal sparing", "Corticosteroids (budesonide, prednisone) are first-line to INDUCE remission in moderate-severe UC/CD but are never used for maintenance — taper to avoid HPA suppression and relapse", "Thiopurines (azathioprine/6-MP) maintain remission only (not induction), with delayed onset of 3-6 months; check TPMT before starting — low/absent TPMT raises severe myelosuppression risk", "Anti-TNF biologics (infliximab, adalimumab) require screening for TB/hepatitis B/C and updated immunizations before starting; combine with a thiopurine/methotrexate to reduce anti-drug antibody formation and secondary loss of response", "Methotrexate can induce remission in steroid-resistant/dependent Crohn's (not established for UC) — give folic acid, contraindicated in pregnancy, stop 3-12 months pre-conception"]}, {"heading": "Hepatic Disease Pearls", "bullets": ["Lactulose titrated to 2-3 soft BMs/day is mainstay for hepatic encephalopathy (traps ammonia as NH4+)", "Ursodiol: dissolves radiolucent cholesterol gallstones only (not pigment stones); first-line for primary biliary cholangitis", "Acetaminophen max dose lowered (often ≤2g/day) in significant liver disease — avoid NSAIDs (bleeding/renal risk in cirrhosis with varices/ascites)", "Hepatic encephalopathy precipitants include GI bleed/high protein load, infection, constipation, hypokalemia, alkalosis, and dehydration — correcting these matters as much as giving lactulose", "Avoid or minimize benzodiazepines, opioids, and alcohol in cirrhosis/hepatic encephalopathy — all worsen encephalopathy via GABA/CNS depression", "Rifaximin is added if no improvement on lactulose within 24-48h, and continued long-term after a 2nd hepatic encephalopathy episode to prevent recurrence (lactulose is continued after just the 1st episode)", "Child-Pugh score (bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites, encephalopathy) grades cirrhosis severity/prognosis — Class A best, Class C worst"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam Traps", "bullets": ["Ranitidine was withdrawn in Canada (NDMA) — famotidine is the H2RA now used", "Omeprazole/esomeprazole (CYP2C19 inhibitors) reduce clopidogrel activation — use pantoprazole if PPI needed with clopidogrel", "Metoclopramide EPS risk means therapy is capped at ~12 weeks", "Docusate alone is NOT sufficient to treat established hard/impacted constipation", "Trap: thinking PPIs are less effective than H2RAs — the opposite is true; PPIs outperform H2RAs for healing esophagitis and supporting H. pylori eradication", "Trap: using domperidone in pregnancy — contraindicated; metoclopramide is the prokinetic used instead for persistent nausea/vomiting of pregnancy", "Trap: using loperamide or other antidiarrheals in suspected C. difficile — raises toxic megacolon risk; stop antidiarrheals and the causative antibiotic instead", "Trap: assuming docusate effectively treats constipation — evidence supports prevention only, not treatment of existing hard/impacted stool"]}, {"heading": "C. difficile Infection (CDI) — Diagnosis & Treatment", "bullets": ["Risk factors: recent antibiotic exposure (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, broad-spectrum penicillins, 3rd/4th-generation cephalosporins), PPI/H2RA use, hospitalization, advanced age, immunocompromise", "The highest risk window for CDI is the first month AFTER stopping the causative antibiotic, not just during its use", "Non-severe CDI (WBC ≤15,000, SCr <133 µmol/L): fidaxomicin 200mg PO BID x10 days preferred; vancomycin 125mg PO QID x10 days alternative; metronidazole only if neither is available", "Fulminant CDI (severe plus hypotension/shock/ileus/toxic megacolon): vancomycin 500mg PO QID ± IV metronidazole; a vancomycin retention enema can be added if ileus is present", "First recurrence (within 8 weeks): fidaxomicin is preferred, or a tapered/pulsed vancomycin regimen", "Alcohol-based hand sanitizer does NOT kill C. difficile spores — wash hands with soap and water; use chlorine-based disinfectants for environmental cleaning", "Probiotics are NOT routinely recommended for CDI prevention, and antibiotics are not used prophylactically to prevent CDI"]}, {"heading": "Nausea/Vomiting — Special Populations & Types", "bullets": ["Motion sickness: pre-treat 30-60 minutes before exposure with dimenhydrinate or diphenhydramine; targets acetylcholine/histamine pathways", "PONV risk factors: female sex, non-smoker, history of motion sickness/PONV, opioid use — incidence climbs from ~10% to ~80% as risk factors accumulate", "Nausea/vomiting of pregnancy ('morning sickness') generally subsides by ~16 weeks; hyperemesis gravidarum (persistent, severe, with weight loss/dehydration) requires referral", "NVP therapy ladder: pyridoxine ± doxylamine (Diclectin, scheduled not PRN) → add dimenhydrinate → metoclopramide/promethazine/chlorpromazine → IV rehydration ± ondansetron for refractory cases → methylprednisolone only after the 1st trimester for resistant hyperemesis", "Iron-containing prenatal vitamins can be discontinued in the 1st trimester if nausea is severe — substitute folic acid or a low-iron prenatal", "Dimenhydrinate/diphenhydramine are NOT considered effective for chemotherapy-induced nausea/vomiting — use 5-HT3 antagonists, dexamethasone, or NK1 antagonists instead"]}]}, {"topic": "pain", "title": "Pain & MSK — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "WHO-Style Analgesic Ladder", "bullets": ["Step 1: non-opioid (acetaminophen, NSAID) +/- adjuvant", "Step 2: weak opioid (codeine, tramadol) + non-opioid", "Step 3: strong opioid (morphine, hydromorphone, fentanyl) + non-opioid", "Acetaminophen is first-line for OA pain given favourable safety profile; NSAIDs added/used for inflammatory component", "For acute/subacute low back pain, NSAIDs are first-line; acetaminophen is considered largely ineffective for low back pain despite its favourable safety profile", "Muscle relaxants can be added short-term (5-7 days) for acute low back pain/spasm — benefit is seen within the first week, not intended for long-term chronic use", "Duloxetine has a Health Canada-approved indication for chronic low back pain and is a good option in the elderly (avoids NSAID GI/CV/renal risk)", "For chronic non-cancer pain, reserve opioids for moderate-severe pain unresponsive to non-opioids per the 2017 Canadian Opioid Guideline — try weak opioids (tramadol/codeine) before strong controlled-release agents"]}, {"heading": "Acetaminophen Safety", "bullets": ["Max ~4 g/day healthy adults; consider ≤3 g/day chronic use or hepatic risk factors, ≤2 g/day significant liver disease", "Watch for hidden acetaminophen in combo products (Tylenol #3, Tramacet, cold/flu remedies) — stacking risk", "Antidote for overdose: N-acetylcysteine (NAC) — time-critical, based on Rumack-Matthew nomogram", "PRICE protocol (Protect, Rest, Ice, Compress, Elevate) is the acute non-pharm mainstay for sports injuries; heat therapy should be reserved for ≥48h post-injury", "Acetaminophen is preferred over NSAIDs in pregnancy and in elderly patients with persistent MSK pain due to lower GI/CV/renal risk", "Caffeine-free analgesics are preferred for patients with chronic pain and coexisting sleep difficulty"]}, {"heading": "NSAID Risk Triad", "bullets": ["GI: ulcer/bleed risk (COX-1 inhibition) — higher with age, prior ulcer, steroid/anticoagulant co-use", "Renal: avoid/caution in CKD, dehydration, 'triple whammy' (NSAID + ACEi/ARB + diuretic)", "CV: boxed warning class effect, even naproxen (lowest relative CV risk) — use lowest dose/shortest duration", "Timing with low-dose ASA: take ASA first; ibuprofen ≥30-60 min after (or ≥8h before) to preserve antiplatelet effect", "Fluoroquinolones increase tendon rupture risk (especially Achilles) — discontinue immediately at the first sign of tendon pain/swelling; this can occur even months after stopping the antibiotic", "NSAIDs should be avoided in pregnancy, especially the 3rd trimester and beyond 20 weeks (premature ductus arteriosus closure, oligohydramnios) — avoid in the 1st trimester too if possible", "Celecoxib has the lowest GI risk among NSAIDs — consider it when an NSAID is needed in a patient with GI risk factors", "NSAIDs do NOT alter rheumatoid arthritis disease progression — they are short-term adjunctive symptom control only, never disease-modifying therapy"]}, {"heading": "Opioid Equianalgesic & Safety Pearls", "bullets": ["Oral morphine : oral hydromorphone ≈ 5:1 (hydromorphone much more potent per mg)", "Fentanyl patch: ONLY for opioid-tolerant patients, never opioid-naive/acute pain — heat exposure raises absorption, risk of fatal overdose", "Codeine/tramadol: CYP2D6 prodrugs — contraindicated in children <12 and breastfeeding (ultrarapid metabolizer infant toxicity risk)", "Constipation does NOT develop tolerance — always co-prescribe a stimulant laxative prophylactically", "CDSA (controlled substances): no refills permitted on the original opioid prescription — new Rx required each time", "Buprenorphine patch may help chronic pain with central sensitization; well tolerated in the elderly and in renal impairment, but ~20% of patients develop contact dermatitis from the adhesive", "Tramadol has only limited evidence over placebo for chronic low back pain in uncomplicated cases — not a guaranteed win", "Reserve long-acting/controlled-release opioids for patients already using short-acting agents frequently — don't start chronic pain patients on long-acting agents first", "For low back pain, exhaust non-opioid options and multimodal non-pharm therapy before opioids — harms likely outweigh benefits for most chronic LBP patients"]}, {"heading": "Gabapentinoids", "bullets": ["Gabapentin: renally eliminated, nonlinear/saturable absorption (why TID dosing), NOT a controlled substance federally", "Pregabalin: linear PK (BID dosing), IS subject to greater prescribing control/misuse potential than gabapentin", "Both: taper on discontinuation (withdrawal symptoms), caution combining with opioids (additive respiratory depression — Health Canada warning)", "May be useful adjuncts when chronic pain (e.g., neck/back) is present in multiple areas or has a neuropathic component", "Considered by expert opinion for subacute/chronic neck pain alongside duloxetine, TCAs, and tramadol, despite limited high-quality evidence", "Gabapentin's nonlinear, saturable absorption at higher doses is why splitting into TID dosing improves total absorption compared to less frequent dosing"]}, {"heading": "RA / DMARD & Gout Essentials", "bullets": ["Methotrexate for RA is dosed ONCE WEEKLY — classic fatal dosing error if given daily; give folic acid on a different day", "MTX monitoring: CBC, LFTs, renal function; contraindicated in pregnancy (teratogenic), avoid alcohol and live vaccines", "Acute gout flare: colchicine (specific short loading-dose regimen), NSAID, or corticosteroid — NOT allopurinol", "Allopurinol: never start during an active flare; titrate slowly to urate target (<360, or <300 if tophi); flare prophylaxis (colchicine/NSAID) for first 3-6 months of initiation/titration", "Allopurinol + azathioprine/6-MP: major interaction — reduce thiopurine dose ~75% (xanthine oxidase inhibition)", "Allopurinol Hypersensitivity Syndrome (AHS): rare but life-threatening SJS/TEN-like reaction; risk factors include high starting dose, renal impairment, concurrent diuretic use, and the HLA-B*5801 allele (more common in Han Chinese, Thai, Korean, and African American patients)", "Losartan has a mild uricosuric effect — it is the preferred antihypertensive choice in a gout patient who also needs BP treatment; switching HCTZ to losartan is a reasonable exam answer", "ULT candidacy: start if ≥1 tophus, radiographic joint damage, or ≥2 flares/year; consider if first attack plus CKD ≥stage 3, urolithiasis, or serum urate >535 µmol/L — do NOT start for asymptomatic hyperuricemia alone", "Anti-inflammatory prophylaxis (colchicine/NSAID/low-dose steroid) continues for 3-6 months after starting ULT to prevent flares from urate mobilization", "Methotrexate for RA: gold-standard first-line csDMARD, titrate to ≥15 mg/week within 4-6 weeks, switch PO to SC if response is inadequate or GI intolerance occurs (>15mg poorly absorbed orally); contraindicated in pregnancy, breastfeeding, severe renal/hepatic impairment", "Hydroxychloroquine is the least toxic DMARD (no myelosuppression/renal/hepatic toxicity) but needs baseline plus annual (after 5 years) eye exams for retinopathy risk — used first-line for mild RA"]}, {"heading": "Muscle Relaxants", "bullets": ["Cyclobenzaprine: TCA-like structure, sedating/anticholinergic; short-term use only (~2-3 weeks); Beers List — caution in elderly", "Works centrally (CNS sedation), not a direct anti-inflammatory or peripheral muscle-acting agent", "Additive CNS depression with opioids/benzos/alcohol — counsel on driving and combination risk", "Baclofen, tizanidine, methocarbamol, and orphenadrine are alternatives to cyclobenzaprine — all sedating, all intended for short-term (5-7 day) use only", "Orphenadrine is contraindicated in bladder obstruction, angle-closure glaucoma, achalasia, and myasthenia gravis due to its anticholinergic effects", "Muscle relaxants provide more benefit in acute pain/spasm than in chronic pain, where supporting evidence is weak"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Methotrexate weekly (not daily) dosing error is one of the most tested pharmacist-verification scenarios", "Fentanyl patch in an opioid-naive patient = dangerous/contraindicated", "Allopurinol started during an acute flare can worsen/prolong it — start after flare resolves", "Hydromorphone potency (~5x oral morphine) — mixing up equianalgesic ratios is a common calculation trap", "NSAID + low-dose ASA timing matters for preserving cardioprotection — not just 'avoid combining'", "Trap: assuming NSAIDs are disease-modifying in RA — they only control symptoms and never alter disease progression", "Trap: believing ULT can never be started during a gout flare — it CAN be started during an acute flare (with anti-inflammatory prophylaxis), contrary to older teaching", "Trap: forgetting fluoroquinolone-induced tendinopathy as a red-flag drug cause of new tendon pain, especially at the Achilles", "Trap: overlooking that NSAIDs are not disease progression-modifying nor first-line for RA erosive disease — DMARDs are the mainstay"]}, {"heading": "Low Back Pain, Neck Pain & Sports Injury Red Flags", "bullets": ["Cauda equina syndrome is a medical emergency: loss of anal sphincter tone, saddle anesthesia, bilateral leg weakness, urinary retention/overflow incontinence — refer immediately", "Back pain red flags: age >50 with new pain, unexplained weight loss, history of cancer, IV drug use/infection signs, major trauma, history of osteoporosis/steroid use (fracture risk)", "Sports injury red flags: >2 weeks since injury, inability to bear weight, obvious deformity, age <2 or >50, systemic symptoms (fever, confusion)", "PRICE protocol for acute sports injury: Protect, Rest (early mobilization is better than prolonged rest), Ice (10-15 min QID for the first 48h), Compress, Elevate; avoid heat until ≥48h post-injury", "Avoid bed rest for acute low back pain — encourage continuation of normal activity, which speeds recovery compared to rest", "Yellow flags (psychosocial risk factors for chronicity): fear-avoidance beliefs, passive coping, social withdrawal, claims/compensation issues — refer for CBT/case management if present"]}, {"heading": "Osteoporosis & Bone Health Basics", "bullets": ["Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, zoledronic acid) are first-line antiresorptive agents — they bind bone mineral and inhibit osteoclast activity; oral agents are preferred over IV zoledronic acid for ease of administration", "Oral bisphosphonate counselling: take on an empty stomach 30 minutes before the first food/drink other than water, remain upright for 30 minutes, and avoid other medications/minerals/dairy within 30-60 minutes due to absorption interference", "Denosumab (RANKL inhibitor, SC every 6 months) causes rebound rapid bone loss and vertebral fracture risk if a dose is delayed or discontinued — stopping requires bridging with a bisphosphonate for about 1 year", "Rare but serious bisphosphonate/denosumab class effects: osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femoral fractures, especially with bisphosphonate use beyond 6 years", "Teriparatide (PTH analogue, anabolic) builds bone rather than just slowing resorption, but is limited to 24 months of total use due to osteosarcoma risk seen in animal studies", "Bisphosphonate drug holiday: after 3-6 years of therapy (up to 6 years if high fracture risk), consider stopping and reassessing in about 3 years — unlike denosumab, benefit persists somewhat after stopping", "FRAX (preferred in Canada) estimates 10-year major fracture risk using age, sex, BMI, prior fracture, parental hip fracture, steroid use, secondary causes, smoking, and alcohol ± femoral neck T-score; osteoporosis is diagnosed if 10-year risk ≥20% OR a low-trauma fracture occurs after age 40 in someone ≥50"]}]}, {"topic": "womens", "title": "Women's Health — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Combined Hormonal Contraceptives (CHC) — Absolute Contraindications", "bullets": ["Smoker AND age ≥35 → no estrogen, ever", "Migraine WITH aura at any age → CHC absolutely contraindicated (stroke risk)", "Migraine without aura + age ≥35 → caution/avoid estrogen", "History of VTE, or known thrombophilia → no estrogen", "Uncontrolled HTN (≥160/100), known CVD, or complicated valvular disease → avoid", "<21 days postpartum → avoid estrogen (VTE risk)", "Breast cancer (current) → avoid all hormonal contraception", "When estrogen is out, think progestin-only (POP, DMPA, implant, LNG-IUD)", "CHC missed-pill rules: <24h late → take immediately and continue the pack; ≥24h late in week 1 → back-up contraception x7 days plus consider EC; late in week 2/3 → skip the hormone-free interval instead of taking a break", "Extended/continuous CHC regimens (no or shortened hormone-free interval) reduce dysmenorrhea/heavy flow and help endometriosis/PCOS symptoms — the main downside is unscheduled breakthrough bleeding", "Rifampin and other CYP3A4 inducers (phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, griseofulvin, St. John's Wort) reduce CHC efficacy — use back-up contraception; non-oral CHC (patch/ring) are equally affected by rifampin", "Transdermal patch (Evra) is less effective in patients >90 kg; vaginal ring (NuvaRing) can be removed for up to 3 hours without loss of efficacy", "Breakthrough bleeding pattern is diagnostic: early-cycle (days 1-9) or continuous bleeding = insufficient estrogen; late-cycle (days 10-21) = insufficient progestin"]}, {"heading": "Progestin-Only Options — Quick Facts", "bullets": ["POP ('mini-pill') must be taken same time every day — >3h late = backup needed", "DMPA (Depo-Provera) injection q12wks — delayed return to fertility", "DMPA long-term use linked to reversible BMD loss — caution in adolescents", "LNG-IUD safe in migraine with aura and VTE history — progestin only, minimal systemic absorption", "Progestin-only methods generally safe in breastfeeding and smokers ≥35", "DMPA average weight gain: 2.5 kg in year 1, 3.7 kg by year 2, 6.3 kg by year 4 — counsel proactively", "Progestin-only pill missed-dose threshold is only >3 hours late (much stricter than CHC's 24h rule) — use back-up x48h and consider EC if unprotected intercourse occurred", "Etonogestrel implant (Nexplanon) lasts 3 years and needs 7 days of back-up contraception after insertion unless placed within the first 5 days of the cycle or immediately postpartum", "Progestin-only methods are the preferred choice postpartum regardless of breastfeeding status — lower VTE risk in the first 6 weeks and no effect on milk supply"]}, {"heading": "Pregnancy Drug Safety by Trimester", "bullets": ["1st trimester (weeks 3-8) = organogenesis → highest teratogen risk", "NSAIDs: avoid in 3rd trimester → premature ductus arteriosus closure", "ACE inhibitors/ARBs: contraindicated ALL trimesters → fetal renal damage, oligohydramnios", "Warfarin: teratogenic 1st trimester ('warfarin embryopathy') → switch to LMWH", "Isotretinoin: absolute contraindication → severe birth defects, requires 2 forms contraception", "Valproate: high neural tube defect risk → avoid if possible in pregnancy", "Live vaccines (MMR, varicella): contraindicated in pregnancy", "Folic acid 0.4-1mg/day preconception + 1st trimester prevents neural tube defects", "Methotrexate: contraindicated, teratogenic + abortifacient", "Lithium: small risk of Ebstein's anomaly (cardiac defect) with 1st trimester exposure, dose-dependent (>900 mg/day)", "Tetracyclines: after 17 weeks, cause permanent discoloration of the infant's teeth; doxycycline carries a lower staining risk than other tetracyclines", "Thalidomide: severe limb/ear/cardiac malformations even from a single dose <1 mg/kg — window of risk as early as day 34-50 post-LMP", "Carbamazepine and valproate both raise neural tube defect risk in the 1st trimester; carbamazepine is also linked to cardiovascular malformations", "High-dose retinoids/vitamin A cause craniofacial, cardiac, and CNS malformations plus a high miscarriage rate — isotretinoin is the classic example but the risk applies more broadly"]}, {"heading": "Breastfeeding Drug Safety", "bullets": ["Codeine contraindicated while breastfeeding — CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers → excess morphine → infant deaths reported", "Same CYP2D6 logic applies to tramadol — avoid in nursing mothers", "Preferred analgesics while breastfeeding: acetaminophen, ibuprofen", "Sulfonamides: avoid in newborns with G6PD deficiency or hyperbilirubinemia risk (kernicterus)", "Most antidepressants (sertraline preferred) considered compatible with breastfeeding", "Radioactive iodine and most chemotherapy: contraindicated during breastfeeding", "Exam trap: 'safe in pregnancy' does NOT automatically mean 'safe in breastfeeding' — assess separately", "Ideal breastfeeding-compatible drug profile: short half-life, high protein binding, poor oral bioavailability, low lipophilicity, and established infant safety data", "Take medication immediately after a feed (not before) to minimize infant exposure before the next feed", "IV/IM routes bypass maternal first-pass metabolism and enter milk more readily than oral dosing — factor the route into the risk assessment, not just the drug itself", "Absolutely avoid in breastfeeding: antineoplastics, ergot alkaloids, radioactive iodine-131 (infant hypothyroidism risk); lithium may be used but needs careful infant monitoring"]}, {"heading": "Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) — Risk/Benefit", "bullets": ["Unopposed estrogen in a woman WITH a uterus → endometrial cancer risk — must add progestin", "Estrogen-only HRT is fine if patient has had a hysterectomy", "HRT increases risk: VTE, stroke, breast cancer (combined, long-term use)", "HRT benefits: reduces vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes), prevents osteoporosis", "Lowest effective dose, shortest duration = guiding principle", "Transdermal estrogen carries lower VTE risk than oral estrogen", "Same contraindications as CHC largely apply: current VTE, breast cancer, active liver disease", "HRT duration should generally be limited to 4-5 years given cumulative CV/breast cancer risk with longer use, though ongoing bothersome symptoms can justify individualized continuation beyond age 65", "Estrogen may be cardioprotective if started at menopause onset but harmful if started >10 years after menopause or after age 60 (the 'timing hypothesis')", "Tibolone (a synthetic steroid) treats vasomotor symptoms without requiring added progestogen, but is only appropriate for women with an intact uterus", "Estrogen-SERM combination (estrogen-bazedoxifene) avoids progestin side effects (breast tenderness, bleeding) — an option for women <60 or <10 years postmenopause who want to avoid a progestogen"]}, {"heading": "Emergency Contraception (EC)", "bullets": ["Levonorgestrel (Plan B) — unscheduled/OTC, no age restriction, no ID or prescription needed in Canada", "Most effective within 72h, can be used up to 120h (efficacy declines with time)", "Ulipristal acetate (Ella) — effective up to 120h, more effective than LNG especially days 3-5", "Ulipristal: avoid concurrent progestin use ±5 days (antagonizes each other)", "Copper IUD = most effective EC option, effective up to 7 days post-coitus", "EC efficacy reduced in obesity (BMI >30) — copper IUD preferred if available", "EC does NOT interrupt an established pregnancy — it delays/prevents ovulation", "Neither levonorgestrel nor ulipristal is effective if taken on the day of or after ovulation has already occurred — the copper IUD remains effective regardless of cycle timing", "After LNG-EC, resume/start hormonal contraception immediately (same day) with back-up x7 days; after ulipristal-EC, wait 5 days before starting hormonal contraception (to avoid antagonism), with back-up/abstinence through that window and for 14 days after starting", "A pregnancy test is warranted if no menstrual period occurs within 21 days of taking EC pills or inserting a copper IUD for EC", "Do not discourage EC use based on BMI — still offer LNG or ulipristal even with reduced efficacy if a copper IUD is unavailable or refused"]}, {"heading": "Key Guideline Thresholds to Memorize", "bullets": ["Age 35 + smoking = the classic CHC contraindication combo", "VTE risk with CHC use is highest in first year of use", "Migraine WITH aura = absolute; WITHOUT aura = relative (age/smoking dependent)", "BMI ≥30 → reduced efficacy of LNG EC and some contraceptive patches", "CHC absolute contraindication combo to memorize: smoker ≥15 cigarettes/day AND age ≥35", "Wait at least 3 months before concluding a contraceptive isn't working or switching due to side effects like breakthrough bleeding — most resolve by 3-6 months", "Postpartum COC timing: delay until breastfeeding is established (~6 weeks) if breastfeeding; can start at 3-4 weeks postpartum if not breastfeeding"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam Traps", "bullets": ["Trap: assuming all hormonal contraceptives are contraindicated in migraine — only estrogen-containing ones are (POP/LNG-IUD OK)", "Trap: forgetting codeine's CYP2D6 breastfeeding danger — a favorite NAPLEX/PEBC scenario", "Trap: confusing EC scheduling — LNG is Unscheduled OTC, no prescription; don't say 'behind pharmacist counsel only' as a barrier", "Trap: thinking ACEi/ARB are only risky in 1st trimester — they are contraindicated throughout pregnancy", "Trap: assuming 'natural/herbal' products are automatically pregnancy-safe (e.g., some herbals are abortifacient/teratogenic)", "Trap: assuming levonorgestrel and ulipristal work by disrupting an implanted pregnancy — both act pre-ovulation/pre-fertilization, not as abortifacients", "Trap: applying COC-style forgiving missed-dose logic to POPs — POPs need same-time-daily dosing with a strict >3h cutoff, unlike the more forgiving 24h COC rule", "Trap: thinking mifepristone/misoprostol medical abortion requires prophylactic antibiotics — they are NOT routinely recommended given low infection rates", "Trap: forgetting that fertility can return within about 8 days after a completed medical abortion — contraception counselling must happen immediately, not 'later'"]}, {"heading": "Medical Abortion (Mifepristone/Misoprostol)", "bullets": ["Health Canada-approved combination regimen for pregnancies ≤63 days gestation: mifepristone 200 mg PO on day 1, then misoprostol 800 mcg (4×200 mcg) buccal/vaginal/SL on day 2 or 3", "Contraindications: ectopic pregnancy, IUD in situ, bleeding disorder, anemia (Hb <95 g/L), chronic systemic corticosteroids, chronic adrenal failure, uncontrolled asthma, or ambivalence about the decision", "Expect cramping/bleeding starting 2-4 hours after misoprostol; manage with NSAIDs first-line and opioids second-line for pain, antiemetics/loperamide for prostaglandin GI effects", "Seek emergency care for: soaking ≥2 pads/hour for 2 hours, 1 pad/hour for 10 hours, dizziness/tachycardia, pain unrelieved by analgesics, or foul-smelling discharge", "Prophylactic antibiotics are NOT routinely recommended — infection rates are low; treat a mild infection with broad-spectrum antibiotics if one occurs", "Follow-up at 7 days: beta-hCG should have dropped ≥80% by day 7 (≥50% by day 3) to confirm success; ultrasound is confirmatory if the picture is unclear", "Fertility can return as early as 8 days post-abortion — hormonal contraception can start the same day as misoprostol; IUD/IUS placement must wait until the abortion is confirmed complete", "Mifepristone is metabolized by, and also inhibits, CYP3A4 — CYP3A4 inducers can cause treatment failure and inhibitors can increase ADRs"]}, {"heading": "Perimenopause, Natural Family Planning & Menopause Basics", "bullets": ["Menopause = 12 consecutive months without menses (average age 51); perimenopause (the menopausal transition) averages ~4 years and features irregular cycles from declining follicle number", "Vasomotor symptoms last 7-8 years on average, but genitourinary/vaginal atrophy symptoms do NOT improve over time without treatment — counsel on each differently", "Fertility awareness-based methods (calendar/rhythm, basal body temperature, cervical mucus, symptothermal) have wide efficacy ranges and are much less reliable than LARC methods — best combined with a barrier method", "Lactational amenorrhea method is ~98% effective ONLY if: <6 months postpartum, exclusively/near-exclusively breastfeeding (>75% of feeds), and still amenorrheic — losing any one criterion means back-up contraception is needed", "Vaginal moisturizers (used twice weekly) provide longer-term relief of dryness/dyspareunia vs. lubricants (used PRN) for rapid short-term relief — only water-based lubricants are condom-safe", "Intravaginal (local) estrogen for genitourinary symptoms does not require added progestogen if used ≤1 year, unlike systemic estrogen therapy"]}]}, {"topic": "regs", "title": "Regulations & Law — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "NAPRA Drug Schedules", "bullets": ["Schedule I: Prescription required, behind counter, pharmacist involvement mandatory (e.g., antibiotics, statins, antihypertensives)", "Schedule II: No prescription needed BUT must be purchased from pharmacist area (behind/near counter), pharmacist must be accessible (e.g., pseudoephedrine, some emergency contraception products, insulin syringes in some provinces)", "Schedule III: Self-selection allowed in pharmacy, but must be in area with pharmacist available for consultation (e.g., low-dose ibuprofen, hydrocortisone cream, loperamide)", "Unscheduled: Available outside pharmacy, no pharmacist required (e.g., most vitamins, plain acetaminophen in small pack sizes, basic cosmetics)", "Exam trap: Schedule II ≠ needs Rx — it means pharmacist must be involved/available, not that a prescription is required", "NAPRA schedules are recommended by the National Drug Scheduling Advisory Committee (NDSAC), which weighs risk to the public and need for pharmacist intervention — provinces may adopt, modify, or (like Quebec) diverge from these recommendations", "Schedule II drugs are NOT accessible to the public for self-selection (kept behind/near the counter) — contrast with Schedule III, which IS accessible but must be within a defined distance (e.g., 10m) of the dispensary", "Drug samples: Schedule I samples require a prescription and a professional fee (not a drug cost) may be charged; Schedule II narcotic samples cannot be provided at all", "The Prescription Drug List (PDL) under the Food and Drugs Act determines Rx-only status federally and does NOT include CDSA-controlled substances (narcotics, controlled drugs) — those are governed separately"]}, {"heading": "CDSA (Controlled Drugs and Substances Act) — Verbal Orders", "bullets": ["Verbal orders for narcotics/controlled drugs ARE permitted from a prescriber, but must be documented and often countersigned/confirmed", "Verbal orders NOT permitted for methadone in most provincial frameworks — written Rx required", "Refills on CDSA prescriptions: narcotics/controlled substances generally CANNOT be refilled without a new prescription (no 'refill authorized' like regular meds) — check provincial variance", "Benzodiazepines (targeted substances) — some refill flexibility exists provincially but stricter than regular Rx", "Transfers of controlled substance prescriptions between pharmacies: generally NOT permitted (unlike regular prescriptions) — provincial exceptions exist (e.g., one-time transfer in some provinces)", "Effective October 1, 2026, new Controlled Substances Regulations (CSR) replace the Narcotic Control Regulations, BOTSR, and related regulations — narcotics, controlled drugs, targeted substances, and restricted drugs are consolidated into CSR Schedules 1-4", "Under the CSR, verbal, written, or faxed prescriptions ARE accepted for ALL controlled substance schedules (narcotics, controlled drugs, targeted substances, restricted drugs)", "Under the CSR, refills (including verbal refills) ARE permitted for controlled drugs, targeted substances, and restricted drugs — always confirm current provincial rules as this is an evolving area", "Part-fill prescriptions for controlled substances must specify the total authorized quantity plus the quantity/interval to be dispensed at each fill — the dispensed amount must be less than the total authorized", "Low-dose codeine combination products (≤8mg/unit solid or ≤20mg/30mL liquid, with ≥2 non-narcotic ingredients) may be sold by a pharmacist without a prescription"]}, {"heading": "CDSA — Loss, Theft & Reporting", "bullets": ["Loss or theft of controlled substances/narcotics MUST be reported to Health Canada (Office of Controlled Substances)", "Also report to local police AND provincial regulatory authority (College)", "Report required even for suspected loss/discrepancy, not just confirmed theft", "Inventory/perpetual records for narcotics must be maintained and reconciled regularly", "Destruction of controlled substances requires witnessed process and documentation per Health Canada requirements", "Destruction of unserviceable stock requires 2 witnesses (one can be the person performing the destruction); post-consumer returns have simpler documentation requirements (date, method, container ID/count only)", "Health Canada approval/notification is NOT required before or after destroying controlled substances (as of July 2016) — records must simply be retained for a minimum of 2 years", "Never dispose of controlled substances down the drain — the substance must be rendered unusable, typically via a change of state (e.g., solid to liquid) or through a licensed destruction contractor"]}, {"heading": "Privacy Law — Consent Exceptions", "bullets": ["General rule: patient consent required before disclosing personal health information", "Exception: disclosure required by law (e.g., reporting to public health, court order, coroner)", "Exception: imminent risk of serious harm to patient or identifiable third party (duty to warn)", "Exception: care/treatment purposes — sharing with other treating healthcare providers (circle of care) generally implied consent", "Exception: CDSA loss/theft reporting overrides normal privacy consent requirement", "Substitute decision-maker consent applies when patient lacks capacity (e.g., unconscious, dementia)", "Exam trap: 'circle of care' allows sharing WITHOUT explicit consent among direct treatment team, but NOT to family/employers without consent", "PIPEDA (federal, private sector) applies to pharmacies; the Privacy Act applies only to federal government institutions — a provincial law can supersede PIPEDA if declared 'substantially similar' by the federal Privacy Commissioner", "PIPEDA's 10 fair-information principles include: accountability, identifying purpose, consent, limiting collection/use/disclosure/retention, accuracy, safeguards, openness, individual access, and challenging compliance", "Disclosure is generally REQUIRED (overriding normal consent) for suspected child/elder abuse and gunshot wounds — distinct from disclosures merely PERMITTED (e.g., court order, harm-to-self-or-others, SDM consent)", "PEBC exams are federal exams — provincial-specific privacy law nuances are not tested, only the general federal PIPEDA framework and concepts"]}, {"heading": "Pharmacist Prescribing — Scope Limits", "bullets": ["Pharmacists can NEVER independently prescribe controlled substances (narcotics, CDSA Schedule I drugs) — hard limit across all provinces", "Pharmacist prescribing authority varies by province: minor ailments, renewals/adaptations, smoking cessation, travel health are common scopes", "Prescribing for minor ailments excludes conditions with red flags (e.g., persistent symptoms, red-flag features) — must refer", "Adapting a prescription (dose/formulation change) ≠ initiating new independent prescribing — different authority level", "Exam trap: assuming expanded scope = can prescribe anything; controlled substances remain off-limits regardless of provincial scope expansion", "Pharmacists (and pharmacist interns, depending on jurisdiction) may prescribe varenicline/bupropion for smoking cessation and drugs for listed minor ailments — pharmacy technicians cannot prescribe", "Pharmacists may prescribe oseltamivir (influenza) and nirmatrelvir/ritonavir (COVID-19) — this authority does not always extend to pharmacist interns in the same framework", "Adapting/renewing a prescription (except for monitored/controlled drugs in some jurisdictions) is a distinct, narrower authority than independent prescribing — pharmacist interns can generally also adapt/renew under supervision", "Point-of-care testing (e.g., capillary blood glucose, INR) via lancet devices can be performed by pharmacists and technicians for patient education, but only a pharmacist may interpret the result", "Administering a vaccine, an injectable Schedule 1 substance, or an inhaled Schedule 2 substance requires approved training plus valid first aid/CPR certification"]}, {"heading": "Natural Health Products (NHP) Regulation", "bullets": ["NPN (Natural Product Number) = licensed natural health product (vitamins, herbals, homeopathics) under NHP Regulations", "DIN (Drug Identification Number) = licensed conventional/pharmaceutical drug under Food and Drugs Act", "DIN-HM = homeopathic medicine with a DIN (older classification, being phased toward NPN)", "NHPs undergo lighter evidence review than DIN drugs — efficacy standards are lower", "Exam trap: NPN products can still interact with drugs (e.g., St. John's Wort induces CYP3A4) — 'natural' ≠ 'no interactions'", "NHPs are regulated by the Natural and Non-prescription Health Products Directorate (NNHPD) — a separate directorate from the one regulating conventional drugs, though both fall under Health Canada", "NHP categories include vitamins, minerals, traditional medicines (e.g., Chinese medicine), homeopathic remedies, herbal remedies, probiotics, amino acids, and essential fatty acids", "An NHP does not require a prescription for sale by definition — once a product needs a prescription, it is regulated as a conventional drug (DIN), not as an NHP", "DIN-HM (homeopathic medicines with a DIN) sit at the boundary between the NHP and conventional drug frameworks — an older classification that NHP regulations are moving away from"]}, {"heading": "Error Disclosure Duty", "bullets": ["Pharmacists have an ethical AND legal (in most provinces) duty to disclose medication errors to the patient", "Disclosure required even if the error caused no apparent harm", "Document the error via incident report / continuous quality improvement (CQI) process — separate from patient chart", "Apology and disclosure ≠ admission of legal liability in many provinces (apology legislation protects this)", "Failure to disclose is itself a professional misconduct/discipline risk, independent of the original error", "Nonmaleficence has three duties relevant to errors: avoid causing harm, prevent harm, and remove/mitigate harm once it has occurred — disclosure is part of the 'remove harm' duty", "Forgetting to counsel on an important side-effect-avoidance step (e.g., rinsing after inhaled corticosteroid use) that leads to a preventable complication (e.g., oral thrush) is itself an error of omission, not just a dispensing mistake", "Error-prevention systems like TALLman lettering exist to uphold nonmaleficence proactively (preventing harm) rather than just reacting after an error occurs"]}, {"heading": "MAID (Medical Assistance in Dying) — Pharmacist Role", "bullets": ["Pharmacists may dispense MAID medications but are NOT required to personally administer them", "Conscientious objection is permitted — but objecting pharmacist must ensure timely referral/transfer of care, cannot abandon patient", "Pharmacist verifies eligibility documentation and prescription completeness before dispensing", "Extra vigilance on storage, transport, and unused medication return/destruction given lethality of MAID drugs", "MAID prescriptions require strict documentation per federal/provincial MAID legislation", "MAID eligibility: age ≥18 with decision-making capacity, eligible for public healthcare, voluntary request without external influence, informed consent at the time of request AND immediately before administration, a serious/incurable illness or disability with irreversible decline (need not be terminal), and intolerable suffering that cannot be relieved acceptably", "Two independent assessors (physicians or nurse practitioners, unknown to each other/the patient, no potential personal benefit) must confirm eligibility; if natural death is not reasonably foreseeable, at least one assessor must have expertise in the patient's condition and the process takes about 3 months", "Patients whose sole underlying condition is mental illness remain temporarily excluded from MAID eligibility (exclusion currently extended to March 17, 2027)", "A typical MAID medication kit contains midazolam (anxiolytic), lidocaine (local anesthetic), propofol (anesthetic/coma-inducing agent), and rocuronium (neuromuscular blocker to stop respiration), plus a backup kit in case of complications", "The written MAID request must be signed and dated by the patient AND an independent witness, and can be withdrawn by the patient at any time, in any manner"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam Traps", "bullets": ["Trap: thinking Schedule II means 'needs a prescription' — it means pharmacist-controlled access, not physician Rx", "Trap: assuming controlled substance prescriptions can be transferred like regular meds — generally prohibited", "Trap: believing pharmacist prescribing authority ever extends to narcotics/CDSA drugs — it never does", "Trap: confusing NPN and DIN — NPN is NOT proof of the same efficacy/safety standard as DIN", "Trap: thinking conscientious objection to MAID means no obligation at all — duty to refer/transfer still applies", "Trap: thinking capacity and legal competence are the same thing — capacity is an ethical/clinical, often-fluctuating, decision-specific assessment made by a treating clinician; competence is a legal, all-or-nothing determination made by a judge", "Trap: assuming a family member can automatically consent for an incapable patient — a hierarchy of substitute decision-makers (spouse, then child, then sibling, then closest relative; varies by province) applies only if there is no power of attorney for health", "Trap: believing informed consent is satisfied just by mentioning one treatment option — true informed consent requires disclosing reasonable ALTERNATIVES, not just the provider's preferred choice", "Trap: under the new CSR (Oct 2026), assuming controlled substance prescriptions still cannot be transferred/refilled — the new regulations explicitly permit verbal orders, refills, and transfers within 2 years for narcotics, controlled drugs, and targeted substances, a major change from older rules"]}, {"heading": "Capacity, Consent & Substitute Decision-Making", "bullets": ["Six core bioethical principles: beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, autonomy, veracity (honesty), and fidelity (loyalty/confidentiality) — no single principle automatically overrides the others; judgment is case-specific", "Capacity is not the same as competence: capacity is assessed immediately by treating healthcare professionals and can fluctuate by decision/task; competence is a permanent legal status determined by a court", "Red flags that should trigger a capacity assessment: significant mental illness/dementia/delirium, inability to understand information, extremes of age, polypharmacy, choices inconsistent with prior values, refusing a low-risk/high-benefit treatment, severe depression", "If a patient lacks capacity, follow this order: living will/advance directive first (most autonomy-preserving) → substituted judgment by a surrogate (what the patient WOULD have chosen) → best-interests standard (what is objectively good for the patient) as a last resort", "Substitute decision-maker hierarchy (varies by province) when there is no power of attorney for health: spouse, then child, then sibling, then closest relative", "Informed consent requires disclosure of the nature of the decision, reasonable alternatives, relevant risks/benefits/uncertainties of each option, assessment of patient understanding, and voluntary acceptance", "Paternalism (overriding a capable patient's autonomous choice 'for their own good') is not an ethical principle — it is a violation of autonomy, even when beneficence/nonmaleficence are used to justify it"]}, {"heading": "Scope of Practice — Pharmacists, Interns & Technicians", "bullets": ["Pharmacy technicians can dispense, compound, and handle the technical aspects of prescription preparation/verification, but cannot provide clinical/therapeutic information or counsel on therapy — that remains a pharmacist/intern function", "Accepting verbal prescriptions for controlled drugs, narcotics, and targeted substances is authorized for pharmacists and pharmacist interns, not technicians", "Both pharmacists and pharmacy technicians may destroy controlled substance stock (with proper witnessing), and both may administer certain injections/inhalations with appropriate training and certification", "Prescribing for minor ailments and smoking cessation (varenicline/bupropion) extends to pharmacists and pharmacist interns; prescribing oseltamivir/nirmatrelvir-ritonavir is typically pharmacist-only and not extended to interns in the same way", "A point-of-care test (e.g., fingerstick glucose) can be performed and demonstrated by technicians, but only a pharmacist may interpret the clinical result", "Students in pharmacy/pharmacy technician programs may perform authorized acts within their future role's scope, but only under direct supervision per their program's supervision policy"]}]}, {"topic": "pk", "title": "Pharmacokinetics — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Half-Life & Steady State", "bullets": ["Steady state reached after ~4-5 half-lives, regardless of dose or infusion rate", "1 t1/2 = 50%, 2 = 75%, 3 = 87.5%, 4 = 93.75%, 5 = ~97% of steady state", "Time to steady state depends ONLY on half-life — not dose, not frequency", "Changing the dose changes the new steady-state concentration, not the time to reach it", "Half-life: t1/2 = 0.693 × Vd / Cl — reflects both distribution and elimination, not clearance alone", "Large clearance + small Vd = rapid elimination and short half-life; small clearance + large Vd = slow elimination and long half-life", "After an IV bolus, an early 'distribution phase' (drug moving from plasma to tissue) can be mistaken for elimination on a concentration-time curve — sampling too early overestimates the apparent elimination rate", "For IV infusions, steady-state concentration is independent of Vd — it depends only on infusion rate and clearance (Css = R0/Cl)"]}, {"heading": "Loading Dose & Vd", "bullets": ["Loading Dose = Target Cp x Vd", "Used when time to steady state is too slow clinically (e.g., digoxin, phenytoin loading)", "Vd = Dose / Cp — apparent volume relating amount in body to plasma concentration", "Large Vd (e.g., digoxin ~500L) = extensive tissue distribution; small Vd = stays mostly in plasma (e.g., warfarin)", "Loading dose is NOT adjusted for renal/hepatic impairment — it's based on Vd, not clearance", "Vd for classic drugs: digoxin ~500 L, aminoglycosides ~20 L, warfarin ~8 L, phenytoin ~45 L, propranolol ~420 L — larger Vd roughly tracks lipophilicity and tissue binding", "High-Vd drugs tend to be small, lipophilic, and tissue-protein-bound; low-Vd drugs tend to be larger, hydrophilic, and plasma-protein-bound with poor membrane permeability", "Activated charcoal given after an overdose can lower the apparent Vd of a drug still in the gut by trapping it before absorption, not by pulling it out of tissues", "Loading dose calculations assume instantaneous distribution — in practice a loading dose is often infused slowly to avoid a transient toxic peak (e.g., phenytoin infused no faster than the institutional maximum rate)"]}, {"heading": "Clearance, Maintenance Dose, First-Pass", "bullets": ["Maintenance Dose = Css x Clearance x Dosing interval (rate in = rate out at steady state)", "Clearance is what's adjusted for renal/hepatic impairment — maintenance dose changes, loading dose doesn't", "First-pass metabolism: oral drugs absorbed via the portal vein pass through the liver before reaching systemic circulation, lowering bioavailability (F)", "High first-pass drugs have low oral bioavailability (e.g., propranolol, morphine) — may need much higher oral than IV doses", "Hepatic clearance = hepatic blood flow × extraction ratio; for high-extraction-ratio drugs (E>0.7, e.g., morphine, lidocaine, verapamil, nitroglycerin) clearance depends mainly on liver blood flow, not enzyme activity", "Low-extraction-ratio drugs (E<0.3, e.g., warfarin, phenytoin) have clearance that depends on intrinsic enzyme activity and free fraction, making them more prone to clinically significant enzyme inhibitor/inducer interactions", "Renal clearance = (filtration + secretion − reabsorption); glomerular filtration alone underestimates clearance for drugs that are also actively secreted (e.g., penicillins)", "Total body clearance is the sum of all organ clearances (mainly hepatic + renal) and is additive even when only one organ's function changes"]}, {"heading": "Protein Binding", "bullets": ["Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active and available for renal filtration/metabolism", "Highly protein-bound drugs (warfarin, phenytoin, valproic acid) — displacement or hypoalbuminemia raises free fraction/effect without necessarily changing total level", "Total levels can mislead in hypoalbuminemia — correct or interpret cautiously (e.g., Sheiner-Tozer for phenytoin)", "Acidic drugs bind albumin; basic drugs bind alpha-1-acid glycoprotein", "Sheiner-Tozer equation corrects a measured total phenytoin level for hypoalbuminemia: corrected level = measured level ÷ (0.2 × albumin + 0.1)", "Use the corrected phenytoin level whenever albumin is low or renal function is impaired — the raw total level will underestimate true drug effect", "Conditions that lower albumin and raise free phenytoin fraction: burns, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, pregnancy, cystic fibrosis; uremia and hyperbilirubinemia can also displace phenytoin from binding sites", "In healthy adults roughly 90% of phenytoin is protein-bound, leaving only ~10% free — a small drop in binding capacity can meaningfully raise the pharmacologically active free fraction"]}, {"heading": "CYP450 Phenotype Concepts", "bullets": ["CYP2D6: activates codeine/tramadol/tamoxifen — ultrarapid metabolizers risk opioid toxicity, poor metabolizers get no benefit", "CYP2C19: activates clopidogrel (poor metabolizers = reduced antiplatelet effect); also metabolizes PPIs", "CYP2C9: metabolizes warfarin, phenytoin — poor metabolizers need lower doses, higher toxicity/bleeding risk", "Phenotypes: poor / intermediate / extensive (normal) / ultrarapid metabolizer", "Prodrugs (codeine, clopidogrel, tramadol) need enzyme activity for EFFECT; most drugs need it for CLEARANCE"]}, {"heading": "Zero-Order vs First-Order Kinetics", "bullets": ["First-order (most drugs): constant FRACTION eliminated per unit time; half-life stays constant", "Zero-order (phenytoin at high levels, alcohol, high-dose ASA): constant AMOUNT eliminated per unit time (enzyme saturation)", "In zero-order kinetics, small dose changes can cause disproportionately large concentration changes", "'Half-life' isn't a fixed, meaningful concept once kinetics become zero-order"]}, {"heading": "TDM Target Ranges to Memorize", "bullets": ["Digoxin: 0.5-2.0 ng/mL (HF target lower end ~0.5-0.9)", "Lithium: 0.6-1.2 mmol/L (maintenance), trough drawn 12h post-dose", "Phenytoin: 10-20 mg/L total (free 1-2 mg/L)", "Vancomycin: AUC24/MIC ≥400 (modern target); trough 15-20 mg/L if used", "Theophylline: 10-20 mcg/mL (some target 5-15)", "Carbamazepine 4-12 mg/L; Valproic acid 50-100 mg/L", "Aminoglycosides (gentamicin/tobramycin/netilmicin) conventional dosing targets peak 5-10 mg/L and trough ≤2.5 mg/L; amikacin targets peak 15-20 mg/L and trough ≤5 mg/L", "Extended-interval (once-daily) aminoglycoside dosing uses a nomogram (e.g., Hartford) with a single random level rather than routine peak/trough sampling", "Vancomycin monitoring is shifting from trough-only (10-20 mg/L, or 15-20 mg/L for severe/deep-seated infection) toward AUC24/MIC of 400-600 mg·h/L, which better predicts efficacy and nephrotoxicity risk", "Cyclosporine trough (C0) targets are highest in the first 1-3 months post-transplant (200-300 ng/mL) and lower for maintenance (50-150 ng/mL); two-hour post-dose (C2) levels are used when absorption is in question", "Digoxin levels should be drawn ≥6-8h (ideally ~12h) after a dose to avoid the distribution phase, and only after 7-14 days of a stable dose given its ~48h half-life"]}, {"heading": "Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Loading dose → based on Vd; Maintenance dose → based on clearance — don't mix these up", "Time to steady state depends only on half-life — a classic trap answer says dose changes it", "Trough levels reflect toxicity/accumulation; peak levels reflect efficacy (esp. aminoglycosides)", "Draw digoxin levels ≥6-8h post-dose to avoid the distribution phase (falsely high early level)", "Hepatic impairment mainly affects clearance/first-pass; renal impairment mainly affects clearance of renally-eliminated drugs/active metabolites", "Phenytoin sodium 100 mg is NOT equivalent to phenytoin acid 100 mg — phenytoin acid contains about 8% more active drug, so doses must be adjusted when switching formulations", "Non-linear (Michaelis-Menten) kinetics mean clearance falls as concentration rises — a proportional dose increase can produce a disproportionately large jump in plasma concentration (classic with phenytoin)", "One-compartment models fit drugs with rapid, uniform distribution (e.g., aminoglycosides, theophylline); two- and three-compartment models are needed for drugs like digoxin, vancomycin, amiodarone, and fentanyl that show a distinct distribution phase on a semi-log plot", "'Flip-flop' kinetics occur when a drug's absorption is slower than its elimination (common with extended-release and depot formulations) — the terminal decline in the concentration-time curve then reflects the absorption rate, not the true elimination rate", "For drugs eliminated purely by first-order kinetics, doubling the maintenance dose doubles the steady-state concentration; this proportionality breaks down once elimination becomes saturated (zero-order/non-linear)"]}, {"heading": "Compartment Models", "bullets": ["One-compartment model treats the whole body as a single well-stirred space with instantaneous distribution — fits drugs like aminoglycosides, theophylline, and acetylsalicylic acid", "Two-compartment model adds a peripheral compartment for slower-equilibrating tissue — fits digoxin and vancomycin, producing a visible distribution phase before the elimination phase on a semi-log Cp-vs-time plot", "Three-compartment model adds a deep, poorly-perfused tissue compartment (e.g., fat) — fits amiodarone and fentanyl, both of which have very long terminal half-lives due to slow redistribution out of deep tissue", "The number of distinct linear slopes visible on a semi-log concentration-time plot tells you how many compartments are needed to describe a drug's kinetics", "Michaelis-Menten kinetics (rate = Vmax × C / (Km + C)) describe saturable metabolism: when concentration is much lower than Km the process looks first-order, and when concentration is much higher than Km the process becomes zero-order (enzyme-saturated)"]}, {"heading": "Antibiotic PK/PD & Extended-Interval Dosing", "bullets": ["Concentration-dependent killers (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones) are dosed to maximize peak concentration or AUC/MIC — once-daily extended-interval dosing exploits this plus a post-antibiotic effect", "Time-dependent killers (beta-lactams, vancomycin) are dosed to maximize the time concentration stays above the MIC — more frequent dosing or extended/continuous infusions improve efficacy more than a higher single dose", "Aminoglycosides are nearly 99% renally cleared with a healthy-kidney half-life of ~2h that can stretch to 30-60h in renal failure — extending the dosing interval (not just lowering the dose) is the primary adjustment", "Vancomycin loading doses of 15-20 mg/kg are typical, dosed every 8-12h initially then adjusted by renal function; poor penetration into lung, CNS, and bone limits its usefulness for infections at those sites", "Post-antibiotic effect (continued bacterial suppression despite levels falling below MIC) is a key rationale for extended-interval aminoglycoside dosing and is prominent with aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones"]}]}, {"topic": "inter", "title": "Drug Interactions — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "CYP450 Inhibitors to Memorize", "bullets": ["Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors: azole antifungals (ketoconazole, itraconazole, fluconazole), macrolides (clarithromycin, erythromycin — not azithromycin), protease inhibitors, grapefruit juice", "Broad/multi-CYP inhibitors: cimetidine, amiodarone", "CYP2C9 inhibitors: fluconazole, amiodarone, metronidazole — raise warfarin/phenytoin levels", "CYP2D6 inhibitors: fluoxetine, paroxetine, bupropion — reduce codeine/tamoxifen/tramadol activation", "Inhibition onset is typically fast (days) — faster than induction", "Additional inhibitors worth knowing: cimetidine (broad, including 2D6/2C19), esomeprazole and omeprazole (2C9/2C19), gemfibrozil (1A2, 2C8, 2C9, 2C19 — raises statin levels and rhabdomyolysis risk), fluvoxamine (1A2, 2C19)", "Antidepressants with the LEAST CYP interaction potential: citalopram, escitalopram, venlafaxine — useful choices when polypharmacy is a concern", "Fluoxetine and paroxetine (2D6 inhibitors) and fluvoxamine (1A2/2C19 inhibitor) are the antidepressants most likely to cause clinically significant interactions", "Acid-reducing agents with minimal interaction risk: famotidine, ranitidine-class H2RAs, lansoprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole — preferred over omeprazole/esomeprazole/cimetidine when interaction risk is a concern"]}, {"heading": "CYP450 Inducers to Memorize", "bullets": ["Classic inducers: Rifampin, Carbamazepine, Phenytoin, Phenobarbital, St. John's Wort (chronic alcohol/smoking also induce select pathways)", "Inducers take 1-2 weeks to develop full effect and 1-2 weeks to resolve after stopping", "Effect: reduce levels/efficacy of CYP substrates (OCs, warfarin, cyclosporine, tacrolimus)", "Classic exam trap: starting an inducer can cause treatment failure or breakthrough bleeding weeks into therapy, not immediately", "Mnemonic-friendly inducer list: rifampin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, St. John's Wort — plus efavirenz, modafinil, and bosentan as less commonly tested examples", "Antiepileptics with LOW interaction potential: gabapentin, lamotrigine, levetiracetam — contrast with the classic inducers carbamazepine, phenytoin, and phenobarbital", "Rifampin induces its own metabolism (autoinduction) in addition to inducing other CYP substrates, contributing to its potent enzyme-inducing effect"]}, {"heading": "Serotonin Syndrome Combinations", "bullets": ["Triggers: MAOIs + SSRIs/SNRIs/tramadol/meperidine/triptans/linezolid/St. John's Wort", "Triad: mental status change (agitation), autonomic instability (tachycardia, hyperthermia, diaphoresis), neuromuscular signs (tremor, clonus, hyperreflexia)", "Meperidine + MAOI is a classic, potentially fatal, absolute contraindication", "Washout periods required around MAOIs (e.g., 2 weeks off MAOI before an SSRI; 5 weeks off fluoxetine before an MAOI)", "Onset of serotonin syndrome is typically within hours of adding or increasing the dose of a serotonergic drug — a much faster timeline than enzyme-induction interactions", "Management is largely supportive: discontinue the offending agent(s) and treat symptoms; severe cases may need cyproheptadine or benzodiazepines", "Linezolid is a weak, reversible MAOI — it carries the same serotonin syndrome risk as classic MAOIs when combined with serotonergic antidepressants"]}, {"heading": "QT-Prolonging Combinations", "bullets": ["Classic QT drugs: amiodarone, macrolides, fluoroquinolones, antipsychotics (haloperidol, quetiapine), ondansetron, methadone", "Combining 2+ QT-prolonging drugs has additive/synergistic torsades de pointes risk", "Hypokalemia/hypomagnesemia (e.g., from diuretics) potentiate QT-related arrhythmia risk", "Consider baseline/ongoing ECG when stacking QT-prolonging drugs in higher-risk patients", "General ECG thresholds: exercise caution at QTc ≥450 msec, and avoid adding further QT-prolonging drugs at QTc ≥500 msec or an increase of ≥60 msec from baseline", "Methadone is a notable QT-prolonging opioid due to its long half-life and CYP3A4/2D6 interactions — an easily overlooked contributor when stacking QT drugs", "Correcting hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia (e.g., before/during diuretic therapy) is a key preventive step whenever multiple QT-prolonging drugs are unavoidable"]}, {"heading": "Bleeding-Risk Combinations", "bullets": ["Warfarin/DOACs + NSAIDs/ASA/clopidogrel = additive pharmacodynamic bleeding risk (not a PK interaction)", "Warfarin + CYP2C9 inhibitors (fluconazole, amiodarone, metronidazole, TMP-SMX) = pharmacokinetic rise in INR", "SSRIs alone raise bleeding risk (impaired platelet serotonin uptake) — additive with anticoagulants/NSAIDs", "Broad-spectrum antibiotics can raise INR by reducing vitamin K-producing gut flora", "Lithium levels can rise 25-50% when combined with NSAIDs, thiazide/loop diuretics, or ACEI/ARBs due to reduced renal lithium clearance — monitor lithium level roughly 3-5 days after starting the interacting drug", "Azathioprine or 6-mercaptopurine combined with allopurinol requires reducing the thiopurine dose to about 25% of usual, because allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase-mediated thiopurine clearance and risks severe myelosuppression", "Occasional single ibuprofen doses taken ≥2 hours after low-dose ASA do not meaningfully blunt ASA's antiplatelet effect — but regular/scheduled NSAID use does, via competition for platelet COX-1"]}, {"heading": "'Triple Whammy' & Renal-Risk Combos", "bullets": ["Triple whammy = ACEI/ARB + diuretic + NSAID → acute kidney injury risk (reduced renal perfusion/autoregulation)", "Each drug reduces renal blood flow/filtration by a different mechanism — combined effect is synergistic", "Counsel patients on ACEI/ARB + diuretic to avoid OTC NSAIDs", "Combining nephrotoxic drugs (aminoglycosides, vancomycin, contrast dye, NSAIDs) compounds AKI risk", "Combining multiple nephrotoxic or renally-active drugs (e.g., NSAID + ACEI/ARB + diuretic, or aminoglycoside + vancomycin + contrast) compounds acute kidney injury risk beyond any single agent", "Even short courses of NSAIDs (a few days) can precipitate AKI in dehydrated, elderly, or renally-impaired patients on background ACEI/ARB + diuretic therapy", "Cyclosporine and tacrolimus are hyperkalemia-risk drugs that add to the hyperkalemia potential of ACEI/ARB, spironolactone, and potassium supplements"]}, {"heading": "Absorption / Chelation Interactions", "bullets": ["Divalent/trivalent cations (Ca, Mg, Al, Fe, Zn) chelate fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines — separate by 2h before/4-6h after", "Antacids/dairy/mineral supplements reduce absorption of affected antibiotics and levothyroxine", "Levothyroxine also affected by calcium, iron, and PPIs — take on an empty stomach, separated from interacting products", "Bile acid resins (cholestyramine) can bind and reduce absorption of many co-administered drugs — separate dosing", "Dolutegravir and bisphosphonates are also chelated by polyvalent cations — separate from calcium/iron/magnesium/aluminum-containing products", "PPIs and H2RAs reduce absorption of drugs requiring an acidic environment to dissolve, such as itraconazole capsules and certain iron salts", "Cholestyramine and activated charcoal are non-specific binders — they can reduce absorption of nearly any co-administered oral drug, so timing separation matters broadly, not just for one specific interacting pair"]}, {"heading": "P-glycoprotein (P-gp) Interactions", "bullets": ["P-gp is an efflux transporter (gut, kidney, BBB, liver) affecting absorption/elimination independent of CYP metabolism", "Digoxin is the classic P-gp substrate with a narrow TI — inhibitors (amiodarone, verapamil, clarithromycin, quinidine) raise levels", "P-gp inducers (rifampin, St. John's Wort) lower digoxin levels", "Cyclosporine/tacrolimus are both CYP3A4 AND P-gp substrates — overlapping interaction risk with azoles/macrolides", "P-gp is expressed at the gut wall, renal tubule, blood-brain barrier, and biliary canaliculus — inhibiting it generally raises drug levels because it normally pumps drug OUT of cells/back into the gut lumen", "Loperamide is a P-gp substrate at the blood-brain barrier — this is why it does not cause CNS opioid effects at normal doses despite being an opioid-receptor agonist; P-gp inhibitors could theoretically allow CNS penetration", "Dabigatran is a P-gp substrate with no CYP metabolism — its levels are especially sensitive to P-gp inhibitors/inducers since that is essentially its only interaction pathway"]}, {"heading": "Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Not all interactions are enzyme-based — pharmacodynamic (additive-effect) interactions matter just as much", "Azithromycin is the 'safe' macrolide for CYP3A4 interactions — a common distractor vs. clarithromycin/erythromycin", "A single dose of an inhibitor (e.g., one dose of fluconazole) can still meaningfully interact due to long half-lives", "Grapefruit juice affects intestinal, not hepatic, CYP3A4 — explains why IV drugs are unaffected", "Herbal products (St. John's Wort) often go undisclosed by patients — always ask specifically"]}, {"heading": "OATP Transporter Interactions", "bullets": ["OATPs (organic anion-transporting polypeptides) are influx pumps that move drugs INTO hepatocytes and other cells — the opposite direction of P-gp", "Inhibiting OATP at the liver raises serum levels of statins (simvastatin, atorvastatin), some ARBs, and montelukast because less drug is taken up into the liver for clearance", "Cyclosporine, clarithromycin, erythromycin, gemfibrozil, and grapefruit are notable OATP inhibitors — the cyclosporine + simvastatin combination is a classic example of increased myopathy/rhabdomyolysis risk", "Unlike P-gp, the clinical effect of OATP inhibition depends on which organ the transporter sits in: hepatic or renal inhibition raises drug levels, while intestinal OATP inhibition can instead lower absorption of some drugs"]}, {"heading": "High-Risk Interaction Categories to Recognize", "bullets": ["Hyperkalemia-risk stacking: ACEI/ARB + potassium-sparing diuretic (spironolactone, amiloride, triamterene) + trimethoprim/cotrimoxazole + potassium supplements + cyclosporine/tacrolimus all raise serum potassium by different mechanisms", "Additive hypoglycemia risk: combining sulfonylureas, meglitinides, insulin, or incretin-based therapies with each other or with cotrimoxazole/quinine increases hypoglycemia risk — patient education on recognition is key", "Competing dopaminergic/anticholinergic activity: giving an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor (donepezil) alongside an anticholinergic (oxybutynin, a TCA) makes the drugs work against each other pharmacodynamically rather than interacting kinetically", "In Parkinson disease, if an antipsychotic is unavoidable, quetiapine or clozapine carry the lowest risk of worsening dopaminergic symptoms; domperidone (limited CNS penetration) is preferred over metoclopramide for GI symptoms in these patients"]}]}, {"topic": "peds", "title": "Pediatrics — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Weight-Based Dosing Rules", "bullets": ["Always dose by actual body weight (mg/kg), not by age brackets — age charts are approximations only", "Double-check units: mg/kg/DOSE vs mg/kg/DAY — a classic MCQ trap that changes the answer 3-4x", "Convert lb to kg first (divide by 2.2) before any calculation", "Cap pediatric doses at the adult max dose when weight-based calc would exceed it (common in obese/larger children)", "Use calibrated oral syringes/dosing devices, never household spoons", "Common daily-dose ceilings that apply regardless of mg/kg math: acetaminophen 75 mg/kg/day, ibuprofen 40 mg/kg/day — always check the calculated dose against these caps", "Ibuprofen dosing differs by age band: 5 mg/kg/dose Q8H under 6 months, 5-10 mg/kg/dose Q6-8H from 6 months on — do not extrapolate the higher dose range to infants under 6 months", "Acetaminophen and ibuprofen are typically dosed Q4-6H and Q6-8H respectively — mixing up the interval is as common an error as mixing up the mg/kg amount", "When a pediatric weight-based dose calculates below the smallest practical/measurable volume, round to the nearest measurable amount on a calibrated syringe rather than under- or over-dosing to hit an exact number"]}, {"heading": "Age-Restricted OTC/Rx Products", "bullets": ["ASA contraindicated in children/teens with viral illness — Reye syndrome risk", "Cough/cold combination products not recommended under 6 years (many withdrawn under 6 in Canada)", "Ibuprofen contraindicated under 6 months of age", "Codeine contraindicated in children <12 years and in breastfeeding (CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizer risk of morphine toxicity)", "Diphenhydramine-based sleep aids not recommended in young children — paradoxical excitation risk", "Topical anesthetics containing benzocaine should not be used in children under 2 years old due to risk of methemoglobinemia if ingested or over-applied", "Amber teething necklaces are not recommended — risk of strangulation/choking outweighs any unproven benefit", "Teething biscuits are discouraged due to their high sugar content contributing to early childhood caries", "First-generation antihistamines (e.g., dimenhydrinate) used for simple nausea in young children carry anticholinergic risks similar to diphenhydramine — use cautiously"]}, {"heading": "Common Calculation Traps", "bullets": ["Confusing the drug's concentration (e.g., 160 mg/5 mL vs 80 mg/2.5 mL) — always read label concentration, don't assume", "Forgetting to convert mg to mL using the actual product concentration given in the stem", "Using ideal body weight vs actual weight when the question specifies one or the other", "Missing that some max daily doses are capped independent of weight (e.g., acetaminophen daily max)", "Rounding errors — round to a measurable/practical volume on an oral syringe", "Confusing which weight to use — actual vs. ideal body weight — matters most in obese children; default to actual weight unless a question specifies otherwise", "Overlooking that some products come in multiple concentrations for the same drug and age group (e.g., different acetaminophen concentrations for infant vs. children's formulations) — always confirm which product/concentration is in front of you", "Errors converting between mg of a salt form and mg of active/base drug (e.g., phenytoin sodium vs. acid) also occur in pediatric dosing scenarios, not just adult TDM questions"]}, {"heading": "Vaccination Catch-Up Basics", "bullets": ["Minor illness without fever is NOT a contraindication to vaccination — true contraindications are anaphylaxis to a prior dose/component or specific live-vaccine immunosuppression concerns", "Live vaccines (MMR, varicella) can be given same day or must be spaced ≥4 weeks apart if not simultaneous", "Catch-up schedules follow NACI/provincial guidance — minimum intervals between doses matter more than exact age", "Prophylactic antipyretics before vaccination are generally NOT recommended (may blunt immune response)", "Pharmacists play a key role in identifying gaps and non-judgmental vaccine hesitancy counselling"]}, {"heading": "Pediatric Asthma Devices", "bullets": ["Spacer (+/- mask for young children) is standard of care — dramatically improves MDI lung deposition", "Rescue (SABA e.g. salbutamol) is PRN only; controller (ICS e.g. fluticasone) must be used daily even when well", "Rinse mouth after ICS use to prevent thrush; not needed after SABA", "Needing reliever >2x/week signals poor control and need for step-up therapy", "Age-appropriate device choice: spacer+mask for infants/toddlers, spacer+mouthpiece for older children, dry powder inhalers generally not until child can generate adequate inspiratory flow (~school age)", "Poor inhaler technique is a common cause of apparent 'treatment failure' in pediatric asthma — always assess technique before escalating therapy", "Holding chambers (spacers) reduce oropharyngeal deposition and systemic absorption from inhaled corticosteroids, lowering both local (thrush) and systemic side-effect risk", "Children unable to coordinate breath-actuation should not be started on dry powder inhalers regardless of chronological age — assess inspiratory flow/technique, not just age cutoffs"]}, {"heading": "Red Flags Requiring Referral", "bullets": ["Fever in infant <3 months of age — always urgent medical referral", "Signs of dehydration: no tears, dry mouth, sunken fontanelle, reduced wet diapers, lethargy", "High fever >3 days duration, or any fever with stiff neck/rash/lethargy", "Difficulty breathing, stridor, or retractions", "Any suspected poisoning/overdose (e.g., iron, acetaminophen) — immediate poison control/ED referral", "Persistent fever beyond 3 days, or any fever exceeding 40.5°C, warrants medical assessment regardless of the child's other symptoms", "A fever accompanied by a new rash, persistent cough/wheezing, or occurring after a recently started medication should prompt referral to rule out drug fever or a more serious process", "Recent travel history with fever raises concern for illnesses not typically seen locally and should prompt medical referral", "Antipyretics do NOT reduce the risk of febrile seizures — a common misconception; treat fever for comfort, not seizure prevention"]}, {"heading": "Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Amoxicillin rash in a child with concurrent mononucleosis is a non-allergic reaction, not a true penicillin allergy", "High-dose amoxicillin (80-90 mg/kg/day) is used for AOM specifically to overcome resistant S. pneumoniae — don't pick standard-dose in that scenario", "ORS is preferred over sports drinks/juice for rehydration — osmolarity/electrolyte ratio matters", "Iron overdose is a leading cause of accidental pediatric poisoning death — always counsel on safe storage", "Stimulant ADHD medications require baseline and periodic growth/cardiovascular monitoring, not just symptom tracking", "ASA should be avoided in anyone under 18 years old (not just younger children) due to Reye syndrome risk, including with viral illnesses like influenza or varicella", "Rehydration math: mild-moderate dehydration uses oral rehydration therapy (ORT) at ~50 mL/kg over 4 hours; severe dehydration starts with an IV normal saline bolus (20 mL/kg) before calculating remaining deficit plus maintenance fluids", "Do not rehydrate a dehydrated infant with plain water alone — risk of hyponatremia and hypoglycemia; sports drinks and juice are also discouraged due to inappropriate electrolyte/osmolarity ratios", "The Holliday-Segar method estimates maintenance fluid needs: 100 mL/kg for the first 10 kg, plus 50 mL/kg for the next 10 kg, plus 20 mL/kg for each kg above 20 kg"]}, {"heading": "Dehydration Assessment & Fluid Therapy", "bullets": ["Dehydration severity by volume loss (children ≥1 year at the lower end, infants <1 year at the upper end of each range): mild 3-5%, moderate 6-9%, severe >10%", "Clinical clues separating mild from severe: normal capillary refill and minimal signs in mild dehydration, versus sunken eyes/fontanelle, absent tears, hypotension, and lethargy in severe dehydration", "Oral rehydration therapy (ORT) is first-line for mild-moderate dehydration and is equally effective as IV fluids while being safer and less resource-intensive", "Ondansetron given 15-30 minutes before starting ORT reduces vomiting and improves oral fluid tolerance, reducing the need for IV fluids and hospital admission", "For severe dehydration, give a normal saline bolus (20 mL/kg over 10-15 minutes) first to restore perfusion, then replace the remaining deficit plus maintenance fluids over the next 24 hours (roughly 50% over the first 8h, 50% over the next 16h)", "Refer to medical care: all moderate-severe dehydration, infants <6 months old, and any child with diarrhea >24h or vomiting >6h"]}, {"heading": "Common Infant Minor Ailments (Colic, Teething, Diaper Dermatitis)", "bullets": ["Infant colic is defined by paroxysmal, hard-to-console crying (Wessel's 'rule of three': ≥3 hours/day, ≥3 days/week, ≥3 weeks) in an otherwise healthy, well-fed infant, typically resolving by 3-4 months", "Colic red flags requiring referral: onset <2 weeks or >4-5 months of age, fever, weight loss, blood/mucus in stool, frequent vomiting, or failure to thrive — these suggest an alternate diagnosis, not colic", "First-line colic management is non-pharmacologic (positioning, swaddling, reducing overstimulation); simethicone is the only Health Canada-licensed product for infant colic, though evidence suggests much of its benefit is placebo", "Teething typically begins around 6-7 months; symptoms (gum irritation, drooling, mild fussiness/low-grade fever) peak 1-2 days before tooth eruption and resolve within about 3 days after — topical benzocaine gels are NOT recommended under age 2", "Diaper dermatitis: the irritant type spares the skin folds and is most common; candidal diaper dermatitis is a 'beefy-red' rash WITH involvement of the skin folds plus satellite pustules, often after >3 days of untreated irritant dermatitis or recent antibiotic use", "Correct topical application order for diaper dermatitis with suspected candidal infection: antifungal first, then barrier — UNLESS the antifungal is in a petroleum/PEG-based ointment, in which case apply the steroid before the barrier; zinc oxide 20-40% is used for treatment (10-20% for prevention)"]}]}, {"topic": "geri", "title": "Geriatrics & Palliative Care — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Beers Criteria Highlights", "bullets": ["Avoid benzodiazepines and Z-drugs (zopiclone) in older adults — falls, fractures, cognitive impairment", "Avoid strong anticholinergics (oxybutynin, diphenhydramine, tertiary TCAs like amitriptyline) — delirium, falls, constipation, urinary retention", "Avoid glyburide specifically among sulfonylureas — prolonged, severe hypoglycemia risk in elderly", "Antipsychotics carry boxed warning for increased mortality in dementia-related psychosis — lowest dose, shortest duration", "Digoxin doses >0.125 mg/day generally avoided — narrow therapeutic index, reduced renal clearance with age", "Avoid chronic NSAID use in older adults when possible — elevated risk of GI bleeding, exacerbation of heart failure, and acute kidney injury, especially combined with an ACEI/ARB or diuretic", "Avoid first-generation antihistamines and skeletal muscle relaxants (e.g., cyclobenzaprine) — strong anticholinergic/sedative burden with limited added efficacy in older adults", "Avoid long-term PPI use beyond 8 weeks without a clear ongoing indication — associated risks include fracture, C. difficile infection, and vitamin B12/magnesium deficiency", "Avoid sliding-scale insulin as a sole regimen and avoid megestrol for appetite stimulation — both carry unfavorable risk-benefit in older adults"]}, {"heading": "STOPP/START Tools", "bullets": ["STOPP = Screening Tool of Older Persons' Prescriptions — flags potentially inappropriate medications to STOP", "START = Screening Tool to Alert to Right Treatment — flags indicated therapies that may be missing (e.g., START a bone protection agent in osteoporosis)", "STOPP flags benzodiazepine use ≥4 weeks in patients with a falls history", "Both tools are used together in comprehensive geriatric medication review, not just deprescribing", "Complement, don't replace, clinical judgment — patient goals of care take priority near end of life", "STOPP also flags NSAID use with an anticoagulant (bleeding risk) and long-term proton pump inhibitor use beyond the indicated duration", "START commonly flags: a statin in patients with established atherosclerotic disease, an ACEI in heart failure or diabetic nephropathy, and calcium/vitamin D plus an antiresorptive in documented osteoporosis", "STOPP/START tools are meant for medication REVIEW at any care transition (hospital discharge, new diagnosis, annual review) — not a one-time checklist used only at initiation"]}, {"heading": "Deprescribing Principles", "bullets": ["Review indication for every drug — is it still needed, was it started for a now-resolved issue?", "Taper rather than stop abruptly for drugs with withdrawal/rebound risk (benzodiazepines, opioids, corticosteroids, PPIs)", "Prioritize deprescribing drugs with the highest harm-to-benefit ratio in the current context (e.g., statins in very limited life expectancy)", "Watch for prescribing cascades — a new drug started to treat a side effect of another drug (e.g., anticholinergic causing constipation treated with more medication instead of addressing the cause)", "Involve patient/family in shared decision-making, especially reframing goals near end of life (comfort vs prevention)", "A structured medication review can use the IESU framework — Indication, Efficacy, Safety, Utilization — to systematically decide whether each drug should be continued, adjusted, or stopped", "Ask for every medication: is it still indicated, is it working, is it safe at this dose in this patient, and can the patient actually use/adhere to it — a 'no' to any of these is a trigger to reconsider therapy", "When stopping multiple medications, taper and stop one at a time where feasible so that any withdrawal effect or symptom recurrence can be attributed to the correct drug", "Time-to-benefit matters: some preventive therapies (e.g., statins, bisphosphonates) take years to show benefit — in patients with limited life expectancy, the time-to-benefit may exceed their remaining life expectancy, favoring deprescribing"]}, {"heading": "Anticholinergic Burden", "bullets": ["Cumulative effect matters — multiple mild anticholinergics can add up to high total burden (ACB scale)", "Classic culprits: oxybutynin, diphenhydramine, amitriptyline, some antipsychotics, some antiemetics", "Signs: dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, confusion/delirium, falls", "Higher burden associated with long-term cognitive decline risk in observational studies", "Prefer lower-burden alternatives: mirabegron over oxybutynin, second-gen antihistamines over diphenhydramine, nortriptyline over amitriptyline if a TCA is truly needed", "Anticholinergic burden scales (e.g., ACB scale) assign points per drug and sum them — a patient can reach a 'high burden' from several mild-moderate anticholinergics even if no single drug looks alarming", "Commonly overlooked anticholinergic contributors: some antipsychotics (olanzapine, quetiapine), antispasmodics, and older antidepressants (paroxetine, TCAs) — always check the whole medication list, not just obvious agents like diphenhydramine", "Preferred lower-burden substitutions: mirabegron instead of oxybutynin for overactive bladder, second-generation antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine) instead of diphenhydramine, and SSRIs/SNRIs instead of TCAs where appropriate"]}, {"heading": "Palliative Symptom Management Basics", "bullets": ["Low-dose opioids are first-line for refractory dyspnea, not just pain — key distinguishing palliative concept", "Always co-prescribe a stimulant laxative (e.g., senna) prophylactically with regular opioids — constipation does not resolve with tolerance", "Glycopyrrolate (or scopolamine) is used for terminal secretions ('death rattle') — start early, as it prevents new secretions but doesn't clear existing ones", "Haloperidol (low dose) is used for palliative nausea and for severe delirium-related agitation after non-pharm measures fail", "Dexamethasone serves multiple overlapping palliative roles: appetite stimulation, reducing mass-effect pain/edema, mood/energy — comfort-focused risk/benefit differs from long-term use", "Opioid dose titration for dyspnea uses much lower doses than for pain in opioid-naive patients — starting low and titrating to effect minimizes sedation/respiratory risk while still relieving air hunger", "Bowel regimens for opioid-induced constipation should be started proactively, not reactively — tolerance develops to most opioid side effects except constipation", "Anticipate and pre-order PRN medications for common end-of-life symptoms (pain, dyspnea, secretions, agitation) so a caregiver or nurse can respond without delay when a new symptom appears"]}, {"heading": "Renal & Hepatic Adjustments in Elderly", "bullets": ["Renal function (via Cockcroft-Gault, using actual body weight generally) commonly declines with age even with normal serum creatinine — always calculate CrCl, don't rely on creatinine alone", "Morphine's active metabolite (M6G) accumulates in renal impairment — consider hydromorphone or fentanyl as alternatives", "Digoxin, gabapentin, and many antibiotics need renal dose adjustment in elderly", "Reduced hepatic first-pass metabolism and lower muscle mass alter volume of distribution — 'start low, go slow' applies broadly", "Reassess renal/hepatic function periodically, not just at treatment initiation, as it can decline further with acute illness/dehydration", "Serum creatinine can be misleadingly 'normal' in older adults due to reduced muscle mass — always calculate creatinine clearance (Cockcroft-Gault) rather than relying on serum creatinine alone to judge renal function", "Falsely low serum creatinine (and thus falsely reassuring CrCl) can occur in malnourished, bedbound, or chronically ill elderly patients, or those on chronic corticosteroids — have a lower threshold to dose-reduce in these patients", "Vancomycin and aminoglycoside dosing intervals must be extended (not just dose-reduced) as renal function declines, since these drugs are almost entirely renally cleared"]}, {"heading": "Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["A drug being available 'over-the-counter' does not mean it is safe in the elderly — diphenhydramine is a top Beers-flagged OTC offender", "Sedation plus orthostatic hypotension (e.g., from a TCA) is a classic combination tested as a falls-risk scenario", "Hypoglycemia in an elderly patient can present as confusion or a fall rather than classic sweating/shakiness — consider it in atypical presentations", "'Comfort care' / palliative goals can justify continuing or stopping medications that would otherwise follow standard chronic-disease guidelines (e.g., stopping statins, continuing opioids liberally)", "Glycopyrrolate is preferred over scopolamine when less sedation/delirium risk is desired, because it doesn't cross the blood-brain barrier", "A 'natural' or herbal product is not automatically low-risk in older adults — St. John's Wort, for example, is a potent enzyme inducer that can cause treatment failure of many chronic medications", "Deprescribing a chronic medication is not the same as it never having been needed — a drug can be entirely appropriate when started and become inappropriate later as goals of care, organ function, or life expectancy change", "New symptoms in an elderly patient should always prompt a look for a prescribing cascade before starting an additional drug to treat the 'new' symptom"]}, {"heading": "Medication Review Framework (IESU Model)", "bullets": ["IESU = Indication, Efficacy, Safety, Utilization — a structured model for evaluating whether a prescription (new or refill) remains appropriate", "Indication: is the drug still needed for an active problem, and is it a first-line/guideline-appropriate choice for that indication?", "Efficacy: is the drug/dose actually working — has therapy been titrated, and is there evidence (labs, symptoms, vitals) supporting continued benefit?", "Safety: is the patient experiencing an adverse effect, an unsafe dose for their organ function, or a clinically relevant drug interaction?", "Utilization: can the patient actually take the medication as intended — consider health literacy, affordability, dexterity, swallowing, and device technique before assuming non-adherence is the patient's 'fault'", "A Best Possible Medication History (BPMH) — capturing every prescription, OTC, and natural health product with dose/route/frequency — is the foundation any deprescribing or IESU review is built on"]}, {"heading": "Chat, Check, Chart & Documentation for Geriatric Reviews", "bullets": ["The 'Chat, Check, Chart' framework structures a geriatric medication review: Chat (open-ended questions about purpose, directions, and expected effect), Check (assess indication/efficacy/safety/utilization), Chart (document findings and plan)", "Common documentation formats for a medication review: SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) and DAP (Data, Assessment, Plan) — either works as long as findings, rationale, and a monitoring/follow-up plan are captured", "A pharmacist recommendation to a prescriber should always include the drug-related problem identified, supporting rationale, and a specific actionable suggestion (e.g., a specific alternative drug and dose), not just 'please review'", "Effective follow-up specifies who will follow up, how (in-person, phone, labs), and by when — a vague plan to 'monitor' is not an adequate deprescribing follow-up plan"]}]}, {"topic": "onc", "title": "Oncology & Hematology — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Chemo Safety Handling Basics", "bullets": ["Hazardous drugs require PPE: chemo gloves (double-gloved), gown, eye protection when compounding/administering", "Use a biological safety cabinet (Class II) or isolator for compounding; never compound hazardous drugs on an open bench", "Spill kits must be readily available wherever hazardous drugs are stored/prepared/administered", "Closed-system transfer devices (CSTDs) reduce aerosolization/exposure risk during preparation and administration", "Pregnant/breastfeeding staff should avoid handling hazardous drugs per workplace policy", "Dispose of chemo waste in designated cytotoxic waste containers, not regular sharps/trash", "G-CSF (filgrastim) should not be given within 24 hours before or after cytotoxic chemotherapy — timing errors around this window worsen marrow toxicity rather than helping", "Live vaccines should be avoided for at least 4 weeks before, and generally 3-6 months after, completing chemotherapy due to immunosuppression", "Inactivated vaccines remain appropriate during chemotherapy and are an important infection-prevention measure alongside hand hygiene during neutropenic periods"]}, {"heading": "Emetogenicity Risk Tiers & Prophylaxis", "bullets": ["High emetic risk (e.g., cisplatin, AC combo): 5-HT3 antagonist + NK1 antagonist + dexamethasone (+/- olanzapine)", "Moderate risk (e.g., carboplatin, oxaliplatin): 5-HT3 antagonist + dexamethasone (+/- NK1 antagonist)", "Low risk (e.g., paclitaxel, gemcitabine): single agent, e.g., dexamethasone or 5-HT3 antagonist alone", "Minimal risk (e.g., vincristine, bleomycin): no routine prophylaxis needed", "Delayed CINV (24h-5 days post-chemo) needs ongoing coverage — dexamethasone/NK1 antagonist continued, not just acute-phase ondansetron", "Anticipatory nausea is best prevented, not treated — consider lorazepam adjunct for anxious patients with prior bad experience", "For delayed CINV prevention after high-risk chemo, continue dexamethasone + NK1 antagonist + olanzapine (drop the 5-HT3 antagonist) — except with the netupitant/palonosetron (NEPA) combination pill, which needs no additional NK1 dosing for the delayed phase due to both components' long half-lives", "Palonosetron has roughly 100-fold higher 5-HT3 receptor affinity and a much longer half-life (~40h vs ~4h for ondansetron) than older 5-HT3 antagonists, making it preferred for delayed-phase coverage and highly emetogenic regimens", "Aprepitant/fosaprepitant are moderate CYP3A4 inhibitors (and CYP2C9 inducers) — when combined with dexamethasone, the dexamethasone dose should be reduced by about 50% to avoid excess corticosteroid exposure", "Olanzapine is both a preferred add-on for high-risk acute/delayed CINV prophylaxis AND the preferred agent for breakthrough CINV if not already part of the scheduled regimen"]}, {"heading": "Myelosuppression & Neutropenic Fever Red Flags", "bullets": ["Nadir (lowest blood counts) typically occurs 7-14 days post-chemo — highest infection risk window", "Febrile neutropenia = single temp >=38.3C (or >=38.0C sustained 1h) PLUS ANC <0.5x10^9/L (or <1.0 trending down) — this is a MEDICAL EMERGENCY", "Febrile neutropenia requires immediate empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics — do not wait for culture results", "Counsel patients to seek immediate care for fever during nadir period, avoid crowds/sick contacts, and practice hand hygiene", "G-CSF (filgrastim) primary prophylaxis considered when regimen carries >20% febrile neutropenia risk", "Avoid NSAIDs/antipyretics that could mask fever in at-risk neutropenic patients without medical guidance", "Nadir timing and neutropenia risk both stem from the same cause: chemotherapy disrupts bone marrow cell renewal, which normally takes about 10-14 days to differentiate, so counts drop before they recover", "Live vaccines are contraindicated during active myelosuppression; inactivated vaccines are preferred and should still be kept up to date", "The G-CSF primary prophylaxis threshold (>20% febrile neutropenia risk with the regimen) is a testable number — filgrastim is typically given daily for about 7 days, while pegfilgrastim is given as a single dose per cycle"]}, {"heading": "Extravasation Risk Drugs", "bullets": ["Vesicants (severe tissue damage if extravasated): doxorubicin, vincristine, vinblastine, mitomycin, dactinomycin", "Irritants (less severe but still concerning): oxaliplatin, carboplatin, etoposide", "Anthracycline extravasation: STOP infusion, aspirate, apply cold compress, consider dexrazoxane antidote", "Vinca alkaloid extravasation: apply WARM compress (opposite of anthracyclines) — classic exam contrast point", "Early signs: pain, burning, swelling, redness at IV site during infusion — stop immediately and assess", "Specific antidotes exist for some vesicants: dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) for anthracyclines and antitumor antibiotics, dexrazoxane for anthracyclines, and sodium thiosulfate for cisplatin", "Mechlorethamine and platinum compounds (in addition to anthracyclines, vinca alkaloids, and antitumor antibiotics) are also recognized vesicants/irritants requiring extravasation precautions", "Prevention starts before the infusion: confirm good IV line flow and appropriate placement, and monitor the site throughout the infusion rather than only reacting to reported pain", "General extravasation management: stop the infusion, aspirate residual drug from the line/tissue, then apply the temperature-specific compress (cold for anthracyclines, warm for vinca alkaloids) four times daily for 1-2 days"]}, {"heading": "Cancer-Associated Thrombosis (CAT) Anticoagulation", "bullets": ["Cancer patients have markedly elevated VTE risk (Trousseau's syndrome) from tumor procoagulant factors", "LMWH (e.g., dalteparin) historically preferred over warfarin — better efficacy, avoids interaction complexity with chemo/nutrition", "DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban) now guideline-supported alternatives EXCEPT in GI/GU luminal malignancies (higher bleeding risk)", "Warfarin is generally avoided as first-line in active cancer due to unpredictable INR control with chemo interactions/nutritional changes", "Duration of anticoagulation is typically extended (often indefinite) while cancer remains active, unlike standard provoked VTE", "Minimum treatment duration for cancer-associated VTE is 3-6 months; extend longer if the patient still has active/metastatic disease, is on ongoing systemic therapy, or has persistent risk factors and low bleeding risk", "LMWH is specifically preferred over a DOAC when there is GI/GU tract malignancy or active GI/GU bleeding, significant thrombocytopenia, or pregnancy/breastfeeding", "Thromboembolism is a recognized side effect of anti-angiogenic drugs (bevacizumab, sunitinib) and thalidomide — prophylactic anticoagulation is considered for ambulatory patients on thalidomide-based regimens"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Vincristine is FATAL if given intrathecally — IV administration only, always double-check route", "Weekly low-dose oral methotrexate (RA) mistakenly given DAILY has caused fatal overdoses — verify frequency", "Leucovorin (folinic acid, MTX rescue) vs leuprolide (GnRH agonist) — sound-alike names, very different drugs", "G-CSF should NOT be given within 24h before/after cytotoxic chemo — timing error worsens marrow toxicity", "Anthracyclines: cold compress for extravasation; Vinca alkaloids: warm compress — do not mix these up", "TNM staging: T describes primary tumor size/extent, N describes regional lymph node involvement, and M describes distant metastasis — a higher number in any category means more extensive disease", "The log-kill hypothesis explains why chemotherapy is given in repeated pulses rather than one large dose — each cycle kills a constant FRACTION (not a constant number) of remaining cancer cells, so multiple cycles are needed to approach zero", "Coronary vasospasm is a distinctive toxicity of fluoropyrimidines (capecitabine, 5-fluorouracil) — reversible on stopping the drug, sometimes managed with calcium-channel blockers and nitrates", "Hand-foot syndrome (palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia) is associated with capecitabine, liposomal doxorubicin, sorafenib, sunitinib, and 5-fluorouracil — prevention includes regular moisturizing and avoiding heat/friction to hands and feet"]}, {"heading": "Chemotherapy Drug Classes & Mechanisms", "bullets": ["Alkylating agents (cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, melphalan, chlorambucil, platinum compounds) cross-link DNA strands, preventing unwinding and causing breakage — common toxicities include myelosuppression, nausea/vomiting, and mucositis", "Antimetabolites (5-fluorouracil, methotrexate, cytarabine) are incorporated into DNA/RNA synthesis pathways as fraudulent building blocks, blocking nucleotide synthesis — associated with diarrhea, myelosuppression, and mucositis", "Antitumor antibiotics/anthracyclines (doxorubicin, daunorubicin, epirubicin, bleomycin) intercalate into DNA and generate free radicals that cause strand breaks — anthracyclines carry a dose-dependent cardiotoxicity risk requiring lifetime cumulative dose limits", "Vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine, vinorelbine) block microtubule polymerization, arresting mitosis — vincristine's dose-limiting toxicity is neurotoxicity rather than myelosuppression", "Taxanes (paclitaxel, docetaxel) stabilize microtubules so they can't disassemble, also arresting mitosis — associated with hypersensitivity reactions, neuropathy, and fluid retention in addition to typical myelosuppression", "Topoisomerase inhibitors: epipodophyllotoxins (etoposide) inhibit topoisomerase II causing double-strand breaks; camptothecins (irinotecan, topotecan) inhibit topoisomerase I causing single-strand breaks", "Targeted/tyrosine kinase inhibitors (imatinib, sunitinib, sorafenib) block specific growth-signaling receptors rather than broadly targeting rapidly dividing cells, shifting their toxicity profile toward skin reactions, hypertension, and hand-foot syndrome rather than classic myelosuppression alone"]}, {"heading": "Anemia Classification & Iron Studies (Oncology-Relevant Hematology)", "bullets": ["Classify anemia by red cell size (MCV): microcytic (iron deficiency, thalassemia), normocytic (anemia of chronic disease, acute blood loss, hemolysis), or macrocytic (B12/folate deficiency)", "Iron deficiency anemia shows low ferritin, low serum iron, and HIGH total iron-binding capacity (TIBC); anemia of chronic disease/inflammation typically shows normal-to-high ferritin with LOW TIBC — an important distinguishing pattern in cancer patients", "Anemia of chronic disease is common in active malignancy — treat the underlying cancer/inflammation first; only add an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) once true iron/B12/folate deficiency has been ruled out or corrected", "B12 deficiency must be ruled out before starting folic acid supplementation alone — folic acid can correct the megaloblastic blood picture while allowing B12-related neurologic damage to silently progress", "Hemoglobin <100 g/L is a common threshold used to consider anemia treatment in oncology patients; not everyone with chemotherapy-related anemia is an appropriate candidate for an ESA (e.g., an ESA is not appropriate if the anemia is actually due to iron deficiency)", "Oral iron is generally first-line even in cancer-associated iron deficiency; parenteral iron is reserved for malabsorption, intolerance, chronic kidney disease, or when a rapid rise in iron stores is needed"]}]}, {"topic": "immun", "title": "Immunizations & Travel Health — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "NACI Schedule Highlights — Childhood", "bullets": ["Routine series starts at 2 months: DTaP-IPV-Hib, pneumococcal conjugate (PCV), rotavirus", "MMR: first dose at 12 months, second dose per province (commonly 18 months or 4-6 years)", "Varicella vaccine typically given around 12-18 months, second dose in childhood", "Meningococcal conjugate given in infancy and boosted in adolescence", "HPV vaccine typically offered around grade 6-7 (age 9-12), 2-dose schedule if started before age 15", "Hib and pneumococcal conjugate infant series both run alongside DTaP-IPV at 2, 4, 6 months plus a toddler booster; Hib is not needed after age 5 in healthy children", "Rotavirus vaccine has strict age windows for starting and completing the series (varies by product) because of an intussusception risk if given too late", "Routine hepatitis B childhood series is typically 3 doses at 0, 1, and 6 months, though some provinces use a 2-dose adolescent schedule instead (e.g., grade 7)", "Children who already completed the older 13-valent pneumococcal conjugate series do not need catch-up doses of the newer 15/20-valent products"]}, {"heading": "NACI Schedule Highlights — Adult & Senior", "bullets": ["Td/Tdap booster every 10 years; at least one adult dose should be Tdap to maintain pertussis coverage", "Influenza vaccine annually for all adults 6 months+; high-dose/adjuvanted formulations preferred for 65+", "Shingrix (recombinant zoster) recommended starting age 50, 2-dose series 2-6 months apart", "Pneumococcal vaccination (PCV +/- PPSV23) recommended for 65+ or high-risk chronic conditions", "RSV vaccine recommended for adults 60+, particularly those with chronic cardiopulmonary conditions", "Tdap recommended in every pregnancy, ideally 27-32 weeks gestation, for passive newborn protection", "Adults 65+ with no prior pneumococcal vaccine: single dose of the newer higher-valent conjugate vaccine; those previously given an older pneumococcal vaccine can receive one dose of a newer conjugate vaccine at least 1 year later (minimum 8 weeks if starting immunosuppressive therapy)", "Adults 50-64 with chronic heart, liver, lung, or kidney disease, diabetes, or immunocompromise also qualify for pneumococcal vaccination ahead of the general 65+ recommendation", "The RSV vaccine indicated in pregnancy is given at 32-36 weeks gestation and is not needed if the infant will instead receive the RSV monoclonal antibody", "The RSV monoclonal antibody (nirsevimab) is not a vaccine — it is passive immunization given to infants under 8 months entering their first RSV season"]}, {"heading": "Live vs Inactivated Vaccines — Contraindications", "bullets": ["Live vaccines (MMR, varicella, live zoster [old], oral typhoid, yellow fever, FluMist, rotavirus) contraindicated in pregnancy and significant immunocompromise", "Inactivated/recombinant vaccines (flu shot, Tdap, HPV, hepatitis A/B, Shingrix, pneumococcal) are safe in pregnancy and immunocompromised patients", "Two live vaccines given on different days must be spaced >=4 weeks apart; no spacing needed if given same day", "No spacing restriction needed between a live and an inactivated vaccine given on different days", "Antibody-containing blood products can blunt live vaccine response — spacing (weeks-months) required before/after MMR/varicella depending on product", "Live vaccines must actively replicate to generate immunity — this is exactly why uncontrolled replication in immunocompromised patients makes them unsafe", "Inactivated/subunit vaccines produce a mostly humoral response and always require more than one dose for full protection, unlike some live vaccines", "Severe asthma (recent oral or high-dose inhaled steroids, or active wheezing within 7 days) is specifically a contraindication to the live attenuated (intranasal) influenza vaccine, not the injectable flu shot"]}, {"heading": "Cold Chain Basics", "bullets": ["Most vaccines require storage at 2-8C (refrigerator) — monitor with a calibrated thermometer/data logger", "Never freeze vaccines that require refrigeration (e.g., most inactivated vaccines) — freezing destroys efficacy", "Some products (e.g., certain mRNA vaccines) require ultra-cold or freezer storage — check product monograph", "Have a cold-chain break protocol: quarantine affected vaccines, do not use/discard until manufacturer/public health confirms viability", "Transport vaccines in insulated coolers with appropriate cold packs and monitor temperature during transit", "Frozen vaccine products are stored between -15C and -50C; most routine vaccines are refrigerated only, so always check the specific product before assuming freezer storage", "Reconstituted vaccines have their own, usually much shorter, stability window separate from the unopened vial — track and label the reconstitution time", "mRNA vaccines requiring dilution have the shortest post-dilution stability of commonly used products (a matter of hours in the fridge) — mark the dilution time on the vial"]}, {"heading": "Travel Vaccine Categories", "bullets": ["Routine: ensure standard schedule (MMR, Tdap, flu) is up to date regardless of destination", "Recommended: risk-based on destination/activity — hepatitis A/B, typhoid, rabies, Japanese encephalitis", "Required: legally mandated for entry — yellow fever (IHR-regulated countries), meningococcal ACWY (Hajj/Umrah)", "Yellow fever vaccine must be given at a designated Yellow Fever Vaccination Centre; certificate valid 10 days after dose", "Malaria prevention is chemoprophylaxis (not a vaccine) — separate counselling on antimalarials plus bite prevention", "Oral cholera/ETEC travelers' diarrhea vaccine requires avoiding food, drink, or other oral medication for 1 hour before and after each dose", "Typhoid vaccine comes as an oral live series (multiple doses over about a week, booster every several years) or a single-dose injectable inactivated form (booster less frequently) — pick based on timeline and patient preference", "Japanese encephalitis vaccine is a 2-dose series with a standard or accelerated schedule depending on age; the final dose should be completed at least 1 week before potential exposure"]}, {"heading": "Injection Technique & Administration", "bullets": ["IM injections are given at a 90 degree angle; SC injections are given at a 45 degree angle", "Standard IM needle gauge is 22-25G, with length (5/8 to 1.5 inch) chosen based on patient weight and site so the needle reliably reaches muscle", "IM site by age: under 12 months = anterolateral thigh; 12 months to 12 years = deltoid or anterolateral thigh; adolescents/adults = deltoid preferred", "SC site by age: under 12 months = anterolateral thigh; 12 months and older = upper triceps area or anterolateral thigh", "Space multiple injections in the same limb at least 1 inch apart", "Monitor every patient for at least 15 minutes after injection for immediate reactions (syncope, anaphylaxis); local reactions like pain, redness, and swelling are typically delayed-onset", "Report suspected adverse events following immunization through the national post-market vaccine safety surveillance system", "Intranasal is one of the recognized vaccine administration routes (used for live attenuated influenza vaccine), alongside IM, SC, ID, and IV"]}, {"heading": "Malaria Prophylaxis & Other Travel-Specific Prevention", "bullets": ["Atovaquone-proguanil: start 1-2 days before travel, continue daily during the trip, stop 7 days after leaving — convenient for last-minute or short trips, but avoid in severe renal impairment and pregnancy", "Doxycycline: start 1-2 days before, continue for 28 days after leaving — least expensive option, but causes photosensitivity and must be taken with food and plenty of water while staying upright to avoid esophageal irritation", "Mefloquine and hydroxychloroquine are dosed weekly and are the antimalarial options considered acceptable in pregnancy, but hydroxychloroquine only works where chloroquine resistance is absent, and mefloquine is contraindicated with a psychiatric or seizure history", "Primaquine is preferred where P. vivax predominates but requires G6PD testing first because of hemolysis risk", "No antimalarial regimen is 100% effective — insect-bite precautions (DEET/icaridin repellent, permethrin-treated nets and clothing) are always required in addition to chemoprophylaxis", "Acute mountain sickness prevention: ascend gradually, avoid alcohol and sedatives, favor a high-carbohydrate diet; acetazolamide or dexamethasone can be used prophylactically for rapid ascents above 3000 meters", "Routine anticoagulation is not recommended for flights over 4 hours without VTE risk factors; calf exercises, hydration, and graduated compression stockings are reasonable non-drug measures for higher-risk travelers", "Melatonin taken the first night after arrival (before the new local bedtime) is the best-evidenced treatment for jet lag; eastward travel causes more severe jet lag than westward"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Egg allergy is NOT a contraindication to standard-dose influenza or MMR vaccines — outdated teaching still shows up as a distractor", "MenACWY is legally REQUIRED for Hajj/Umrah travel, not just recommended — distinct from most other travel vaccines", "Yellow fever vaccine can only be administered at designated centres in Canada — cannot be given at any random pharmacy", "Conjugate (PCV) vaccines must be given BEFORE polysaccharide (PPSV23) when both are indicated, to optimize immune priming", "Passive maternal immunization (Tdap, RSV/Abrysvo in pregnancy) protects the newborn before their own vaccine series can start — same underlying concept, two different diseases", "A recent course of oral or high-dose inhaled corticosteroids, or wheezing in the past week, blocks the LIVE nasal flu vaccine specifically — the injectable flu shot remains an option", "Don't confuse a monoclonal antibody used for passive protection (e.g., against RSV in infants) with an active vaccine — they protect differently and are counted separately", "Pre-exposure rabies prophylaxis dosing depends on both risk level and how long that risk will last, not just occupation alone"]}]}, {"topic": "derm", "title": "Dermatology, Eye & ENT — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Topical Corticosteroid Potency Ladder", "bullets": ["7 potency groups in Canada: I = super-high potency (clobetasol) down to VII = low (hydrocortisone 0.5-1%)", "Face, groin, axillae, eyelids = LOW potency only (thin skin, high absorption)", "Palms/soles/thick plaques = higher potency often needed for penetration", "Occlusion (diapers, dressings) increases potency/absorption — adjust choice accordingly", "Long-term high-potency use risk: atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, HPA-axis suppression", "EXAM TRAP: 'just use the strongest one' is wrong — potency must match site and severity", "Avoid corticosteroids near the eyes — periocular use raises the risk of glaucoma and cataracts", "Combination products pairing a higher-potency steroid with a keratolytic (e.g., salicylic acid) improve penetration into thick plaques", "Ointments give the strongest occlusion and potency for a given steroid concentration; lotions, gels, and foams are better tolerated on hairy areas like the scalp"]}, {"heading": "Acne Treatment Ladder", "bullets": ["Mild comedonal: topical retinoid (tretinoin/adapalene) ± benzoyl peroxide", "Mild-moderate inflammatory: add topical antibiotic (clindamycin) WITH benzoyl peroxide (never topical antibiotic alone — resistance)", "Moderate-severe: add oral antibiotic (doxycycline/minocycline) for limited duration, or consider hormonal therapy (females)", "Severe/nodulocystic/scarring: oral isotretinoin (specialist-initiated, pregnancy prevention program mandatory)", "BPO + tretinoin = do NOT mix simultaneously (BPO oxidizes/inactivates tretinoin) — use adapalene instead or stagger AM/PM", "EXAM TRAP: benzoyl peroxide prevents antibiotic resistance — always pair topical/oral antibiotics with BPO", "Comedones are non-inflammatory; papules, pustules, nodules, and cysts are inflammatory lesions", "Mild acne (comedones, few papules/pustules, no nodules/cysts/scarring) is appropriate for self-care; any nodules, cysts, or scarring signal moderate-severe disease needing referral", "Adapalene is the least irritating topical retinoid and the only one that's photostable enough to apply in the morning; it's also available in a fixed-dose combination with benzoyl peroxide", "Benzoyl peroxide 2.5% is about as effective as 5-10% strengths and less irritating, with no evidence of bacterial resistance developing even with long-term monotherapy", "Oral antibiotics for acne should be limited to about 3 months of use and always paired with benzoyl peroxide to reduce resistance — never used alone", "Oral isotretinoin requires baseline and monthly pregnancy testing plus two reliable contraception methods in patients who can become pregnant, and a waiting period before starting a repeat course", "Spironolactone has no official acne indication but is used off-label as antiandrogenic therapy in women with hormonally-driven acne", "A washout period is recommended when switching from a tetracycline antibiotic to isotretinoin because combining the two raises the risk of pseudotumor cerebri"]}, {"heading": "Glaucoma Drop Classes", "bullets": ["Prostaglandin analogues (latanoprost) — increase uveoscleral outflow, once daily HS, first-line; causes iris/lash pigmentation", "Beta-blockers (timolol) — decrease aqueous production; avoid in asthma/COPD/bradycardia/heart block (systemic absorption via nasolacrimal duct)", "Alpha-2 agonists (brimonidine) — decrease production + increase outflow; avoid in young children (CNS depression risk)", "Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (dorzolamide) — decrease aqueous production; sulfa-related, caution with sulfa allergy", "EXAM TRAP: punctal occlusion after ANY eye drop reduces systemic absorption — critical for timolol to avoid systemic beta-blockade", "Normal intraocular pressure is roughly 10-21 mmHg; primary open-angle glaucoma (the most common type) develops slowly from increased resistance to aqueous humour drainage", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma is a medical emergency: sudden severe eye pain, blurred vision, halos around lights, and nausea/vomiting, with markedly elevated intraocular pressure", "Drug-induced glaucoma culprits include systemic/inhaled/nasal corticosteroids, anticholinergic agents (antihistamines, antidepressants), topiramate (linked to acute angle-closure), and long-term oral contraceptive use (open-angle)", "Cholinergic agonists (pilocarpine) are third-line for glaucoma because of frequent dosing and side effects like miosis, brow ache, and blurred vision, and are poorly tolerated in children and young adults", "Oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are reserved for emergencies because of systemic side effects (metabolic acidosis, paresthesias, renal stones) that roughly half of patients cannot tolerate long-term"]}, {"heading": "Eye Drop Administration Technique", "bullets": ["Wash hands, tilt head back, pull down lower lid to form pocket, instill drop without touching tip to eye", "Space multiple drops at least 5 minutes apart to prevent washout/dilution", "Apply punctal occlusion (press inner corner) for 1-2 minutes to reduce systemic absorption", "Ointments go in after drops (thicker, blurs vision) — use gtts before ung", "Remove contact lenses before most drops; wait 15 min before reinsertion (check product-specific advice)", "Without nasolacrimal (punctal) occlusion, a large fraction of an eye drop dose drains into the nasal system and can be absorbed systemically — occlusion reduces systemic exposure and increases ocular contact time", "Discard eye drop bottles 28 days after opening unless the product monograph specifies otherwise", "Shake suspension-based eye drops well before instilling"]}, {"heading": "Otic Drops — Key Points", "bullets": ["Otitis externa: acidifying/antibiotic ± steroid drops (e.g., ciprofloxacin/dexamethasone)", "Warm drops before use — cold drops can trigger vertigo/dizziness (caloric reflex)", "AVOID aminoglycosides (neomycin, gentamicin) if tympanic membrane perforation is suspected — ototoxicity risk", "Fluoroquinolone-based otic combos (e.g., Ciprodex) are safe even with perforated TM/tubes", "Lie with affected ear up for 1-2 minutes after instillation for contact time"]}, {"heading": "Red Flags — Eye Complaints Needing Referral", "bullets": ["Sudden vision loss or significant vision change", "Severe eye pain, photophobia with pain (possible uveitis/keratitis)", "Trauma, chemical exposure, or foreign body sensation", "Contact lens wearer with pain + redness (risk of microbial keratitis — urgent)", "Unequal pupils, halos around lights + severe pain (possible acute angle-closure glaucoma — emergency)", "New floaters or flashes of light can signal retinal detachment and need urgent referral"]}, {"heading": "Red Flags — Ear/Nose Complaints Needing Referral", "bullets": ["Hearing loss, severe pain, or bleeding from the ear", "Suspected tympanic membrane perforation with drainage", "Persistent nosebleeds not controlled with basic first aid, or recurrent epistaxis", "Signs of mastoiditis (swelling/redness behind ear, fever) — emergency", "Nasal symptoms with high fever, facial swelling, or vision changes (possible orbital cellulitis)", "Sudden hearing loss is an otologic emergency and needs prompt referral, distinct from gradual age-related hearing decline"]}, {"heading": "Rosacea — Phenotypes & Treatment", "bullets": ["Four major presentations: centrofacial erythema/flushing, papules/pustules (mimics acne but without comedones), phymatous changes (e.g., rhinophyma, more common in men), and ocular involvement (dryness, burning, blepharitis)", "Common triggers: sun exposure, spicy food, alcohol, hot drinks, temperature extremes, stress, and vasodilating drugs such as calcium channel blockers", "First-line for redness/flushing: topical brimonidine, metronidazole, or azelaic acid; first-line for papules/pustules: topical azelaic acid, metronidazole, or ivermectin", "Oral tetracyclines (doxycycline, tetracycline) are added for moderate-severe or treatment-resistant disease; avoid in pregnancy and in children under 8", "Ocular rosacea starts with eyelid hygiene (warm water or gentle cleanser) and artificial tears before escalating to oral antibiotics or specialist referral", "Long-term topical corticosteroid use can precipitate or worsen rosacea, so it should be avoided as a treatment for it", "In pregnancy, topical metronidazole and azelaic acid are preferred; tetracyclines and isotretinoin are contraindicated"]}, {"heading": "Psoriasis — Presentation & Treatment Ladder", "bullets": ["Plaque psoriasis (about 90% of cases) presents as well-demarcated red plaques with silvery scale; look for pinpoint bleeding when scale is removed and new plaques forming along lines of trauma", "Severity is estimated by body surface area using the palm method: mild under 3% BSA, moderate 3-10%, severe over 10%", "Guttate psoriasis classically follows a streptococcal infection and presents as sudden, small, drop-like papules on the trunk and limbs", "Mild-moderate plaque psoriasis first-line treatment is topical corticosteroids and/or vitamin D analogues; use low-potency steroids on the face and skin folds", "Moderate-severe disease requires systemic therapy: methotrexate, cyclosporine, oral retinoids, a PDE-4 inhibitor, or biologics targeting TNF, IL-17, or IL-23", "Avoid live vaccines during biologic or other systemic immunosuppressive therapy for psoriasis", "In pregnancy, topical corticosteroids, UVB, and cyclosporine (reserved for severe cases) are options; retinoids and methotrexate are contraindicated", "Psoriasis carries higher rates of psoriatic arthritis, cardiovascular disease, metabolic syndrome, depression, and inflammatory bowel disease — it behaves as a systemic disease, not just a skin condition"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Tretinoin + benzoyl peroxide together = tretinoin gets oxidized/inactivated — stagger dosing or switch to adapalene", "Latanoprost causes PERMANENT iris/lash pigmentation changes — must counsel proactively, not just list as a side effect", "Aminoglycoside otic drops (neomycin) are ototoxic if the eardrum is perforated — Ciprodex is the safe alternative", "Nasal corticosteroids need DAYS of regular use for full effect — not a PRN rescue med like decongestant sprays", "Punctal occlusion reduces systemic absorption/side effects for ALL eye drops, especially important for timolol (beta-blocker) systemic effects", "Prostaglandin analogues are first-line for glaucoma mainly for their safety profile — they are only slightly more effective than beta-blockers, not dramatically so", "Rosacea is frequently mistaken for acne, but rosacea lacks comedones, and long-term topical corticosteroid use can actually trigger or worsen it"]}]}, {"topic": "otc", "title": "OTC & Natural Health Products — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Self-Care Triage Frameworks", "bullets": ["WWHAM: Who is it for? What are the symptoms? How long? Action already taken? Medications currently taken?", "ASMETHOD: Age/appearance, Self or someone else, Medications, Extra symptoms, Time/duration, History, Danger symptoms, Other diagnoses", "Always confirm the patient isn't self-treating something that needs a diagnosis (e.g., chest pain as 'heartburn')", "Duration matters: symptoms persisting beyond typical self-limiting window (e.g., >1-2 weeks) = refer", "Screening for red flags and differential diagnoses always comes before recommending self-treatment, not after", "Reassess if self-treatment fails within the expected resolution window for that specific ailment — the window varies from days (insect bites) to weeks (corns, acne)"]}, {"heading": "Red Flags Requiring Referral (General)", "bullets": ["GI: rectal bleeding, black/tarry stools, unintended weight loss, dysphagia, persistent vomiting", "Pain: severe/unexplained pain, chest pain, pain not responding to appropriate OTC treatment", "Fever: high fever, fever >3 days, fever in infants <3 months (always refer)", "Any symptom in infants, pregnant/breastfeeding patients, or immunocompromised needs lower threshold for referral", "Symptoms recurring frequently or worsening despite appropriate self-treatment", "Age extremes (infants, frail elderly) and pregnancy/breastfeeding lower the threshold for referral across every minor ailment, not just the obviously serious ones", "New severe pain, a rapidly spreading rash, or systemic signs of infection (fever, malaise) warrant prompt referral regardless of the presenting complaint"]}, {"heading": "Classic NHP-Drug Interactions", "bullets": ["St. John's Wort — potent CYP3A4/P-gp INDUCER: reduces efficacy of OCPs, warfarin, cyclosporine, many antidepressants; serotonin syndrome risk with SSRIs/SNRIs/triptans", "Ginkgo biloba — increases bleeding risk with warfarin, DOACs, ASA, NSAIDs; stop 1-2 weeks pre-surgery", "Fish oil (high dose >3g/day) — antiplatelet effect, caution with anticoagulants", "Glucosamine — shellfish-derived (allergy caution), theoretical mild blood glucose increase", "Vitamin K-containing supplements — antagonize warfarin effect (INR destabilization)", "Calcium/iron/antacids — chelate and reduce absorption of levothyroxine, tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones — separate by 2+ hours", "Melatonin and valerian can add to sedation when combined with other CNS depressants such as benzodiazepines, opioids, or alcohol"]}, {"heading": "Age Restrictions on OTC Products", "bullets": ["ASA — avoid in children/teens with viral illness (Reye's syndrome risk)", "Cough/cold combination products — not recommended under 6 years in Canada; caution/avoid under 12 per current guidance", "Diphenhydramine/first-gen antihistamines — caution in elderly (anticholinergic burden, falls, confusion — Beers Criteria) and avoid as routine sleep aid long-term", "Codeine-containing OTC products — restricted/prescription-only for those under 18 in most Canadian jurisdictions now", "Decongestants (pseudoephedrine/phenylephrine) — caution in hypertension, and behind-counter sales due to methamphetamine precursor control", "Pinworm/threadworm anthelmintics require a repeat dose about 2 weeks later (longer for some products) and treatment of all household contacts even if they're asymptomatic", "OTC ocular lubricants are first-line and safe across ages for dry eye; refer if there's no improvement within about a week, or if red flags like pain, vision change, or contact lens use are present"]}, {"heading": "Antacid vs H2RA vs PPI — Onset/Duration", "bullets": ["Antacids (calcium carbonate) — fastest onset (minutes), shortest duration (20-60 min)", "H2RAs (famotidine) — onset ~1 hour, duration 10-12 hours; good for prevention before trigger meals", "PPIs (omeprazole OTC) — slower onset (1-4 days for full effect), longest duration; best for frequent/regular heartburn (>2x/week)", "Separate antacids from other oral meds by 2+ hours (absorption interference)"]}, {"heading": "Laxative Selection", "bullets": ["Bulk-forming (psyllium) — first-line, needs adequate fluid intake, slow onset (days)", "Osmotic (PEG 3350) — safe for long-term/chronic use, onset 1-3 days, minimal electrolyte disturbance", "Stimulant (senna, bisacodyl) — short-term use, onset 6-12 hours, more cramping, avoid chronic daily use", "Stool softener (docusate) — prevention of straining, not a true laxative, mild effect only", "Hemorrhoid self-care pairs a topical anesthetic/astringent/vasoconstrictor product with laxatives for constipation and sitz baths; refer if there's no improvement within a week or any red flags (bleeding, young age, higher-grade internal hemorrhoids)"]}, {"heading": "Sleep Aids & Smoking Cessation Quick Points", "bullets": ["Melatonin — low dose (0.5-3mg) 30-60 min before bed; timing > dose for jet lag", "Diphenhydramine/doxylamine — short-term only, tolerance develops in days, avoid in elderly", "NRT — match product to need: patch = steady background, gum/lozenge/inhaler = breakthrough cravings; combination therapy often superior", "NRT gum — 'chew and park' technique; avoid acidic beverages 15 min before/during use (reduces absorption)"]}, {"heading": "Minor Ailments — Key Red Flags & Treatment Pearls", "bullets": ["Cough red flags include fever lasting more than 3 days or very high fever, bloody or dark sputum, chest pain, cough lasting more than 3 weeks, or a recent ACE inhibitor/beta-blocker start suggesting a drug-induced cough", "Non-productive cough may use an antitussive; productive cough should avoid antitussives and consider an expectorant instead", "Dysmenorrhea red flags for a secondary cause include new onset after adolescence, pain outside the usual pattern, fever, or a palpable pelvic mass — NSAIDs, not acetaminophen, are first-line for primary dysmenorrhea", "For insect bites and stings, assess for anaphylaxis first (epinephrine, emergency services); a tick bite needs assessment for possible Lyme disease prophylaxis", "Shingles self-care is only appropriate if onset is within about 72 hours, there's no facial/ocular/ear involvement, and the patient isn't at risk of disseminated disease; antivirals are most useful within that early window, especially for older adults", "Seborrheic dermatitis/dandruff of the scalp starts with an antifungal shampoo; non-scalp disease starts with gentle skin care, escalating to a topical antifungal or low-potency corticosteroid if needed", "Warts (verrucae) are treated first-line with topical salicylic acid; refer lesions on the face, genitals, or nails, any sign of infection, or a patient with diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, or immunocompromise"]}, {"heading": "Commonly Refrigerated Products — Storage Pearls", "bullets": ["Most biologics, insulins, GLP-1 agonists, and vaccines require refrigeration at 2-8C and must never be frozen", "Many refrigerated injectables can tolerate a defined period at room temperature once removed from the fridge — always confirm the specific product's stability window before counselling on travel or a missed refrigeration event", "Reconstituted antibiotic suspensions must be refrigerated and are typically only good for about 2 weeks after mixing, even though the dry powder was shelf-stable at room temperature beforehand", "mRNA vaccines have the most complex cold chain: frozen storage until use, a limited thawed-and-undiluted window in the fridge, and a much shorter window once diluted (hours, not days)", "Some hormonal contraceptive patches/rings and ophthalmic drops are refrigerated for shelf life but become stable at room temperature for a defined period once dispensed or opened — check the product monograph rather than assuming"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["St. John's Wort can make hormonal birth control FAIL — always ask about OCP use before recommending", "Ginkgo + warfarin/ASA/NSAIDs = bleeding risk — a favourite interaction question", "Iron supplements turn stool black (benign) — don't confuse with melena, but always clarify with patient history", "Antacids/calcium/iron block absorption of levothyroxine and fluoroquinolones — timing separation is the tested answer", "PPI vs H2RA vs antacid onset/duration ranking is frequently tested — antacid fastest/shortest, PPI slowest/longest", "For cough, avoid antitussives with a productive cough (you want to clear secretions) and avoid cough/cold combination products in young children", "For corns and calluses, topical salicylic acid is first-line, but diabetics, patients with peripheral vascular disease, and the immunocompromised should be referred up front rather than trying self-care first", "Canker sore red flags include ulcers over 1 cm, more than 5 lesions, a first occurrence after age 30, or duration beyond 2 weeks — these suggest something other than a simple minor aphthous ulcer"]}]}, {"topic": "ethics", "title": "Ethics & Professional Practice — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Four Core Principles", "bullets": ["Autonomy — respect a capable patient's right to decide, even to refuse treatment", "Beneficence — act in the patient's best interest", "Non-maleficence — 'first, do no harm'; prevent harm from errors/interactions", "Justice — fair, equitable treatment regardless of who the patient is", "Exam trap: no single principle is absolute — weigh in context, don't default to paternalism", "Two additional principles often tested alongside the classic four are fidelity (loyalty, keeping promises, confidentiality) and veracity (truthfulness without deception)", "When principles conflict, follow the applicable law, regulation, or standard of practice first, then use professional judgment to decide precedence, and document the decision", "If you have good reason to suspect something illegal or unethical is happening, report it — don't participate in a cover-up"]}, {"heading": "Informed Consent", "bullets": ["Elements: capacity + voluntariness + disclosure of material info + comprehension", "Consent is ongoing — can be withdrawn any time", "Emergency exception: implied consent if incapable, no SDM available, delay = harm", "Teach-back confirms real understanding, not just a signature", "Informed consent requires disclosing the nature of the decision, reasonable alternatives, and the relevant risks/benefits of each option — omitting alternatives violates autonomy even if you fully disclose the option you personally prefer", "A request to withhold side-effect information from a patient to improve adherence asks a provider to violate both veracity and autonomy, and should not be honoured"]}, {"heading": "Confidentiality & Exceptions", "bullets": ["Default: no disclosure without consent", "Exceptions: imminent serious harm to self/others, mandatory reporting (e.g., certain diseases, child abuse), court order, college inspection", "Family members are NOT automatically entitled to info — confirm consent/SDM status first", "Circle of care allows sharing among treating providers, but patient can 'lock box' specific info", "Confirming or denying that someone is a patient at a specialty clinic to a family member without consent breaches confidentiality, even when the request seems harmless", "Sharing information within the circle of care is implied consent; sharing with family members is not automatic and requires the patient's consent or confirmed SDM status"]}, {"heading": "Conscientious Objection", "bullets": ["May decline personally on moral grounds, but must NOT abandon the patient", "Must give timely, effective referral (not vague 'try elsewhere')", "No lecturing/moralizing to the patient", "In a true emergency with no alternative, most colleges require providing the service anyway", "The appropriate response to a moral objection is declining to participate while pointing the patient to a specific alternate source of care — not simply refusing and leaving the patient to find help alone", "A pharmacist may personally object to dispensing a medication but must not lecture the patient or refuse without arranging alternate care"]}, {"heading": "Error Disclosure", "bullets": ["Disclose to patient promptly and honestly — separate duty from internal CQI/incident reporting", "Disclose even if no harm occurred or patient hasn't noticed", "Apologize sincerely; explain corrective steps", "Silence or delay is itself an ethical breach", "Even if an error causes no apparent harm, the pharmacist should still document it, monitor the patient, and follow the practice's error-reporting process — absence of harm does not remove the duty to address the error"]}, {"heading": "Capacity & SDM", "bullets": ["Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific, not global", "Disagreeing with pharmacist advice ≠ incapacity", "SDM hierarchy (varies by province): guardian > attorney for personal care > spouse > child > parent > sibling", "Reassess capacity once a temporary condition (intoxication, delirium) resolves, if decision is non-urgent", "Capacity is an ethical/clinical concept assessed immediately by the treating provider; competence is a legal concept determined by a judge through a lengthier process — the two terms are not interchangeable", "Red flags that should trigger a capacity assessment include significant mental illness, dementia, delirium, an inability to understand information, extremes of age, polypharmacy, choices inconsistent with prior values, or refusal of a low-risk, high-benefit treatment", "When a patient lacks capacity, the order of preference is: the patient's own living will/advance directive, then substituted judgment by a surrogate (what the patient would have wanted), then a best-interests standard if neither is known", "If there's no power of attorney for health, provincial SDM hierarchies generally proceed from spouse to child to sibling to closest relative — confirm the applicable provincial order rather than assuming"]}, {"heading": "Scope of Practice & Conflicts of Interest", "bullets": ["Practicing beyond authorized scope = regulatory violation even if clinically confident", "Refer when complexity exceeds scope", "Disclose financial/personal interests that could bias recommendations", "Some conflicts require avoidance, not just disclosure (e.g., dispensing to self)", "Gifts of only minimal value from industry are generally acceptable, but pharmacists should never solicit or accept anything that could influence, or appear to influence, clinical judgment", "A pharmacist involved in a formulary decision about a product they were paid to promote should disclose the conflict and recuse themselves from that specific decision — disclosure alone is not always sufficient", "Removing a conflict of interest entirely (for example, charging market rent rather than offering free space to a co-located prescriber) is preferred over merely managing or disclosing it"]}, {"heading": "Non-Judgmental Care & Boundaries", "bullets": ["Use objective criteria (PMP checks) consistently for ALL patients — inconsistent scrutiny = discrimination", "Patients with substance use disorder still deserve dignified, non-stigmatizing care", "No dual/romantic relationships with patients — power imbalance always exists", "Cultural competence: ask, don't assume; use professional interpreters, not children"]}, {"heading": "Fidelity, Veracity & Justice — Applied Cases", "bullets": ["Fidelity is the duty of loyalty underlying confidentiality and avoiding conflicts of interest — delaying one patient's appointment for a more urgent one can be justified, but the original patient should still be informed and rescheduled", "Veracity means telling the whole truth without deception — using a placebo or deliberately withholding known side effects, even with good intentions, violates this principle", "A dispensing error still requires prompt disclosure to the patient even if no harm resulted — the duty to disclose is separate from the duty to prevent harm", "Justice questions often hinge on fair distribution: recruiting only easier-to-reach low-income patients for a trial, or stockpiling a scarce drug for family members who aren't high-risk, both violate justice", "The 'veil of ignorance' thought experiment is a useful lens for justice questions — decide a policy as if you didn't know in advance which role in the situation would be yours"]}, {"heading": "Self-Prescribing, Family Care & Industry Relationships", "bullets": ["Pharmacists and physicians should generally not treat themselves or family members except for minor conditions or true emergencies when no other qualified provider is available, and should transfer ongoing care to another professional as soon as possible", "Never prescribe narcotics, controlled substances, or other addictive medications for yourself or a family member — this crosses from merely inadvisable into professional misconduct in most jurisdictions", "Research involving human subjects requires prior approval from a research ethics board, which typically includes clinicians, a legal member, and at least one public member, and considers informed consent, privacy, justice, and harm-benefit balance", "If you suspect a colleague is inappropriately self-prescribing or prescribing for family members, report it to the regulatory college rather than only addressing it informally"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Conscientious objection ≠ clinical judgment — don't confuse the two", "Duty-to-warn overrides confidentiality only for imminent, serious, identifiable-target harm", "'Apology' language is protected from being used as legal admission in most provinces", "Therapeutic privilege (withholding truth to protect the patient) is essentially never justified today", "Reporting a suspected impaired colleague is mandatory, not optional collegiality", "Paternalism is not an ethical principle — it's what happens when a provider's judgment overrides a capable patient's autonomy, often falsely justified using beneficence or non-maleficence", "When law and ethics conflict on the exam, the law wins — mandatory reporting and controlled-substance regulations always take precedence over a competing ethical instinct", "Never choose an answer where a pharmacist passes the buck by only telling the patient to see their doctor without first attempting to resolve the issue within their own scope"]}]}, {"topic": "calc", "title": "Calculations & Compounding — High-Yield Review", "sections": [{"heading": "Renal Dosing — Cockcroft-Gault", "bullets": ["CrCl = [(140-age) x wt(kg)] / (72 x SCr mg/dL) x 0.85 if female", "Convert SCr from micromol/L to mg/dL by dividing by 88.4", "Use adjusted body weight if >30% over ideal body weight", "Don't forget the female correction factor — classic omission", "Use actual body weight if it's below ideal body weight; use ideal body weight if actual weight is between ideal and 130% of ideal; use adjusted body weight if actual weight exceeds 130% of ideal", "Adjusted body weight = ideal body weight + 0.4 x (actual weight − ideal body weight), used for renal dosing in obese patients", "Cockcroft-Gault can overestimate GFR in patients with rapidly declining renal function and underestimate it in patients recovering from a condition that had temporarily lowered their renal function"]}, {"heading": "IV Flow Rates", "bullets": ["mL/hr = total volume / total time(hr)", "gtt/min = (volume mL x drop factor gtt/mL) / time(min) — convert hr to min first", "Macrodrip: 10/15/20 gtt/mL; Microdrip: 60 gtt/mL (peds/precision)", "Shortcut: with microdrip (60gtt/mL), gtt/min numerically = mL/hr", "Always confirm the time units match before calculating — convert hours to minutes (or vice versa) as the very first step to avoid a 60-times error"]}, {"heading": "Alligation & Dilution", "bullets": ["Grid: desired % in middle; higher stock top-left, lower stock (or 0) bottom-left", "Parts of higher = |desired-lower|; parts of lower = |higher-desired|", "Desired % must fall between the two stock %'s", "Verify with mass balance: (V1C1+V2C2)/Vtotal = desired conc", "C1V1 = C2V2 is the core dilution equation; the difference between final and initial volume equals the diluent volume added", "Alligation medial calculates the resulting strength of a mixture from known component volumes; alligation alternate (cross-subtract) calculates the ratio of two stock strengths needed to reach a target strength", "When one of two stock strengths is limited in supply, set up the alligation to find what fraction of the final product that stock represents, then scale the whole batch up from the limited quantity actually on hand"]}, {"heading": "BSA & Pediatric Dosing", "bullets": ["Mosteller BSA(m^2) = sqrt[(height cm x weight kg)/3600]", "Dose = BSA x dose/m^2 (chemo, some peds protocols)", "Peds: mg/kg/day ÷ doses/day = per-dose amount — don't confuse mg/kg/day with mg/kg/dose", "Always compare pediatric calculated dose to adult max ceiling", "Young's Rule (age-based) and Clark's Rule (weight-based) are outdated formulas used only when no pediatric-specific dosing reference is available", "Young's Rule: pediatric dose = adult dose x [age in years / (age in years + 12)]", "Clark's Rule: pediatric dose = adult dose x (weight in lb / 150)"]}, {"heading": "Days Supply & Electrolytes", "bullets": ["Days supply = total qty dispensed / qty used per day", "Taper regimens: calculate phase-by-phase, then sum", "mEq = mmol x valence; Na+/K+ valence=1 (mEq=mmol), Ca2+/Mg2+ valence=2 (mEq=2x mmol)", "Watch salt-form MW vs elemental ion MW in electrolyte calcs"]}, {"heading": "Compounding — Aliquots & BUD", "bullets": ["Aliquot method used when needed drug qty < minimum weighable qty (~120mg on Class A balance)", "Aliquot: dilute to a convenient total, then take proportional portion containing target amount", "Non-sterile BUD (no stability data): water-containing = 14 days refrigerated; non-aqueous = lesser of 25% remaining expiry or 6 months", "Sterile BUD depends on risk level (immediate/low/medium/high) and storage temp — always shorter than non-sterile", "Minimum weighable quantity = balance sensitivity / maximum acceptable error (commonly 5%) — this is the smallest mass a given balance can reliably measure", "Aliquot method: choose a dilution factor that brings the needed drug amount above the minimum weighable quantity, weigh out drug plus diluent to make that dilution, then weigh out only the fraction of the dilution containing the target dose", "Sterile compounding beyond-use dating depends on both contamination risk level (low/medium/high) and storage condition — refrigeration generally extends the allowed dating compared to room temperature", "Hazardous drugs are classified as cytotoxic (chemotherapy agents, highest occupational risk) or non-cytotoxic (reproductive, endocrine, immune, or respiratory risk); required PPE and engineering controls differ by classification and dosage form"]}, {"heading": "Osmolarity, Tonicity & % Strength", "bullets": ["Osmolarity(mOsm/L) = mmol/L x number of dissociated particles", "0.9% NaCl ≈ 308 mOsm/L (isotonic); CaCl2 dissociates into 3 particles — don't forget", "D5W is isotonic in the bag; acts hypotonic physiologically after dextrose is metabolized", "%w/v x10 = mg/mL; use %w/w for semisolids (creams/ointments), not w/v", "Specific gravity = weight of a substance / weight of an equal volume of water; multiply a liquid's volume by its specific gravity to find its weight in grams", "ppm and ppb describe very dilute solutions: 1 ppm = 1 mg/L = 1 mcg/mL = 1 mcg/g; 1 ppb = 1 mcg/L = 1 ng/mL = 1 ng/g"]}, {"heading": "Loading/Maintenance Dose & Dimensional Analysis", "bullets": ["Loading dose = (target Cp x Vd)/F — independent of clearance", "Maintenance rate = (target Cp x Clearance)/F — depends on clearance, not Vd", "Renal/hepatic impairment: usually adjust maintenance dose, not loading dose", "Always write out units in a conversion chain and cancel diagonally to catch inversion errors", "BMI = weight(kg) / height(m)^2 flags obesity for weight-based dosing decisions, but does not by itself decide which body weight (actual, ideal, or adjusted) to use for a specific drug"]}, {"heading": "Ratio Strength & TPN Calories", "bullets": ["Ratio strength 1:X = 1g/X mL; convert to %: (1/X)x100; convert to mg/mL: 1000/X", "Epinephrine 1:1000 = 1mg/mL (IM anaphylaxis); 1:10000 = 0.1mg/mL (IV arrest) — never confuse the two", "Dextrose = 3.4 kcal/g; 20% lipid emulsion ≈ 2 kcal/mL; amino acids = 4 kcal/g (usually excluded from non-protein calorie counts)", "Nitrogen(g) = protein(g)/6.25, used for nitrogen balance/TPN calculations"]}, {"heading": "Opioid & Fentanyl Patch Conversions", "bullets": ["Standard oral equianalgesic reference doses: morphine 30mg is roughly equivalent to oxycodone 20mg, hydromorphone 6mg, and codeine 200mg", "Steps to convert between opioids: match formulation/route first, use an equianalgesic table to find the equivalent dose, then reduce the new dose by 25-50% for incomplete cross-tolerance", "IV morphine is roughly 3 times more potent than oral morphine — always confirm the route before converting", "Fentanyl patch dosing uses a fixed range table based on total daily oral morphine dose — no additional 25-50% dose reduction is needed since incomplete cross-tolerance is already built into that conversion", "Round the final converted dose to an available product strength and split appropriately between long-acting and breakthrough formulations rather than leaving an odd total"]}, {"heading": "Body Weight Selection & BMI for Dosing", "bullets": ["BMI = weight(kg) / height(m)^2; categories are under 18.5 underweight, 18.5-24.9 normal, 25-29.9 overweight, 30+ obese", "Ideal body weight: men = 50kg + 2.3kg per inch over 5 feet; women = 45.5kg + 2.3kg per inch over 5 feet", "Decision rule for which weight to use in dosing: actual weight if it's below ideal body weight; ideal body weight if actual weight is between ideal and 130% of ideal; adjusted body weight if actual weight exceeds 130% of ideal", "Adjusted body weight = ideal body weight + 0.4 x (actual weight − ideal body weight); the 0.4 factor accounts for partial drug distribution into excess adipose tissue for many lipophilic drugs", "Convert lb to kg (divide by 2.2) and feet/inches to cm/m before plugging into these formulas — unit mix-ups are the most common source of error"]}, {"heading": "Classic Exam-Trap Pearls", "bullets": ["Rounding too early in multi-step calcs compounds error — round only at the final step", "Confusing mg/kg/day with mg/kg/dose is one of the most common pediatric dosing errors", "Forgetting to convert lb→kg (÷2.2) before weight-based dosing", "Forgetting the female (x0.85) factor in Cockcroft-Gault", "Mixing up valence for divalent electrolytes (Ca2+, Mg2+) in mEq/mmol conversions", "When converting between two opioids, reduce the calculated equivalent dose by 25-50% for incomplete cross-tolerance — except when converting TO a fentanyl patch, where that reduction is already built into the conversion table", "Don't confuse Young's/Clark's Rule (legacy, rarely appropriate in practice) with true weight-based pediatric dosing from a current reference", "The '130% of ideal body weight' cutoff determines whether to use actual, ideal, or adjusted body weight for dosing — memorize the full three-tier decision rule, not just one formula"]}]}];
